{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BS8iTPhUBtjXKQ5ZWkiL5m","type":"tool_result","content":"% What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement -- drafted by loop0015\n% item 03 (2026-11-05), rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the line + 1);\n% every Lean location is in the clone at commit 4eaee875, read with cat/sed -n\n% only. Every page, line, fragment and number of this section is checked and\n% the two tables are written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved.\n\\section{What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement}\n\\label{sec:beyond}\n\nThe manuscript \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nis 67 pages long. The verified statement is 220 lines long. Section~\\ref{sec:statement}\nread the statement. This section reads the manuscript's claims one by\none. For each claim it says one of three things: this is what the Lean\ntheorem asserts; this follows from the Lean theorem by an argument that\nexists only on paper; or this rests on a bound that the Lean library\ndoes not contain at all.\n\nThe collection draws the line itself, in a short note on the family.\nWe quote its two sentences in full (\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, lines\n9 and 11):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\\sloppy\n``For fixed $k \\ge 2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly\ncounts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$\nvariables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$,\nwhere $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has\nvisited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.''\n\n``For $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m \\le Dn$ for\nfixed $D > 0$, this improves to\n$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.''\n\\end{quote}\nIn words (the notation $O_k(1)$ means a constant that depends only on $k$, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the Lean proves the bound with the exponent of Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, the one with the \\emph{squared} double logarithm. The manuscript claims two better exponents, one with the\ndouble logarithm not squared, and one, for formulas with few clauses,\nwith no double logarithm at all. Everything below is an elaboration of\nthat note against the manuscript's text and the library's files.\nTable~\\ref{tab:beyond-claims} is the summary.\n\n\\subsection{Three statuses, and what ``paper-only'' means}\n\\label{sec:beyond-statuses}\n\nWe tag every claim with one of three words.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Verified.} The claim is the comparator statement\n  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, or one of its parts, or a theorem of\n  the solution tree that the replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay} compiled\n  and whose axioms it printed. Nothing else is called verified.\n\\item \\textbf{Supported.} The claim follows from the verified statement\n  by an argument that is on paper only: a short reduction, or a\n  citation. The argument is not in the Lean library, and we have not\n  checked it. What we have checked is narrower: the only property of\n  the counter the argument uses is one the verified statement provides,\n  and the exponent it inherits is therefore $\\rhon$ and not a sharper\n  one.\n\\item \\textbf{Paper-only.} The claim rests on a bound that the Lean\n  library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} does not contain: Theorem~1.1's\n  two sharper exponents, and whatever is derived from them.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nOur working plan for this section had two tags, verified and paper-only. The\nmiddle one is ours. The manuscript's corollaries are of two different\nkinds, and one word would have put Corollary~1.3, an encoding into the\nverified counter, beside Corollary~1.4, a bound whose proof calls the\nsharper exponent by name. In that two-way split, ``supported'' is\na kind of ``paper-only'': not in the library. In no case does\n``paper-only'' mean ``wrong''. We did not read the manuscript's proofs\nfor correctness. The tag records where a claim is established, not\nwhether it is.\n\n\\subsection{The sharper exponents of Theorem~1.1}\n\\label{sec:beyond-exponents}\n\nTheorem~1.1 (p.~2, lines 56--62) states two running-time bounds for the\nsame algorithm. The first holds for every $k$-CNF on $n \\ge 20$\nvariables: a polynomial in $n + m$ times\n$2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. The second holds when the number of\nclauses is at most a fixed multiple $D$ of $n$: a polynomial times\n$2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. Both use a polynomial amount of space\n(line 60). The sentence that follows the theorem is the one this paper's\ntitle is about (p.~2, lines 63--67):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The rational interpolation argument in Section 6.4 gives the weaker\nexponent $O_k(\\rho(n))$, where\n$\\rho(n) = n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n} = n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}$.\nAppendix D proves both bounds in Theorem 1.1 by tensor contraction.''\n\\end{quote}\nProposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476) states the weaker bound on its\nown: for each fixed $k \\ge 2$, the interpolation algorithm counts in a\npolynomial times $2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ bit operations and polynomial space,\nfor $n \\ge 20$.\n\n\\paragraph{Which of the three is in the Lean.}\nThe verified exponent is the weaker one. The comparator's\n\\runtimeScale{} (lines 132--134 of the comparator file) is\n$n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$, the squared form. The solution's\nown exponent, called \\texttt{rho}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines 10--11), is the same\nfunction with base-2 logarithms, and a lemma,\n\\path{runtimeScale_le_four_rho} (lines 190--191), bounds the\ncomparator's exponent by four times it for $n \\ge 20$. The proof is built around the parameters of the manuscript's Section~6.4, the three numbers that set how the proof thins the formula before counting (its ``sparsification''), to the letter: the manuscript selects $\\lambda_n = \\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$,\n$\\nu_n = \\lceil\\log_2\\lambda_n\\rceil$ and\n$s = \\lfloor\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}/\\nu_n^2\\rfloor$, ``the largest nonnegative\ninteger satisfying $s^2\\nu_n^4 \\le \\lambda_n$'' (p.~43, lines 2480--2486; $\\lceil x\\rceil$ is the smallest whole number at least $x$ and $\\lfloor x\\rfloor$ the largest at most $x$), and \\path{Asymptotics.lean} lines 14--20 define\n\\texttt{sparsifyLambda}, \\texttt{sparsifyNu} and\n\\texttt{sparsifySchedule} as exactly those three quantities. What\nAppendix~D of the manuscript proves is not in the library in any form we\ncould find (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}). So the general exponent\n$n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ and the fixed-density exponent\n$n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ are \\textbf{paper-only}: proved at p.~57 (lines\n3222--3246) and p.~54 (lines 3045--3064) respectively, by the\ncontraction theorem of Appendix~D.\n\n\\paragraph{Both bounds from one algorithm.}\nThe manuscript notes that a single algorithm attains both paper-only\nbounds, by switching between two methods according to the number of\nclauses (p.~57, lines 3247--3250). That algorithm is not the verified one\neither.\n\n\\paragraph{The three exponents, in numbers.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} puts the three exponents side by side,\neach divided by $n$ and each with its constant taken to be $1$. A value\nabove $1$ means the exponent is above $n$, so the bound is worse than\ntrying every assignment. The verified ratio is the one of\nTable~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}. The general paper-only ratio,\n$\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$, never reaches $1$: it peaks at $2/e = 0.736$\nat $n \\approx 1{,}618$ and falls on both sides. The fixed-density ratio\n$1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ falls from $0.578$ at $n = 20$. So, at constant $1$,\nboth paper-only guarantees sit below $2^n$ at every size, and the\nverified one sits above $2^n$ from $n = 50$ to $n \\approx 10^{2390}$.\nThat is the whole content of the word ``verified'' in the title: the\nmanuscript's exponents would make a different sentence, and they are\nnot what the kernel checked. Two cautions apply to the table equally.\nThe constants in front of every exponent are not computed anywhere, in\nthe manuscript or in the Lean. And the manuscript's exponents are\nundefined below $n = e$ and are stated for $n \\ge 20$, while the\nverified one is stated for all $n$. Figure~\\ref{fig:central} draws the\nsame three curves.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The three exponents of the manuscript, each divided by $n$,\nwith every constant taken to be $1$. A value above $1$ means the bound\nis worse than trying every assignment at that $n$. Columns: the verified\n$\\rhon/n = (\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$ (comparator lines\n132--134); Theorem~1.1's general exponent $\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2,\nline 59); its fixed-density exponent $1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2, line 62).\nNatural logarithms throughout (p.~1, lines 48--49). The row $e^{e^2}$ is\nwhere the general ratio peaks ($2/e$). Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}\n\\label{tab:beyond-exponents}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/beyond_exponents}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{The abstract's form.}\nThe manuscript's abstract states the running time as a polynomial times\n$2^{n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}}$ (p.~1, lines 6--9). The small term $o(1)$ in\nthat exponent is a quantity that goes to zero as $n$ grows. The verified exponent is of that form too, because the square of a double logarithm grows more slowly than any fixed power of the logarithm (in symbols, $(\\ln\\ln n)^2$ is $(\\ln n)^{o(1)}$), and the manuscript says so at lines 64--66. We tag the\nabstract's form \\textbf{verified}, with the remark that its $o(1)$\nhides exactly the factor by which the three rows of\nTable~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} differ: at $n = 10^6$ that factor is\n$6.9$ for the verified exponent, $2.6$ for the general one and $1$ for\nthe fixed-density one.\n\n\\subsection{The corollaries}\n\\label{sec:beyond-corollaries}\n\nThe corollaries speak of objects from the manuscript's own fields:\ncircuits, graphs, maps. We gloss each in a clause where it first appears\nand define none further; a reader who needs more should go to the\nmanuscript at the lines cited.\n\\paragraph{Finding and sampling a satisfying assignment (p.~2, lines 68--71).}\n``When $\\#F > 0$, the standard self-reduction finds a satisfying\nassignment by fixing one variable at a time and checking that the\nremaining count is positive. Choosing each bit according to the two\nconditional counts instead gives an exactly uniform random satisfying\nassignment,'' with polynomial expected overhead and a citation.\n\\textbf{Supported}. The method calls the verified counter at most $n$\ntimes on formulas of the same width, and the verified statement\nprovides the exact count it needs. The method is two sentences of prose\nand is not in the library.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.2, Max-$k$-SAT within $\\varepsilon m$ (p.~2, lines 77--94).}\nA randomised algorithm finds an assignment that satisfies at least\n$\\mathrm{OPT}(F) - \\varepsilon m$ clauses, that is, within a small\nfraction of the best possible, with high probability, in time\n\\[\n  (n+m)^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(\\rho(n))}\n\\]\n(line 80). The corollary is stated with $\\rho(n)$ itself, the verified\nexponent. Its proof samples $O(n/\\varepsilon^2)$ clauses, builds a small circuit (a network of logic gates), turns the circuit into a 3-CNF with\n$O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ variables by a cited lemma, and calls Theorem~1.1 repeatedly, narrowing down the best value and then fixing variables one at a time (lines 82--94).\n\\textbf{Supported}: the exponent matches the verified one because\n$\\rho(cn) = O_c(\\rho(n))$, and every call is to a fixed-width counter on\n$O(n)$ variables; the sampling, the circuit and the reduction are on\npaper. Its randomness is in the reduction, not in the counter.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.3, independent sets, colourings, perfect matchings (p.~2--3, lines 100--120).}\nFor a graph on $v$ vertices and $e$ edges: the number of independent sets (sets of vertices no two of which are joined by an edge) and, for fixed $q$, of proper $q$-colourings (colourings with $q$ colours such that no edge joins two vertices of the same colour), in time $2^{o(v)}$, and the number of perfect matchings (sets of edges touching every vertex exactly once) in time $2^{o(v+e)}$, all in polynomial space. The encodings are explicit: one variable per\nvertex and a clause of two literals per edge (lines 107--112); $q$\nvariables per vertex with ``exactly one colour'' and ``no equal\ncolours'' clauses, a $q$-CNF on $qv$ variables (lines 112--115); one\nvariable per edge and a linear-size circuit for ``exactly one selected\nedge meets each vertex'', reduced to 3-CNF with $O(v+e)$ variables, with\nthe extra variables fixed by the edge bits so that the count is\npreserved (lines 116--119). \\textbf{Supported}: the conclusion is\n$2^{o(\\cdot)}$, which needs only that $\\rhon$ grows more slowly than $n$\n\\leanref{rho-little-o}, and each encoding preserves the count into a\nfixed-width CNF on $O(v)$ or $O(v+e)$ variables. None of the three\nencodings is in the library.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.4, a circuit lower bound for $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$ (p.~3, lines 121--127).}\n``The fixed-density case of Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound for\n$\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$'' (line 121). A circuit lower bound is a proof that some problem needs large circuits; $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$ is a class of problems whose definition is not needed here. The claim: there is a problem $L$ in that class and a constant $c > 0$ such that $L$ needs circuits of more than $c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ gates for infinitely many $n$ (lines 123--127). The proof is Appendix~C.1 (p.~51--52, lines 2925--2967), an argument of Williams's,\nand it invokes the paper-only exponent in so many words: ``Put\n$a(t) = \\sqrt{\\log_2 t}$. Testing whether the count in the\nconstant-density part of Theorem 1.1 is positive decides 3-SAT with $V$\nvariables and at most $5V$ clauses in time $V^b\\,2^{AV/a(V)}$ for fixed\n$A, b > 0$'' (p.~52, lines 2949--2950); the threshold\n$s_n = \\lfloor\\eta n\\lfloor a(n)\\rfloor\\rfloor$ is chosen against that\n$a$ (line 2953), and the constant $c$ comes out as\n$\\sqrt{\\eta/(d\\log 2)}$ (line 2967). \\textbf{Paper-only}: the\nquantitative bound $c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ is tied to $2^{AV/\\sqrt{\\log V}}$,\nwhich the library does not contain. What the same argument would give\nfrom the verified exponent is a question about the manuscript's proof,\nwhich we do not examine (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-not}).\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary C.1, the class $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$ has no linear-size circuits (p.~51, lines 2912--2916).}\n``Williams proved that if ETH is false, then $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$\ndoes not have linear-size circuits [Wil13, Theorem 6.1]. Apply this\nimplication to the deterministic subexponential-time 3-SAT algorithm\nsupplied by Theorem 1.1.'' \\textbf{Supported}: the only input is a\ndeterministic $2^{o(n)}$ 3-SAT decision, which the verified statement\nprovides on its answer tape for $k = 3$; the implication is a citation.\nThe manuscript's own scope sentence applies: the corollary ``does not\nassert a superpolynomial circuit lower bound for NP'' (lines 2923--2924).\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary C.2, inverting constant-locality maps (p.~52, lines 2974--2977).}\nFor a Boolean map whose every output bit reads at most $d$ input bits (a ``constant-locality'' map), the number of inputs mapping to a given output, and one such input, in\ndeterministic time $(n+m)^{O_d(1)}\\,2^{O_d(\\rho(n))}$ and polynomial\nspace, for $n \\ge 20$. Stated with $\\rho(n)$. The proof writes one\nclause per forbidden local assignment, a CNF of width at most $d$ on the\noriginal $n$ variables with at most $2^d m$ clauses whose satisfying\nassignments are exactly the wanted inputs, and applies Theorem~1.1 with\n$k = \\max\\{2, d\\}$ (lines 2979--2988). \\textbf{Supported}: the encoding\nis on paper; the counter and the exponent are the verified ones. The remark that follows, on Goldreich's candidate one-way functions (functions easy to compute and conjectured hard to invert; p.~53, lines 2994--2999), inherits the same status and the manuscript's own\nlimit, ``This does not rule out one-wayness against polynomial-time\nalgorithms'' (line 2999).\n\n\\subsection{Appendix D: the contraction route}\n\\label{sec:beyond-appendix-d}\n\nAppendix~D of the manuscript, ``A sharper bound for exact counting''\n(p.~53--64, from line 3001), proves Theorem~1.1 ``by contracting Boolean tensor networks. The two running times reflect the number of wires in the networks'' (lines 3002--3003). A tensor network is a way of writing one large sum as a product of small tables; the terms in the rest of this paragraph are the manuscript's, and we do not define them further. Theorem~D.1 (p.~53, lines\n3017--3028) is the contraction theorem: a bipartite tensor network with\nat most $Cs$ edges, maximum degree at most $\\lceil s^{9/10}\\rceil$ and\nentries in $(\\mathbb{Z}/2^{P_0}\\mathbb{Z})[V]/(V^\\ell)$ is contracted in\n$(s+L)^{O(1)}\\,2^{O(s/\\sqrt{\\log s})}$ bit operations and polynomial\nspace. The fixed-density proof (p.~54, lines 3045--3064) encodes a\nformula as a network with $O_k(n+m)$ edges and applies Theorem~D.1 with\n$s = n + m$; the arbitrary-density proof (p.~57, lines 3222--3246)\ncombines the sparsification of Section~6 with Lemma~D.2 (p.~54, line\n3069) and reaches the exponent $O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})$ at line\n3243. Section~D.4 changes the logarithm convention for its own proof,\n``log denotes a base-two logarithm and ln denotes a natural logarithm''\n(p.~57, lines 3251--3252). All of this is \\textbf{paper-only}: no\ndeclaration about tensor networks, contraction, Hermite recovery or\npackets exists in the family's 3,977 files, by the identifier search of\nSection~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}. We make no statement about Appendix~D's\nargument; we did not read it for correctness.\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses of \\S1.1.1 and the fine-grained remarks of \\S1.1.3}\n\\label{sec:beyond-hypotheses}\n\n\\paragraph{\\S1.1.1 (p.~4, lines 187--191).}\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [\\ldots] and SETH [\\ldots], and of Gap-ETH.''\nAnd \\S1.1.2 (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves\n\\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time and\ntherefore refutes \\#ETH.'' These are the four named hypotheses about how\nhard satisfiability is, their randomised forms, and the counting\nhypothesis; Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} states each one in words.\n\\textbf{Supported}, and by the shortest argument in the manuscript: the\nverified statement is a deterministic machine whose answer tape says\nwhether the count is positive and whose count tape carries the count,\nwithin a subexponential bound, for each fixed $k \\ge 2$; each hypothesis\nis the denial of that. The hypotheses themselves are not defined in the\nlibrary (the strings ``ETH'' and ``SETH'' occur in the collection's Lean\nonly in two files of a different family,\n\\clonefile{lean/OAI/Combinatorics/ParityLifts/}, which take an ETH-like\nproposition as a hypothesis and are not among the files the proof depends on, its closure, Section~\\ref{sec:proof}).\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences} says exactly how the verified statement\nnegates each one. The two sentences the manuscript adds at lines\n191--194, on unconditional lower bounds for restricted proof systems and\non Williams's 2016 MA-SETH protocol, are context, not claims about the\nresult.\n\n\\paragraph{\\S1.1.3 (p.~5, lines 213--243).}\nAfter a list of lower bounds proved from ETH or SETH (lines 213--225),\nthe manuscript writes: ``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis\nfor the hardness claims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from\nweaker hypotheses that may still hold'' (lines 226--227). It names the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, a hypothesis about a different problem (lines 229--232), and the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$\nalgorithm, adding: ``Theorem 1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each\nfixed clause width $k$, while this hypothesis allows the width to grow\nwith $n$'' (lines 235--236). These are \\textbf{paper-only} remarks in\nthe sense that nothing about any other hypothesis or any reduction is in\nthe library; they are also the manuscript's own statement of what the\nresult does not do, and Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} rests on them.\nThe one positive transfer, Set Splitting with sets of size at most $k$\nby one variable per element and two clauses per set (lines 237--243), is\n\\textbf{supported} in the same sense as Corollary~1.3: an encoding on\npaper into the verified counter, inheriting $\\rhon$. The caveat at\np.~2, lines 95--99, that $2^{o(n)}$ bounds ``do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions,'' is not a claim but a limit the\nmanuscript places on its own result; it is quoted again in\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences}.\n\n\\subsection{What the library carries beyond the comparator statement}\n\\label{sec:beyond-lean}\n\n``Paper-only'' is defined against the whole library at commit\n\\texttt{4eaee875}, not only against the comparator file, so we say what\nelse the library proves about this family. The file\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} (201 lines) states five theorems,\neach followed by a \\texttt{\\#print axioms} command, which asks Lean to\nlist the axioms a proof uses (lines 197--201). The replay's build log\ncarries the kernel's answer for all five: the three standard axioms\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/run2/axioms_in_build.txt}).\n\\begin{itemize}\\sloppy\n\\item \\texttt{compiled\\_exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 10) and\n  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 74): the comparator statement,\n  first over the explicit 80-register, 1,220-tape program and then over\n  the machine it compiles to (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} (lines 104--147): the same conclusion\n  with the polynomial factor written as $(n + |F| + 1)^{d}$ instead of\n  $(L+1)^d$, the exponent as $A\\cdot\\mathtt{rho}\\ n$ in base 2, and the\n  condition $20 \\le n$ (line 109); its proof sets its own constants to twice, twice and four times the comparator theorem's $d$, $e$ and $A$ (line 132). This is\n  Proposition~6.4's $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ form in the\n  library, so the manuscript's polynomial factor is verified as well as\n  its exponent; it is \\textbf{verified}, and it is still the weaker\n  exponent.\n\\item \\texttt{sat\\_polynomial\\_space} (lines 149--183), for $k \\ge 3$:\n  the answer bit is true exactly when the formula is satisfiable\n  (\\texttt{answer = true $\\leftrightarrow$ SAT.Satisfiable F}), the\n  decision form made explicit.\n\\item \\texttt{original\\_sat\\_main} (line 184): the sentence named\n  \\path{SqrtLog.Statement.MainTheorem}, a Lean \\texttt{Prop}\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:lean}; \\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementCanonical.lean},\n  line 37), a decision-only\n  target for every width $k \\ge 3$ with the same \\texttt{runtimeBound},\n  stated there without proof (``no inhabitant is supplied here'') and proved in \\path{Final.lean}.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\begin{sloppypar}\nBeyond these, a search for names over the family's 3,977 \\texttt{.lean}\nfiles, by the read-only script \\repofile{paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py},\nfinds no occurrence of \\texttt{SimpleGraph}, \\texttt{PerfectMatching},\n\\texttt{Coloring}, \\texttt{IndependentSet}, \\texttt{CircuitSize},\n\\texttt{OrthogonalVectors}, \\texttt{MaxSat}, \\texttt{TensorNetwork},\n\\texttt{HermiteInterp}, \\texttt{ENP}, \\texttt{SIZE},\n\\texttt{SetSplitting}, \\texttt{GapETH} or \\texttt{NSETH} as an\nidentifier: none of the corollaries, none of Appendix~D, and none of\nthe hypotheses is formalised. The comparator's JSON,\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}, names one\ntheorem, \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, and no definition. A text\nsearch is necessary and not sufficient (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); here\nit is used to show absence, where a miss would require a corollary to\nbe formalised under none of the words its own statement uses.\n\\end{sloppypar}\n\n\\subsection{The table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:beyond-claims} lists every claim examined above with its\nstatus and the one-line reason.\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{Every claim of the manuscript examined in this section: where\nit is stated (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of the committed\nextract), its status in the sense of Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-statuses}\n(verified, supported, or paper-only), and the one-line reason.\nGenerated, with every page and fragment checked against the extract and\nthe clone, by \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}\n\\label{tab:beyond-claims}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/beyond_claims}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:beyond-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the paper-only proofs.} Appendix~D,\n  Appendix~C.1 and the two sharper bounds of Theorem~1.1 were located\n  and quoted, not read for correctness. ``Paper-only'' means not in the\n  Lean library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}; it does not mean wrong, and\n  nothing here suggests it.\n\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the supported arguments either.} The\n  reductions behind Corollaries~1.2, 1.3, C.1, C.2 and the Set Splitting\n  transfer are standard in kind and short, and we checked only which\n  property of the counter they use. Whether each is correct as written\n  is the manuscript's, not ours.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about what the verified exponent would yield\n  through Corollary~1.4's argument.} A reader can rerun Appendix~C.1\n  with $a(t)$ replaced by the verified rate; we did not, because it is\n  a question about a paper-only proof.\n\\item \\textbf{No constants.} Every constant in this section is as\n  unknown as the comparator's $C, A, S, d, e$; the comparisons of\n  Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} are at constant $1$ and say nothing\n  about any machine's running time.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the collection.} Whether the sharper bounds\n  have since been formalised elsewhere, or the manuscript revised, is\n  not examined; what is read is the commit and the dated manuscript.\n\\end{itemize}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1cad2094-11ee-40b3-a86f-8ba4c86457ea","uuid":"32ec3910-5f6d-4208-9682-ec9922537cac","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:09:40.100Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"% What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement -- drafted by loop0015\n% item 03 (2026-11-05), rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the line + 1);\n% every Lean location is in the clone at commit 4eaee875, read with cat/sed -n\n% only. Every page, line, fragment and number of this section is checked and\n% the two tables are written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved.\n\\section{What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement}\n\\label{sec:beyond}\n\nThe manuscript \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nis 67 pages long. The verified statement is 220 lines long. Section~\\ref{sec:statement}\nread the statement. This section reads the manuscript's claims one by\none. For each claim it says one of three things: this is what the Lean\ntheorem asserts; this follows from the Lean theorem by an argument that\nexists only on paper; or this rests on a bound that the Lean library\ndoes not contain at all.\n\nThe collection draws the line itself, in a short note on the family.\nWe quote its two sentences in full (\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, lines\n9 and 11):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\\sloppy\n``For fixed $k \\ge 2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly\ncounts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$\nvariables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$,\nwhere $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has\nvisited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.''\n\n``For $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m \\le Dn$ for\nfixed $D > 0$, this improves to\n$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.''\n\\end{quote}\nIn words (the notation $O_k(1)$ means a constant that depends only on $k$, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the Lean proves the bound with the exponent of Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, the one with the \\emph{squared} double logarithm. The manuscript claims two better exponents, one with the\ndouble logarithm not squared, and one, for formulas with few clauses,\nwith no double logarithm at all. Everything below is an elaboration of\nthat note against the manuscript's text and the library's files.\nTable~\\ref{tab:beyond-claims} is the summary.\n\n\\subsection{Three statuses, and what ``paper-only'' means}\n\\label{sec:beyond-statuses}\n\nWe tag every claim with one of three words.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Verified.} The claim is the comparator statement\n  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, or one of its parts, or a theorem of\n  the solution tree that the replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay} compiled\n  and whose axioms it printed. Nothing else is called verified.\n\\item \\textbf{Supported.} The claim follows from the verified statement\n  by an argument that is on paper only: a short reduction, or a\n  citation. The argument is not in the Lean library, and we have not\n  checked it. What we have checked is narrower: the only property of\n  the counter the argument uses is one the verified statement provides,\n  and the exponent it inherits is therefore $\\rhon$ and not a sharper\n  one.\n\\item \\textbf{Paper-only.} The claim rests on a bound that the Lean\n  library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} does not contain: Theorem~1.1's\n  two sharper exponents, and whatever is derived from them.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nOur working plan for this section had two tags, verified and paper-only. The\nmiddle one is ours. The manuscript's corollaries are of two different\nkinds, and one word would have put Corollary~1.3, an encoding into the\nverified counter, beside Corollary~1.4, a bound whose proof calls the\nsharper exponent by name. In that two-way split, ``supported'' is\na kind of ``paper-only'': not in the library. In no case does\n``paper-only'' mean ``wrong''. We did not read the manuscript's proofs\nfor correctness. The tag records where a claim is established, not\nwhether it is.\n\n\\subsection{The sharper exponents of Theorem~1.1}\n\\label{sec:beyond-exponents}\n\nTheorem~1.1 (p.~2, lines 56--62) states two running-time bounds for the\nsame algorithm. The first holds for every $k$-CNF on $n \\ge 20$\nvariables: a polynomial in $n + m$ times\n$2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. The second holds when the number of\nclauses is at most a fixed multiple $D$ of $n$: a polynomial times\n$2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. Both use a polynomial amount of space\n(line 60). The sentence that follows the theorem is the one this paper's\ntitle is about (p.~2, lines 63--67):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The rational interpolation argument in Section 6.4 gives the weaker\nexponent $O_k(\\rho(n))$, where\n$\\rho(n) = n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n} = n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}$.\nAppendix D proves both bounds in Theorem 1.1 by tensor contraction.''\n\\end{quote}\nProposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476) states the weaker bound on its\nown: for each fixed $k \\ge 2$, the interpolation algorithm counts in a\npolynomial times $2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ bit operations and polynomial space,\nfor $n \\ge 20$.\n\n\\paragraph{Which of the three is in the Lean.}\nThe verified exponent is the weaker one. The comparator's\n\\runtimeScale{} (lines 132--134 of the comparator file) is\n$n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$, the squared form. The solution's\nown exponent, called \\texttt{rho}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines 10--11), is the same\nfunction with base-2 logarithms, and a lemma,\n\\path{runtimeScale_le_four_rho} (lines 190--191), bounds the\ncomparator's exponent by four times it for $n \\ge 20$. The proof is built around the parameters of the manuscript's Section~6.4, the three numbers that set how the proof thins the formula before counting (its ``sparsification''), to the letter: the manuscript selects $\\lambda_n = \\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$,\n$\\nu_n = \\lceil\\log_2\\lambda_n\\rceil$ and\n$s = \\lfloor\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}/\\nu_n^2\\rfloor$, ``the largest nonnegative\ninteger satisfying $s^2\\nu_n^4 \\le \\lambda_n$'' (p.~43, lines 2480--2486; $\\lceil x\\rceil$ is the smallest whole number at least $x$ and $\\lfloor x\\rfloor$ the largest at most $x$), and \\path{Asymptotics.lean} lines 14--20 define\n\\texttt{sparsifyLambda}, \\texttt{sparsifyNu} and\n\\texttt{sparsifySchedule} as exactly those three quantities. What\nAppendix~D of the manuscript proves is not in the library in any form we\ncould find (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}). So the general exponent\n$n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ and the fixed-density exponent\n$n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ are \\textbf{paper-only}: proved at p.~57 (lines\n3222--3246) and p.~54 (lines 3045--3064) respectively, by the\ncontraction theorem of Appendix~D.\n\n\\paragraph{Both bounds from one algorithm.}\nThe manuscript notes that a single algorithm attains both paper-only\nbounds, by switching between two methods according to the number of\nclauses (p.~57, lines 3247--3250). That algorithm is not the verified one\neither.\n\n\\paragraph{The three exponents, in numbers.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} puts the three exponents side by side,\neach divided by $n$ and each with its constant taken to be $1$. A value\nabove $1$ means the exponent is above $n$, so the bound is worse than\ntrying every assignment. The verified ratio is the one of\nTable~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}. The general paper-only ratio,\n$\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$, never reaches $1$: it peaks at $2/e = 0.736$\nat $n \\approx 1{,}618$ and falls on both sides. The fixed-density ratio\n$1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ falls from $0.578$ at $n = 20$. So, at constant $1$,\nboth paper-only guarantees sit below $2^n$ at every size, and the\nverified one sits above $2^n$ from $n = 50$ to $n \\approx 10^{2390}$.\nThat is the whole content of the word ``verified'' in the title: the\nmanuscript's exponents would make a different sentence, and they are\nnot what the kernel checked. Two cautions apply to the table equally.\nThe constants in front of every exponent are not computed anywhere, in\nthe manuscript or in the Lean. And the manuscript's exponents are\nundefined below $n = e$ and are stated for $n \\ge 20$, while the\nverified one is stated for all $n$. Figure~\\ref{fig:central} draws the\nsame three curves.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The three exponents of the manuscript, each divided by $n$,\nwith every constant taken to be $1$. A value above $1$ means the bound\nis worse than trying every assignment at that $n$. Columns: the verified\n$\\rhon/n = (\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$ (comparator lines\n132--134); Theorem~1.1's general exponent $\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2,\nline 59); its fixed-density exponent $1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2, line 62).\nNatural logarithms throughout (p.~1, lines 48--49). The row $e^{e^2}$ is\nwhere the general ratio peaks ($2/e$). Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}\n\\label{tab:beyond-exponents}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/beyond_exponents}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{The abstract's form.}\nThe manuscript's abstract states the running time as a polynomial times\n$2^{n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}}$ (p.~1, lines 6--9). The small term $o(1)$ in\nthat exponent is a quantity that goes to zero as $n$ grows. The verified exponent is of that form too, because the square of a double logarithm grows more slowly than any fixed power of the logarithm (in symbols, $(\\ln\\ln n)^2$ is $(\\ln n)^{o(1)}$), and the manuscript says so at lines 64--66. We tag the\nabstract's form \\textbf{verified}, with the remark that its $o(1)$\nhides exactly the factor by which the three rows of\nTable~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} differ: at $n = 10^6$ that factor is\n$6.9$ for the verified exponent, $2.6$ for the general one and $1$ for\nthe fixed-density one.\n\n\\subsection{The corollaries}\n\\label{sec:beyond-corollaries}\n\nThe corollaries speak of objects from the manuscript's own fields:\ncircuits, graphs, maps. We gloss each in a clause where it first appears\nand define none further; a reader who needs more should go to the\nmanuscript at the lines cited.\n\\paragraph{Finding and sampling a satisfying assignment (p.~2, lines 68--71).}\n``When $\\#F > 0$, the standard self-reduction finds a satisfying\nassignment by fixing one variable at a time and checking that the\nremaining count is positive. Choosing each bit according to the two\nconditional counts instead gives an exactly uniform random satisfying\nassignment,'' with polynomial expected overhead and a citation.\n\\textbf{Supported}. The method calls the verified counter at most $n$\ntimes on formulas of the same width, and the verified statement\nprovides the exact count it needs. The method is two sentences of prose\nand is not in the library.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.2, Max-$k$-SAT within $\\varepsilon m$ (p.~2, lines 77--94).}\nA randomised algorithm finds an assignment that satisfies at least\n$\\mathrm{OPT}(F) - \\varepsilon m$ clauses, that is, within a small\nfraction of the best possible, with high probability, in time\n\\[\n  (n+m)^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(\\rho(n))}\n\\]\n(line 80). The corollary is stated with $\\rho(n)$ itself, the verified\nexponent. Its proof samples $O(n/\\varepsilon^2)$ clauses, builds a small circuit (a network of logic gates), turns the circuit into a 3-CNF with\n$O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ variables by a cited lemma, and calls Theorem~1.1 repeatedly, narrowing down the best value and then fixing variables one at a time (lines 82--94).\n\\textbf{Supported}: the exponent matches the verified one because\n$\\rho(cn) = O_c(\\rho(n))$, and every call is to a fixed-width counter on\n$O(n)$ variables; the sampling, the circuit and the reduction are on\npaper. Its randomness is in the reduction, not in the counter.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.3, independent sets, colourings, perfect matchings (p.~2--3, lines 100--120).}\nFor a graph on $v$ vertices and $e$ edges: the number of independent sets (sets of vertices no two of which are joined by an edge) and, for fixed $q$, of proper $q$-colourings (colourings with $q$ colours such that no edge joins two vertices of the same colour), in time $2^{o(v)}$, and the number of perfect matchings (sets of edges touching every vertex exactly once) in time $2^{o(v+e)}$, all in polynomial space. The encodings are explicit: one variable per\nvertex and a clause of two literals per edge (lines 107--112); $q$\nvariables per vertex with ``exactly one colour'' and ``no equal\ncolours'' clauses, a $q$-CNF on $qv$ variables (lines 112--115); one\nvariable per edge and a linear-size circuit for ``exactly one selected\nedge meets each vertex'', reduced to 3-CNF with $O(v+e)$ variables, with\nthe extra variables fixed by the edge bits so that the count is\npreserved (lines 116--119). \\textbf{Supported}: the conclusion is\n$2^{o(\\cdot)}$, which needs only that $\\rhon$ grows more slowly than $n$\n\\leanref{rho-little-o}, and each encoding preserves the count into a\nfixed-width CNF on $O(v)$ or $O(v+e)$ variables. None of the three\nencodings is in the library.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.4, a circuit lower bound for $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$ (p.~3, lines 121--127).}\n``The fixed-density case of Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound for\n$\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$'' (line 121). A circuit lower bound is a proof that some problem needs large circuits; $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$ is a class of problems whose definition is not needed here. The claim: there is a problem $L$ in that class and a constant $c > 0$ such that $L$ needs circuits of more than $c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ gates for infinitely many $n$ (lines 123--127). The proof is Appendix~C.1 (p.~51--52, lines 2925--2967), an argument of Williams's,\nand it invokes the paper-only exponent in so many words: ``Put\n$a(t) = \\sqrt{\\log_2 t}$. Testing whether the count in the\nconstant-density part of Theorem 1.1 is positive decides 3-SAT with $V$\nvariables and at most $5V$ clauses in time $V^b\\,2^{AV/a(V)}$ for fixed\n$A, b > 0$'' (p.~52, lines 2949--2950); the threshold\n$s_n = \\lfloor\\eta n\\lfloor a(n)\\rfloor\\rfloor$ is chosen against that\n$a$ (line 2953), and the constant $c$ comes out as\n$\\sqrt{\\eta/(d\\log 2)}$ (line 2967). \\textbf{Paper-only}: the\nquantitative bound $c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ is tied to $2^{AV/\\sqrt{\\log V}}$,\nwhich the library does not contain. What the same argument would give\nfrom the verified exponent is a question about the manuscript's proof,\nwhich we do not examine (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-not}).\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary C.1, the class $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$ has no linear-size circuits (p.~51, lines 2912--2916).}\n``Williams proved that if ETH is false, then $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$\ndoes not have linear-size circuits [Wil13, Theorem 6.1]. Apply this\nimplication to the deterministic subexponential-time 3-SAT algorithm\nsupplied by Theorem 1.1.'' \\textbf{Supported}: the only input is a\ndeterministic $2^{o(n)}$ 3-SAT decision, which the verified statement\nprovides on its answer tape for $k = 3$; the implication is a citation.\nThe manuscript's own scope sentence applies: the corollary ``does not\nassert a superpolynomial circuit lower bound for NP'' (lines 2923--2924).\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary C.2, inverting constant-locality maps (p.~52, lines 2974--2977).}\nFor a Boolean map whose every output bit reads at most $d$ input bits (a ``constant-locality'' map), the number of inputs mapping to a given output, and one such input, in\ndeterministic time $(n+m)^{O_d(1)}\\,2^{O_d(\\rho(n))}$ and polynomial\nspace, for $n \\ge 20$. Stated with $\\rho(n)$. The proof writes one\nclause per forbidden local assignment, a CNF of width at most $d$ on the\noriginal $n$ variables with at most $2^d m$ clauses whose satisfying\nassignments are exactly the wanted inputs, and applies Theorem~1.1 with\n$k = \\max\\{2, d\\}$ (lines 2979--2988). \\textbf{Supported}: the encoding\nis on paper; the counter and the exponent are the verified ones. The remark that follows, on Goldreich's candidate one-way functions (functions easy to compute and conjectured hard to invert; p.~53, lines 2994--2999), inherits the same status and the manuscript's own\nlimit, ``This does not rule out one-wayness against polynomial-time\nalgorithms'' (line 2999).\n\n\\subsection{Appendix D: the contraction route}\n\\label{sec:beyond-appendix-d}\n\nAppendix~D of the manuscript, ``A sharper bound for exact counting''\n(p.~53--64, from line 3001), proves Theorem~1.1 ``by contracting Boolean tensor networks. The two running times reflect the number of wires in the networks'' (lines 3002--3003). A tensor network is a way of writing one large sum as a product of small tables; the terms in the rest of this paragraph are the manuscript's, and we do not define them further. Theorem~D.1 (p.~53, lines\n3017--3028) is the contraction theorem: a bipartite tensor network with\nat most $Cs$ edges, maximum degree at most $\\lceil s^{9/10}\\rceil$ and\nentries in $(\\mathbb{Z}/2^{P_0}\\mathbb{Z})[V]/(V^\\ell)$ is contracted in\n$(s+L)^{O(1)}\\,2^{O(s/\\sqrt{\\log s})}$ bit operations and polynomial\nspace. The fixed-density proof (p.~54, lines 3045--3064) encodes a\nformula as a network with $O_k(n+m)$ edges and applies Theorem~D.1 with\n$s = n + m$; the arbitrary-density proof (p.~57, lines 3222--3246)\ncombines the sparsification of Section~6 with Lemma~D.2 (p.~54, line\n3069) and reaches the exponent $O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})$ at line\n3243. Section~D.4 changes the logarithm convention for its own proof,\n``log denotes a base-two logarithm and ln denotes a natural logarithm''\n(p.~57, lines 3251--3252). All of this is \\textbf{paper-only}: no\ndeclaration about tensor networks, contraction, Hermite recovery or\npackets exists in the family's 3,977 files, by the identifier search of\nSection~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}. We make no statement about Appendix~D's\nargument; we did not read it for correctness.\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses of \\S1.1.1 and the fine-grained remarks of \\S1.1.3}\n\\label{sec:beyond-hypotheses}\n\n\\paragraph{\\S1.1.1 (p.~4, lines 187--191).}\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [\\ldots] and SETH [\\ldots], and of Gap-ETH.''\nAnd \\S1.1.2 (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves\n\\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time and\ntherefore refutes \\#ETH.'' These are the four named hypotheses about how\nhard satisfiability is, their randomised forms, and the counting\nhypothesis; Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} states each one in words.\n\\textbf{Supported}, and by the shortest argument in the manuscript: the\nverified statement is a deterministic machine whose answer tape says\nwhether the count is positive and whose count tape carries the count,\nwithin a subexponential bound, for each fixed $k \\ge 2$; each hypothesis\nis the denial of that. The hypotheses themselves are not defined in the\nlibrary (the strings ``ETH'' and ``SETH'' occur in the collection's Lean\nonly in two files of a different family,\n\\clonefile{lean/OAI/Combinatorics/ParityLifts/}, which take an ETH-like\nproposition as a hypothesis and are not among the files the proof depends on, its closure, Section~\\ref{sec:proof}).\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences} says exactly how the verified statement\nnegates each one. The two sentences the manuscript adds at lines\n191--194, on unconditional lower bounds for restricted proof systems and\non Williams's 2016 MA-SETH protocol, are context, not claims about the\nresult.\n\n\\paragraph{\\S1.1.3 (p.~5, lines 213--243).}\nAfter a list of lower bounds proved from ETH or SETH (lines 213--225),\nthe manuscript writes: ``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis\nfor the hardness claims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from\nweaker hypotheses that may still hold'' (lines 226--227). It names the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, a hypothesis about a different problem (lines 229--232), and the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$\nalgorithm, adding: ``Theorem 1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each\nfixed clause width $k$, while this hypothesis allows the width to grow\nwith $n$'' (lines 235--236). These are \\textbf{paper-only} remarks in\nthe sense that nothing about any other hypothesis or any reduction is in\nthe library; they are also the manuscript's own statement of what the\nresult does not do, and Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} rests on them.\nThe one positive transfer, Set Splitting with sets of size at most $k$\nby one variable per element and two clauses per set (lines 237--243), is\n\\textbf{supported} in the same sense as Corollary~1.3: an encoding on\npaper into the verified counter, inheriting $\\rhon$. The caveat at\np.~2, lines 95--99, that $2^{o(n)}$ bounds ``do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions,'' is not a claim but a limit the\nmanuscript places on its own result; it is quoted again in\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences}.\n\n\\subsection{What the library carries beyond the comparator statement}\n\\label{sec:beyond-lean}\n\n``Paper-only'' is defined against the whole library at commit\n\\texttt{4eaee875}, not only against the comparator file, so we say what\nelse the library proves about this family. The file\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} (201 lines) states five theorems,\neach followed by a \\texttt{\\#print axioms} command, which asks Lean to\nlist the axioms a proof uses (lines 197--201). The replay's build log\ncarries the kernel's answer for all five: the three standard axioms\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/run2/axioms_in_build.txt}).\n\\begin{itemize}\\sloppy\n\\item \\texttt{compiled\\_exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 10) and\n  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 74): the comparator statement,\n  first over the explicit 80-register, 1,220-tape program and then over\n  the machine it compiles to (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} (lines 104--147): the same conclusion\n  with the polynomial factor written as $(n + |F| + 1)^{d}$ instead of\n  $(L+1)^d$, the exponent as $A\\cdot\\mathtt{rho}\\ n$ in base 2, and the\n  condition $20 \\le n$ (line 109); its proof sets its own constants to twice, twice and four times the comparator theorem's $d$, $e$ and $A$ (line 132). This is\n  Proposition~6.4's $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ form in the\n  library, so the manuscript's polynomial factor is verified as well as\n  its exponent; it is \\textbf{verified}, and it is still the weaker\n  exponent.\n\\item \\texttt{sat\\_polynomial\\_space} (lines 149--183), for $k \\ge 3$:\n  the answer bit is true exactly when the formula is satisfiable\n  (\\texttt{answer = true $\\leftrightarrow$ SAT.Satisfiable F}), the\n  decision form made explicit.\n\\item \\texttt{original\\_sat\\_main} (line 184): the sentence named\n  \\path{SqrtLog.Statement.MainTheorem}, a Lean \\texttt{Prop}\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:lean}; \\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementCanonical.lean},\n  line 37), a decision-only\n  target for every width $k \\ge 3$ with the same \\texttt{runtimeBound},\n  stated there without proof (``no inhabitant is supplied here'') and proved in \\path{Final.lean}.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\begin{sloppypar}\nBeyond these, a search for names over the family's 3,977 \\texttt{.lean}\nfiles, by the read-only script \\repofile{paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py},\nfinds no occurrence of \\texttt{SimpleGraph}, \\texttt{PerfectMatching},\n\\texttt{Coloring}, \\texttt{IndependentSet}, \\texttt{CircuitSize},\n\\texttt{OrthogonalVectors}, \\texttt{MaxSat}, \\texttt{TensorNetwork},\n\\texttt{HermiteInterp}, \\texttt{ENP}, \\texttt{SIZE},\n\\texttt{SetSplitting}, \\texttt{GapETH} or \\texttt{NSETH} as an\nidentifier: none of the corollaries, none of Appendix~D, and none of\nthe hypotheses is formalised. The comparator's JSON,\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}, names one\ntheorem, \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, and no definition. A text\nsearch is necessary and not sufficient (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); here\nit is used to show absence, where a miss would require a corollary to\nbe formalised under none of the words its own statement uses.\n\\end{sloppypar}\n\n\\subsection{The table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:beyond-claims} lists every claim examined above with its\nstatus and the one-line reason.\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{Every claim of the manuscript examined in this section: where\nit is stated (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of the committed\nextract), its status in the sense of Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-statuses}\n(verified, supported, or paper-only), and the one-line reason.\nGenerated, with every page and fragment checked against the extract and\nthe clone, by \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}\n\\label{tab:beyond-claims}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/beyond_claims}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:beyond-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the paper-only proofs.} Appendix~D,\n  Appendix~C.1 and the two sharper bounds of Theorem~1.1 were located\n  and quoted, not read for correctness. ``Paper-only'' means not in the\n  Lean library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}; it does not mean wrong, and\n  nothing here suggests it.\n\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the supported arguments either.} The\n  reductions behind Corollaries~1.2, 1.3, C.1, C.2 and the Set Splitting\n  transfer are standard in kind and short, and we checked only which\n  property of the counter they use. Whether each is correct as written\n  is the manuscript's, not ours.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about what the verified exponent would yield\n  through Corollary~1.4's argument.} A reader can rerun Appendix~C.1\n  with $a(t)$ replaced by the verified rate; we did not, because it is\n  a question about a paper-only proof.\n\\item \\textbf{No constants.} Every constant in this section is as\n  unknown as the comparator's $C, A, S, d, e$; the comparisons of\n  Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} are at constant $1$ and say nothing\n  about any machine's running time.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the collection.} Whether the sharper bounds\n  have since been formalised elsewhere, or the manuscript revised, is\n  not examined; what is read is the commit and the dated manuscript.\n\\end{itemize}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BS8iTPhUBtjXKQ5ZWkiL5m","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
