{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K6LvBJZUVrP19X5MMtr4pu","type":"tool_result","content":"% What follows, in general terms -- drafted by loop0015 item 05 (2026-11-05),\n% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the line + 1);\n% every comparator line is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone, read with cat/sed -n\n% only. Every page, line, fragment and the one table of this section are checked\n% and written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved. The section is in general terms\n% by the owner's rule (2026-11-05): no example from any width parameter, no\n% problem named beyond what the manuscript's §1.1.1 and §1.1.3 name. The\n% quantifier forms of the hypotheses and their negations are in Appendix B.\n\\section{What follows, in general terms}\n\\label{sec:consequences}\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:replay} read the verified\nstatement, drew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected the\nproof and replayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified\nstatement alone. Two things: which of the standard hardness hypotheses\nit refutes, and by what step; and what a lower bound that was derived\nfrom one of those hypotheses has lost. The manuscript answers both\nquestions itself, in its \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 (p.~4--5) and \\S1.1.3 (p.~5),\nand we quote it. What we add is the exact logical form of each\nhypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that denies it, and\nthe distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose\npremise has become false and a statement that has been refuted. Nothing\nhere names a problem the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 do not name,\nand nothing here is about any problem's complexity beyond what those two\nsections state.\n\nA \\emph{hypothesis}, in this section, is a conjecture about how hard\nsatisfiability is. The Exponential Time Hypothesis, ETH, says that\n3-SAT needs time exponential in the number of variables. Its strong\nform, SETH, says that as the clause width $k$ grows, no algorithm for\n$k$-SAT does much better than trying all $2^n$ assignments. Several\nvariants exist, and the manuscript lists them. A \\emph{lower bound\nderived from a hypothesis} is a theorem of the form ``if the hypothesis\nholds, then problem $X$ has no fast algorithm''; hundreds of such\ntheorems exist in the literature.\n\n\\subsection{The one property every refutation uses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-property}\n\nEvery refutation below rests on the same parts of the verified theorem\n(comparator file lines 194--217, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). For each\nfixed width $k \\ge 2$ there is one deterministic machine which, given\nany formula of width at most $k$ on $n$ variables, halts with the number\nof satisfying assignments on its count tape (lines 203--204), the\nanswer ``is that number positive'' on its answer tape and in its\nhalting state (lines 205--207), within the bound $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$\n(lines 210--211). Four features of that property do the work.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} The step rule is a function; there\n  is no random choice and no guess. A deterministic algorithm is also a\n  randomised algorithm, one that happens never to err, and also a\n  nondeterministic algorithm, one that happens never to guess. So each\n  refutation of a hypothesis about deterministic algorithms is a\n  refutation of its randomised and nondeterministic forms by the same\n  machine.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is subexponential in $n$ alone.} A formula of\n  width $k$ on $n$ variables has at most about $n^k$ distinct clauses\n  ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$, $k = 3$), so the input length $L$ is at\n  most a polynomial in $n$, and the whole bound is a polynomial in $n$\n  times $2^{A\\rhon}$. Since $\\rhon/n$ goes to zero\n  \\leanref{rho-little-o}, for every rate $\\delta > 0$ there is a size\n  beyond which the bound is below $2^{\\delta n}$; below that size the\n  bound is a constant depending on $k$ and $\\delta$. In the standard\n  notation, the running time is $O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every\n  $\\delta > 0$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}). This is a fact\n  about the limit, and the hypotheses are statements about the limit.\n  It coexists with Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s observation that\n  $\\rhon > n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, which is about where the\n  limit begins.\n\\item \\textbf{One machine per $k$, for every $n$.} The machine and the\n  constants are chosen after $k$ and before $n$\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there is no $n \\ge 20$ condition in the\n  comparator statement, though the manuscript's Theorem~1.1 carries one\n  (p.~2, lines 56--57). So the verified form is the strong form of the denial, one algorithm for every rate at once: a single algorithm that runs within $2^{\\delta n}$ times a\n  polynomial for every $\\delta$ at once, where the denial of a\n  hypothesis of the form ``for every $\\delta$ there is an algorithm''\n  would have allowed a different algorithm for each $\\delta$.\n\\item \\textbf{The machine model is the conservative one.} The\n  comparator's machine is a multitape Turing machine that moves one head\n  one cell or writes one symbol per step. Every step of such a machine is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models of computation (random-access machines, other kinds of Turing machine) in which the hypotheses are stated, so the bound transfers to those models with at\n  most a constant factor per step, which the polynomial absorbs. The\n  input is the manuscript's own encoding, of length polynomial in\n  $n + m$; converting from any other reasonable encoding is polynomial\n  too.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses, one by one}\n\\label{sec:consequences-hypotheses}\n\nThe manuscript's own sentence is (p.~4, lines 187--191):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [DHM$^+$14, Section 1] and SETH [CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH.''\n\\end{quote}\nand, for counting (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1)\nsolves \\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time\nand therefore refutes \\#ETH.'' We take each hypothesis in the form the\nmanuscript states it, with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nand say which part of the verified theorem denies it.\nTable~\\ref{tab:hypotheses} is the summary, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses} gives each hypothesis and its denial\nwith the quantifiers written out. In every case the only thing used is\nthe property of Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}; nothing is used\nfrom the manuscript's sharper exponents, which are not verified\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\n\\paragraph{ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 165--166): ``The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH)\nasserted that 3-SAT requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time in the\nworst case, and hence so does $k$-SAT for every fixed $k \\ge 3$ [IP01,\nTheorem~1],'' citing \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. In words: there is\nsome fixed rate $\\delta > 0$ such that no deterministic algorithm decides\n3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}$ times a polynomial, on all inputs. Its\ndenial: for every rate $\\delta > 0$ there is such an algorithm. The\nverified statement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta$, the same\nmachine, whose answer bit is the decision, within that time (item 2\nabove). This holds on every input of every size, so it refutes the\nhypothesis whether ``requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ time'' is read as a lower\nbound at infinitely many sizes or at all large sizes. In the vocabulary\nof \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where $s_k$ is the smallest rate $\\delta$ such that $k$-SAT has an algorithm within $2^{\\delta n}$ and ETH is the statement that $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement gives $s_k = 0$\nfor every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{SETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 166--169): ``The Strong Exponential Time\nHypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that $k$-SAT requires\n$2^{n(1-o_k(1))}$ time as $k$ grows. Precisely, for every\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic\nalgorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time,''\nwith the motivation attributed to \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}'s open\nproblems (lines 169--171). In words: however small a saving\n$\\varepsilon$ one asks for over trying all $2^n$ assignments, some clause\nwidth $k$ makes that saving impossible. Its denial: there is a saving\n$\\varepsilon$ that every fixed width $k$ allows. The verified statement\ngives more: for \\emph{every} saving $\\varepsilon$ and every $k \\ge 2$,\nthe machine for $k$ runs within $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}$ times a\npolynomial for all large $n$, because $\\rhon$ grows more slowly than\n$n$; below that size the exponential factor is a constant. The\npolynomial's degree depends on $k$, and that is exactly what the\nhypothesis's ``for a fixed $k$'' permits. The refutation does not pass\nthrough ETH; it reads the statement at each $k$. It is also standard\nthat SETH implies ETH, through the Sparsification Lemma of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, a tool that trades one formula with many clauses for a few formulas with few clauses (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses});\nso the refutation of ETH alone would already refute SETH. We note the\nimplication for the record; the direct reading above is what the paper\nrests on.\n\n\\paragraph{Gap-ETH, and its randomised form.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 172--174): ``The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis\n(Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants $\\delta, \\gamma > 0$\nfor which no $O(2^{\\gamma n})$-time algorithm can distinguish a\nsatisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment violates at least a\n$\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, even when $m = O(n)$ [Din16, MR17],''\nciting \\citet{Dinur2016} and \\citet{ManurangsiRaghavendra2017}; and\n(lines 175--176) ``The randomized version rules out an algorithm with\nthe same running time that distinguishes the two cases with probability\nat least $2/3$ on every promised input [CCK$^+$20, Conjecture 2.5],''\nciting \\citet{Chalermsook2020}. In words: it should be hard even to tell\na satisfiable formula from one that is far from satisfiable. Its\ndenial: for every rate $\\gamma$ there is an algorithm within\n$O(2^{\\gamma n})$ that tells the two apart, already when the number of\nclauses is proportional to $n$. Exact decision tells them apart, since a\nformula in which every assignment violates some clauses is\nunsatisfiable; and the verified machine at $k = 3$ decides within\n$O(2^{\\gamma n})$ for every $\\gamma$, at every number of clauses (item\n2). The promise is never used: the machine answers correctly on every\ninput, promised or not, with probability $1$, which refutes the\nrandomised form by item 1.\n\n\\paragraph{NSETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 181--183): ``The Nondeterministic Strong\nExponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every\n$\\varepsilon > 0$, some fixed $k$ admits no nondeterministic\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}\\allowbreak(n+m)^{O(1)}$-time algorithm for deciding whether a\n$k$-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a $k$-DNF is a\ntautology [CGI$^+$16],'' citing \\citet{Carmosino2016}. (A $k$-DNF is the mirror image of a $k$-CNF, an ``or'' of small ``and''s, and a tautology is a formula true under every assignment.) In words: even an algorithm allowed to guess should not be able to certify\nunsatisfiability with a saving over $2^n$. Its denial: there is a saving\nthat every fixed $k$ allows, for a guessing algorithm. The verified\nmachine's answer bit is \\texttt{false} exactly when the count is zero\n(lines 205--207), so it decides unsatisfiability deterministically within\nthe SETH bound of the previous paragraph for every $\\varepsilon$ and\nevery $k$; and a deterministic machine is a guessing machine that never\nguesses, so every convention for nondeterministic time is met. The\nmanuscript's ``decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiability'' (line\n188) is this.\n\n\\paragraph{\\#ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 204--205): ``The counting Exponential Time\nHypothesis (\\#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\nassignments of a 3-CNF requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time\n[DHM$^+$14, Section 1],'' citing \\citet{Dell2014}. Its denial: for every\nrate $\\delta > 0$ there is a deterministic algorithm within\n$O(2^{\\delta n})$ that outputs the exact count. The verified machine at\n$k = 3$ halts with the count on its count tape, a binary number of at\nmost $n + 1$ bits (lines 203--204, 208--209), within the same bound. This\nis the one hypothesis for which the yes-or-no bit is not enough and the\ncount tape is what refutes it. It is also the hypothesis the comparator\nstatement was written for: the count is the theorem's first conclusion\nand the decision its corollary.\n\n\\paragraph{The bounded-error randomised ETH and SETH.}\nThe manuscript names them by citation only (p.~4, lines 190--191):\n``[DHM$^+$14, Section 1]'' for ETH \\citep{Dell2014} and ``[CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1]'' for SETH \\citep{Cygan2016}. In each, the algorithm\nmay toss coins and must be right with probability clearly above one\nhalf on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an algorithm\nthat is right with probability $1$ (item 1), so the refutations of ETH\nand SETH above refute these forms by the same machine, with no change\nto the bound. We have not re-read the two sources' definitions; we\nstate the forms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the\nexact error threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the\ncited section and definition.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{The hypotheses the manuscript's \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 list, each in\nthe form the manuscript states it (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of\nthe committed extract), with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nthe logical form in symbols, and the part of the verified theorem that\ndenies it (lines 194--217 of the comparator file at \\texttt{4eaee875}).\n$A$, $C_k$, $d_k$ are the never-computed constants of\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}. Generated, with every page and fragment\nchecked against the extract and the clone, by\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py}.}\n\\label{tab:hypotheses}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/hypotheses}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{What the manuscript places beside the list.}\nTwo sentences follow the refutation paragraph (p.~4, lines 191--194):\nthe result ``does not conflict with unconditional exponential lower\nbounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13],''\nand ``In 2016, Ryan Williams refuted the Merlin--Arthur version of SETH\n(MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies $k$-CNF unsatisfiability\nin $2^{n/2}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3]''\n\\citep{Williams2016}. Both are context. The first says that a lower bound proved outright for one restricted way of writing proofs of unsatisfiability (``regular resolution'' is one such way) is not a statement about algorithms in general and is not touched. The second says that one member of the family, the version in which the proof of unsatisfiability may be checked with the help of randomness (a Merlin--Arthur protocol), was already false before this result. Neither is used below.\n\n\\subsection{What a derived lower bound loses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-lower-bounds}\n\nThe manuscript's \\S1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were\nproved from ETH or from SETH (p.~5, lines 213--225), for problems with\nexponential algorithms and for problems with polynomial ones, and then\nsays what its result does to them (lines 226--227):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness\nclaims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from weaker\nhypotheses that may still hold.''\n\\end{quote}\nWe say what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of the\ntwo standard shapes,\n\\begin{align*}\n  \\text{(E)}&\\quad \\text{ETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } 2^{o(n)} \\text{ algorithm for } X,\\\\\n  \\text{(S)}&\\quad \\text{SETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } (c - \\varepsilon)^{k} \\text{ algorithm for } Y \\text{ for any } \\varepsilon > 0,\n\\end{align*}\nwhere $n$ and $k$ are whatever measure of the input the theorem names\nand $c$ is its constant. In words: (E) says that if ETH holds, problem\n$X$ has no subexponential algorithm; (S) says that if SETH holds, no\nalgorithm for problem $Y$ beats the base $c$. Nothing below depends on\nwhat $X$ and $Y$ are.\n\n\\paragraph{The theorem stands; its premise is false; it derives nothing.}\nA theorem of shape (E) or (S) is an ``if \\ldots{} then''. It is proved\nby a reduction: a recipe that turns a fast algorithm for $X$ or $Y$ into\na fast algorithm for $k$-SAT. The recipe is unaffected by anything in\nthe verified statement; the ``if \\ldots{} then'' remains a theorem. What\nhas changed is that its ``if'' is now known to be false\n(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}), so the theorem can no\nlonger be applied: it derives nothing about $X$ or $Y$. That is the\nwhole of what ``removes one basis'' means.\n\n\\paragraph{The conclusion is not refuted.}\nA false ``if'' does not make the ``then'' false. The statement ``no\n$2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' and the statement ``no\n$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'' are untouched by anything in the\ncomparator statement, which speaks about $k$-CNF formulas and nothing\nelse. To refute either, one would need an algorithm for $X$ or $Y$, and\nthe verified statement supplies one only through a reduction in the\nopposite direction to the one the lower bound used. The manuscript says\nexactly this (p.~5, lines 237--238): ``To obtain a faster algorithm for\na target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in the\nother direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the\nnumber of variables.'' And it places the general caveat on itself at\np.~2, lines 95--99:\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Note that runtime bounds of the form $2^{o(n)}$ do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions ($2^{n^{o(1)}}$ would be needed for\nthis), so our result does not imply that general NP problems admit\nsubexponential time algorithms.''\n\\end{quote}\nSo for shape (E): a subexponential algorithm for $X$ follows only from a\nreduction of $X$ to fixed-width SAT in which the number of SAT variables\nis proportional to $X$'s own size measure, and the manuscript gives one\nsuch transfer (below). For shape (S): an algorithm for $Y$ with base below $c$ would need the SAT instance to be solved with an exponent a fixed fraction below $k \\log_2 c$; the verified exponent is $A$ times $\\rho$ of the SAT instance's variable count, $A$ is unknown with no value below $257$ on record, and nothing of the kind follows (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}). No\nstatement of shape ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is refuted by\nanything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the manuscript.\n\n\\paragraph{Hardness as a classification.}\nA statement of the form ``$Y$ is SETH-hard'' (or ETH-hard) records that\na reduction exists from $k$-SAT to $Y$ with the stated control of the\nsize measure. The reduction still exists, and the statement is still\ntrue as a statement about reductions. What it used to convey, that a\nfast algorithm for $Y$ is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no\nlonger conveys: a fast algorithm for $Y$ would refute SETH, and SETH is\nrefuted. The classification is now a fact about reductions and no\nlonger a fact about $Y$'s running time.\n\n\\paragraph{The weaker hypotheses the manuscript names are untouched.}\nThe manuscript gives two (p.~5, lines 229--236). The first is the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates to SETH:\n``The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies\nOVC [Wil05, VW19]'' (lines 231--232). That implication is a theorem\nwith a false premise, like (E) and (S): it says nothing about OVC either\nway, and nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal vectors.\nThe second is the form of SETH for formulas of unrestricted width, which\nwe quote with the manuscript's list of the problems it covers elided\n(lines 233--236):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The lower bounds for [\\ldots] also follow from the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$-time\nalgorithm for any constant $\\delta > 0$ [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem\n1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each fixed clause width $k$,\nwhile this hypothesis allows the width to grow with $n$.''\n\\end{quote}\nThe verified statement cannot reach this hypothesis, and the reason is\nvisible in the order of its words (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the\nmachine and the constants $C, A, S, d, e$ are chosen after $k$, and\nnothing in the statement bounds how they grow with $k$. For a formula\nwhose width grows with $n$ there is no single machine and no single\nbound in the statement at all; even the trivial observation that every\n$k$-CNF is a CNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis speaks about\nall widths at once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every\nlower bound that follows from it, is exactly where it was.\n\n\\paragraph{What a transferred algorithm inherits.}\nThe manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of size at most $k$ (given small sets over a universe of elements, colour the elements with two colours so that no set is all one colour; p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per element and\ntwo clauses per set give a $k$-CNF with ``one variable per universe\nelement, so for fixed $k$ Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential time in the\nuniverse size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,'' citing\n\\citet{Cygan2016}'s Observation~3.6. Section~\\ref{sec:beyond} tags this\nsupported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is the verified one.\nWhat such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee on the SAT\ninstance it builds, $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rho(n')}$ with $n'$ the number of\nSAT variables, and therefore the title's limitation with it: at $A = 1$\nthe exponential factor is above $2^{n'}$ for every\n$50 \\le n' < e^{5504}$ (Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}), with $C, A, d$\nunknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified\nstatement through a variable-controlling reduction is a subexponential\nalgorithm whose verified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size,\nthan trying every assignment of the SAT instance it constructs. That is\nnot a defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title,\nread through a reduction.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:consequences-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is\n  refuted}, for any problem, any size measure or any constant $c$. Such\n  a statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth\n  value, which is unknown.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' is\n  refuted} either, unless $X$ has a reduction to fixed-width SAT that\n  controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such\n  problem (Set Splitting) and we add none.\n\\item \\textbf{The unrestricted-width hypothesis is untouched}, and so is\n  the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, and so is every lower bound proved\n  from either; the verified statement is for each fixed $k$ and says\n  nothing about widths that grow with $n$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about any specific problem's complexity} beyond\n  what the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 state and we quote. The\n  problems the manuscript lists at lines 213--225 and 233--235 are not\n  discussed here, and no example from any other setting is given, by\n  the scope of this paper.\n\\item \\textbf{No re-reading of the sources.} Each hypothesis is stated\n  in the form the manuscript gives it, with the manuscript's citation;\n  we did not check that the cited sections and definitions state it in\n  the same words. If a source's form is stronger than the manuscript's,\n  the refutation of the manuscript's form may not reach it, and the\n  reader should check the source.\n\\item \\textbf{About the limit only.} Every refutation here denies a\n  statement about the limit and uses only that $\\rhon$ grows more\n  slowly than $n$. None says that any machine decides or counts\n  anything fast at any size; the constants are as unknown here as in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, and the verified bound is above $2^n$ on\n  every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the verified statement.} The manuscript's\n  sharper exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) are not used; where the\n  manuscript derives a consequence from them, it is not a consequence\n  of what is verified and is not claimed here.\n\\end{itemize}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1cad2094-11ee-40b3-a86f-8ba4c86457ea","uuid":"0a35791a-0b39-4278-bb89-e205dc849308","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:09:41.999Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"% What follows, in general terms -- drafted by loop0015 item 05 (2026-11-05),\n% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the line + 1);\n% every comparator line is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone, read with cat/sed -n\n% only. Every page, line, fragment and the one table of this section are checked\n% and written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved. The section is in general terms\n% by the owner's rule (2026-11-05): no example from any width parameter, no\n% problem named beyond what the manuscript's §1.1.1 and §1.1.3 name. The\n% quantifier forms of the hypotheses and their negations are in Appendix B.\n\\section{What follows, in general terms}\n\\label{sec:consequences}\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:replay} read the verified\nstatement, drew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected the\nproof and replayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified\nstatement alone. Two things: which of the standard hardness hypotheses\nit refutes, and by what step; and what a lower bound that was derived\nfrom one of those hypotheses has lost. The manuscript answers both\nquestions itself, in its \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 (p.~4--5) and \\S1.1.3 (p.~5),\nand we quote it. What we add is the exact logical form of each\nhypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that denies it, and\nthe distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose\npremise has become false and a statement that has been refuted. Nothing\nhere names a problem the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 do not name,\nand nothing here is about any problem's complexity beyond what those two\nsections state.\n\nA \\emph{hypothesis}, in this section, is a conjecture about how hard\nsatisfiability is. The Exponential Time Hypothesis, ETH, says that\n3-SAT needs time exponential in the number of variables. Its strong\nform, SETH, says that as the clause width $k$ grows, no algorithm for\n$k$-SAT does much better than trying all $2^n$ assignments. Several\nvariants exist, and the manuscript lists them. A \\emph{lower bound\nderived from a hypothesis} is a theorem of the form ``if the hypothesis\nholds, then problem $X$ has no fast algorithm''; hundreds of such\ntheorems exist in the literature.\n\n\\subsection{The one property every refutation uses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-property}\n\nEvery refutation below rests on the same parts of the verified theorem\n(comparator file lines 194--217, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). For each\nfixed width $k \\ge 2$ there is one deterministic machine which, given\nany formula of width at most $k$ on $n$ variables, halts with the number\nof satisfying assignments on its count tape (lines 203--204), the\nanswer ``is that number positive'' on its answer tape and in its\nhalting state (lines 205--207), within the bound $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$\n(lines 210--211). Four features of that property do the work.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} The step rule is a function; there\n  is no random choice and no guess. A deterministic algorithm is also a\n  randomised algorithm, one that happens never to err, and also a\n  nondeterministic algorithm, one that happens never to guess. So each\n  refutation of a hypothesis about deterministic algorithms is a\n  refutation of its randomised and nondeterministic forms by the same\n  machine.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is subexponential in $n$ alone.} A formula of\n  width $k$ on $n$ variables has at most about $n^k$ distinct clauses\n  ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$, $k = 3$), so the input length $L$ is at\n  most a polynomial in $n$, and the whole bound is a polynomial in $n$\n  times $2^{A\\rhon}$. Since $\\rhon/n$ goes to zero\n  \\leanref{rho-little-o}, for every rate $\\delta > 0$ there is a size\n  beyond which the bound is below $2^{\\delta n}$; below that size the\n  bound is a constant depending on $k$ and $\\delta$. In the standard\n  notation, the running time is $O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every\n  $\\delta > 0$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}). This is a fact\n  about the limit, and the hypotheses are statements about the limit.\n  It coexists with Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s observation that\n  $\\rhon > n$ on every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, which is about where the\n  limit begins.\n\\item \\textbf{One machine per $k$, for every $n$.} The machine and the\n  constants are chosen after $k$ and before $n$\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}); there is no $n \\ge 20$ condition in the\n  comparator statement, though the manuscript's Theorem~1.1 carries one\n  (p.~2, lines 56--57). So the verified form is the strong form of the denial, one algorithm for every rate at once: a single algorithm that runs within $2^{\\delta n}$ times a\n  polynomial for every $\\delta$ at once, where the denial of a\n  hypothesis of the form ``for every $\\delta$ there is an algorithm''\n  would have allowed a different algorithm for each $\\delta$.\n\\item \\textbf{The machine model is the conservative one.} The\n  comparator's machine is a multitape Turing machine that moves one head\n  one cell or writes one symbol per step. Every step of such a machine is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models of computation (random-access machines, other kinds of Turing machine) in which the hypotheses are stated, so the bound transfers to those models with at\n  most a constant factor per step, which the polynomial absorbs. The\n  input is the manuscript's own encoding, of length polynomial in\n  $n + m$; converting from any other reasonable encoding is polynomial\n  too.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses, one by one}\n\\label{sec:consequences-hypotheses}\n\nThe manuscript's own sentence is (p.~4, lines 187--191):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [DHM$^+$14, Section 1] and SETH [CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH.''\n\\end{quote}\nand, for counting (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1)\nsolves \\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time\nand therefore refutes \\#ETH.'' We take each hypothesis in the form the\nmanuscript states it, with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nand say which part of the verified theorem denies it.\nTable~\\ref{tab:hypotheses} is the summary, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses} gives each hypothesis and its denial\nwith the quantifiers written out. In every case the only thing used is\nthe property of Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-property}; nothing is used\nfrom the manuscript's sharper exponents, which are not verified\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\n\\paragraph{ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 165--166): ``The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH)\nasserted that 3-SAT requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time in the\nworst case, and hence so does $k$-SAT for every fixed $k \\ge 3$ [IP01,\nTheorem~1],'' citing \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. In words: there is\nsome fixed rate $\\delta > 0$ such that no deterministic algorithm decides\n3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}$ times a polynomial, on all inputs. Its\ndenial: for every rate $\\delta > 0$ there is such an algorithm. The\nverified statement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta$, the same\nmachine, whose answer bit is the decision, within that time (item 2\nabove). This holds on every input of every size, so it refutes the\nhypothesis whether ``requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ time'' is read as a lower\nbound at infinitely many sizes or at all large sizes. In the vocabulary\nof \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where $s_k$ is the smallest rate $\\delta$ such that $k$-SAT has an algorithm within $2^{\\delta n}$ and ETH is the statement that $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement gives $s_k = 0$\nfor every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{SETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 166--169): ``The Strong Exponential Time\nHypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that $k$-SAT requires\n$2^{n(1-o_k(1))}$ time as $k$ grows. Precisely, for every\n$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic\nalgorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time,''\nwith the motivation attributed to \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}'s open\nproblems (lines 169--171). In words: however small a saving\n$\\varepsilon$ one asks for over trying all $2^n$ assignments, some clause\nwidth $k$ makes that saving impossible. Its denial: there is a saving\n$\\varepsilon$ that every fixed width $k$ allows. The verified statement\ngives more: for \\emph{every} saving $\\varepsilon$ and every $k \\ge 2$,\nthe machine for $k$ runs within $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}$ times a\npolynomial for all large $n$, because $\\rhon$ grows more slowly than\n$n$; below that size the exponential factor is a constant. The\npolynomial's degree depends on $k$, and that is exactly what the\nhypothesis's ``for a fixed $k$'' permits. The refutation does not pass\nthrough ETH; it reads the statement at each $k$. It is also standard\nthat SETH implies ETH, through the Sparsification Lemma of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, a tool that trades one formula with many clauses for a few formulas with few clauses (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses});\nso the refutation of ETH alone would already refute SETH. We note the\nimplication for the record; the direct reading above is what the paper\nrests on.\n\n\\paragraph{Gap-ETH, and its randomised form.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 172--174): ``The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis\n(Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants $\\delta, \\gamma > 0$\nfor which no $O(2^{\\gamma n})$-time algorithm can distinguish a\nsatisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment violates at least a\n$\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, even when $m = O(n)$ [Din16, MR17],''\nciting \\citet{Dinur2016} and \\citet{ManurangsiRaghavendra2017}; and\n(lines 175--176) ``The randomized version rules out an algorithm with\nthe same running time that distinguishes the two cases with probability\nat least $2/3$ on every promised input [CCK$^+$20, Conjecture 2.5],''\nciting \\citet{Chalermsook2020}. In words: it should be hard even to tell\na satisfiable formula from one that is far from satisfiable. Its\ndenial: for every rate $\\gamma$ there is an algorithm within\n$O(2^{\\gamma n})$ that tells the two apart, already when the number of\nclauses is proportional to $n$. Exact decision tells them apart, since a\nformula in which every assignment violates some clauses is\nunsatisfiable; and the verified machine at $k = 3$ decides within\n$O(2^{\\gamma n})$ for every $\\gamma$, at every number of clauses (item\n2). The promise is never used: the machine answers correctly on every\ninput, promised or not, with probability $1$, which refutes the\nrandomised form by item 1.\n\n\\paragraph{NSETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 181--183): ``The Nondeterministic Strong\nExponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every\n$\\varepsilon > 0$, some fixed $k$ admits no nondeterministic\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}\\allowbreak(n+m)^{O(1)}$-time algorithm for deciding whether a\n$k$-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a $k$-DNF is a\ntautology [CGI$^+$16],'' citing \\citet{Carmosino2016}. (A $k$-DNF is the mirror image of a $k$-CNF, an ``or'' of small ``and''s, and a tautology is a formula true under every assignment.) In words: even an algorithm allowed to guess should not be able to certify\nunsatisfiability with a saving over $2^n$. Its denial: there is a saving\nthat every fixed $k$ allows, for a guessing algorithm. The verified\nmachine's answer bit is \\texttt{false} exactly when the count is zero\n(lines 205--207), so it decides unsatisfiability deterministically within\nthe SETH bound of the previous paragraph for every $\\varepsilon$ and\nevery $k$; and a deterministic machine is a guessing machine that never\nguesses, so every convention for nondeterministic time is met. The\nmanuscript's ``decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiability'' (line\n188) is this.\n\n\\paragraph{\\#ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 204--205): ``The counting Exponential Time\nHypothesis (\\#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\nassignments of a 3-CNF requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time\n[DHM$^+$14, Section 1],'' citing \\citet{Dell2014}. Its denial: for every\nrate $\\delta > 0$ there is a deterministic algorithm within\n$O(2^{\\delta n})$ that outputs the exact count. The verified machine at\n$k = 3$ halts with the count on its count tape, a binary number of at\nmost $n + 1$ bits (lines 203--204, 208--209), within the same bound. This\nis the one hypothesis for which the yes-or-no bit is not enough and the\ncount tape is what refutes it. It is also the hypothesis the comparator\nstatement was written for: the count is the theorem's first conclusion\nand the decision its corollary.\n\n\\paragraph{The bounded-error randomised ETH and SETH.}\nThe manuscript names them by citation only (p.~4, lines 190--191):\n``[DHM$^+$14, Section 1]'' for ETH \\citep{Dell2014} and ``[CDL$^+$16,\nDefinition 2.1]'' for SETH \\citep{Cygan2016}. In each, the algorithm\nmay toss coins and must be right with probability clearly above one\nhalf on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an algorithm\nthat is right with probability $1$ (item 1), so the refutations of ETH\nand SETH above refute these forms by the same machine, with no change\nto the bound. We have not re-read the two sources' definitions; we\nstate the forms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the\nexact error threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the\ncited section and definition.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{The hypotheses the manuscript's \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 list, each in\nthe form the manuscript states it (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of\nthe committed extract), with the manuscript's own source for that form,\nthe logical form in symbols, and the part of the verified theorem that\ndenies it (lines 194--217 of the comparator file at \\texttt{4eaee875}).\n$A$, $C_k$, $d_k$ are the never-computed constants of\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}. Generated, with every page and fragment\nchecked against the extract and the clone, by\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py}.}\n\\label{tab:hypotheses}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/hypotheses}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{What the manuscript places beside the list.}\nTwo sentences follow the refutation paragraph (p.~4, lines 191--194):\nthe result ``does not conflict with unconditional exponential lower\nbounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13],''\nand ``In 2016, Ryan Williams refuted the Merlin--Arthur version of SETH\n(MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies $k$-CNF unsatisfiability\nin $2^{n/2}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3]''\n\\citep{Williams2016}. Both are context. The first says that a lower bound proved outright for one restricted way of writing proofs of unsatisfiability (``regular resolution'' is one such way) is not a statement about algorithms in general and is not touched. The second says that one member of the family, the version in which the proof of unsatisfiability may be checked with the help of randomness (a Merlin--Arthur protocol), was already false before this result. Neither is used below.\n\n\\subsection{What a derived lower bound loses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-lower-bounds}\n\nThe manuscript's \\S1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were\nproved from ETH or from SETH (p.~5, lines 213--225), for problems with\nexponential algorithms and for problems with polynomial ones, and then\nsays what its result does to them (lines 226--227):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness\nclaims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from weaker\nhypotheses that may still hold.''\n\\end{quote}\nWe say what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of the\ntwo standard shapes,\n\\begin{align*}\n  \\text{(E)}&\\quad \\text{ETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } 2^{o(n)} \\text{ algorithm for } X,\\\\\n  \\text{(S)}&\\quad \\text{SETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } (c - \\varepsilon)^{k} \\text{ algorithm for } Y \\text{ for any } \\varepsilon > 0,\n\\end{align*}\nwhere $n$ and $k$ are whatever measure of the input the theorem names\nand $c$ is its constant. In words: (E) says that if ETH holds, problem\n$X$ has no subexponential algorithm; (S) says that if SETH holds, no\nalgorithm for problem $Y$ beats the base $c$. Nothing below depends on\nwhat $X$ and $Y$ are.\n\n\\paragraph{The theorem stands; its premise is false; it derives nothing.}\nA theorem of shape (E) or (S) is an ``if \\ldots{} then''. It is proved\nby a reduction: a recipe that turns a fast algorithm for $X$ or $Y$ into\na fast algorithm for $k$-SAT. The recipe is unaffected by anything in\nthe verified statement; the ``if \\ldots{} then'' remains a theorem. What\nhas changed is that its ``if'' is now known to be false\n(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}), so the theorem can no\nlonger be applied: it derives nothing about $X$ or $Y$. That is the\nwhole of what ``removes one basis'' means.\n\n\\paragraph{The conclusion is not refuted.}\nA false ``if'' does not make the ``then'' false. The statement ``no\n$2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' and the statement ``no\n$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'' are untouched by anything in the\ncomparator statement, which speaks about $k$-CNF formulas and nothing\nelse. To refute either, one would need an algorithm for $X$ or $Y$, and\nthe verified statement supplies one only through a reduction in the\nopposite direction to the one the lower bound used. The manuscript says\nexactly this (p.~5, lines 237--238): ``To obtain a faster algorithm for\na target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in the\nother direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the\nnumber of variables.'' And it places the general caveat on itself at\np.~2, lines 95--99:\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Note that runtime bounds of the form $2^{o(n)}$ do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions ($2^{n^{o(1)}}$ would be needed for\nthis), so our result does not imply that general NP problems admit\nsubexponential time algorithms.''\n\\end{quote}\nSo for shape (E): a subexponential algorithm for $X$ follows only from a\nreduction of $X$ to fixed-width SAT in which the number of SAT variables\nis proportional to $X$'s own size measure, and the manuscript gives one\nsuch transfer (below). For shape (S): an algorithm for $Y$ with base below $c$ would need the SAT instance to be solved with an exponent a fixed fraction below $k \\log_2 c$; the verified exponent is $A$ times $\\rho$ of the SAT instance's variable count, $A$ is unknown with no value below $257$ on record, and nothing of the kind follows (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}). No\nstatement of shape ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is refuted by\nanything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the manuscript.\n\n\\paragraph{Hardness as a classification.}\nA statement of the form ``$Y$ is SETH-hard'' (or ETH-hard) records that\na reduction exists from $k$-SAT to $Y$ with the stated control of the\nsize measure. The reduction still exists, and the statement is still\ntrue as a statement about reductions. What it used to convey, that a\nfast algorithm for $Y$ is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no\nlonger conveys: a fast algorithm for $Y$ would refute SETH, and SETH is\nrefuted. The classification is now a fact about reductions and no\nlonger a fact about $Y$'s running time.\n\n\\paragraph{The weaker hypotheses the manuscript names are untouched.}\nThe manuscript gives two (p.~5, lines 229--236). The first is the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates to SETH:\n``The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies\nOVC [Wil05, VW19]'' (lines 231--232). That implication is a theorem\nwith a false premise, like (E) and (S): it says nothing about OVC either\nway, and nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal vectors.\nThe second is the form of SETH for formulas of unrestricted width, which\nwe quote with the manuscript's list of the problems it covers elided\n(lines 233--236):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The lower bounds for [\\ldots] also follow from the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$-time\nalgorithm for any constant $\\delta > 0$ [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem\n1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each fixed clause width $k$,\nwhile this hypothesis allows the width to grow with $n$.''\n\\end{quote}\nThe verified statement cannot reach this hypothesis, and the reason is\nvisible in the order of its words (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}): the\nmachine and the constants $C, A, S, d, e$ are chosen after $k$, and\nnothing in the statement bounds how they grow with $k$. For a formula\nwhose width grows with $n$ there is no single machine and no single\nbound in the statement at all; even the trivial observation that every\n$k$-CNF is a CNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis speaks about\nall widths at once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every\nlower bound that follows from it, is exactly where it was.\n\n\\paragraph{What a transferred algorithm inherits.}\nThe manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of size at most $k$ (given small sets over a universe of elements, colour the elements with two colours so that no set is all one colour; p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per element and\ntwo clauses per set give a $k$-CNF with ``one variable per universe\nelement, so for fixed $k$ Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential time in the\nuniverse size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,'' citing\n\\citet{Cygan2016}'s Observation~3.6. Section~\\ref{sec:beyond} tags this\nsupported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is the verified one.\nWhat such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee on the SAT\ninstance it builds, $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rho(n')}$ with $n'$ the number of\nSAT variables, and therefore the title's limitation with it: at $A = 1$\nthe exponential factor is above $2^{n'}$ for every\n$50 \\le n' < e^{5504}$ (Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}), with $C, A, d$\nunknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified\nstatement through a variable-controlling reduction is a subexponential\nalgorithm whose verified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size,\nthan trying every assignment of the SAT instance it constructs. That is\nnot a defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title,\nread through a reduction.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:consequences-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is\n  refuted}, for any problem, any size measure or any constant $c$. Such\n  a statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth\n  value, which is unknown.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' is\n  refuted} either, unless $X$ has a reduction to fixed-width SAT that\n  controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such\n  problem (Set Splitting) and we add none.\n\\item \\textbf{The unrestricted-width hypothesis is untouched}, and so is\n  the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, and so is every lower bound proved\n  from either; the verified statement is for each fixed $k$ and says\n  nothing about widths that grow with $n$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about any specific problem's complexity} beyond\n  what the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 state and we quote. The\n  problems the manuscript lists at lines 213--225 and 233--235 are not\n  discussed here, and no example from any other setting is given, by\n  the scope of this paper.\n\\item \\textbf{No re-reading of the sources.} Each hypothesis is stated\n  in the form the manuscript gives it, with the manuscript's citation;\n  we did not check that the cited sections and definitions state it in\n  the same words. If a source's form is stronger than the manuscript's,\n  the refutation of the manuscript's form may not reach it, and the\n  reader should check the source.\n\\item \\textbf{About the limit only.} Every refutation here denies a\n  statement about the limit and uses only that $\\rhon$ grows more\n  slowly than $n$. None says that any machine decides or counts\n  anything fast at any size; the constants are as unknown here as in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, and the verified bound is above $2^n$ on\n  every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the verified statement.} The manuscript's\n  sharper exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) are not used; where the\n  manuscript derives a consequence from them, it is not a consequence\n  of what is verified and is not claimed here.\n\\end{itemize}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01K6LvBJZUVrP19X5MMtr4pu","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
