{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015PseLQVCJJExUCzxA1nH1N","type":"tool_result","content":"% For the practitioner -- drafted by loop0015 item 06 (2026-11-05), rewritten in\n% plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every number of this section is produced by\n%   nice -n 19 python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py --regenerate   (the measurement)\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py                           (table, figure, stdout)\n% from random 3-CNF the script generates; no MOSP or pathwidth instance, nothing\n% under solutions/. Timings are from one machine on one day with named\n% versions (tables/practitioner_meta.json). The two guarantee curves are shapes\n% at constant 1, and nothing here is a running time of the verified machine.\n% The arithmetic of the scaling is in Appendix B.\n\\section{For the practitioner}\n\\label{sec:practitioner}\n\nThe title compares two guarantees. One is the verified bound,\n$C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, with $C$, $A$ and $d$ never computed (comparator\nfile lines 136--139 and 210--211, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The\nother is the trivial bound for trying every assignment, $2^n$ times a\npolynomial. Neither is what a practitioner runs. What runs on a real\nmachine, when the question is whether a formula can be satisfied, is a\n\\emph{SAT solver}; the standard kind is called CDCL, for\nconflict-driven clause learning, a search that learns from each dead\nend it reaches. When the question is how many satisfying assignments\nthere are, what runs is a \\emph{model counter}, exact or approximate.\nNo implementation of the verified machine exists to run beside them.\nThis section measures the tools a practitioner has, on random formulas,\nand draws the two guarantees next to the measurements as shapes, so\nthat the reader can see where each sits. The point is small and the section says it once: the verified guarantee is not the binding one on any machine, because it is above $2^n$ for every $n \\ge 50$\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), while the solvers are many orders of\nmagnitude below $2^n$ at every size they were run at.\n\n\\subsection{Why the comparison is between guarantees and measurements}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-why}\n\nThe verified statement says a machine \\emph{exists} (comparator line 195\nopens with the existence quantifier, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The\nsolution's 3,977 files contain no command to run anything: no\n\\texttt{\\#eval}, no \\texttt{def main} and no input-output (\\texttt{IO})\naction. The\nscript of this section checks that, reading every file of the family\nwith \\texttt{open()} and counting those three patterns: 0, 0, 0. The\nlibrary proves that a machine with the stated bound exists and exhibits\none as a mathematical term, inside a development with 1,048\n\\texttt{noncomputable} declarations and no way to execute anything\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); nothing in the clone runs it on an input, and\nwe found no implementation elsewhere. So the verified algorithm's actual\nrunning time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing says\nit is slow, and nothing says it is fast. What is known is its\n\\emph{guarantee}, and a guarantee is compared with other guarantees. The\nmeasurements below are of other programs entirely. They are in the\nsection because the question a practitioner asks is not ``which\nguarantee is smaller'' but ``what would I run'', and the answer to that\nhas not changed.\n\n\\subsection{What was run}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-setup}\n\n\\paragraph{Instances.} Random 3-CNF on $n$ variables with $m = \\lfloor\n4.26\\,n \\rceil$ clauses, each clause three distinct variables with\nrandom signs, generated by the script from $(n, \\mathrm{seed})$; twenty\nseeds per size; $n = 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300,\n400, 500$. The ratio $4.26$ clauses per variable is where random 3-SAT\nswitches from mostly satisfiable to mostly unsatisfiable\n\\citep{Selman1996Hard}; random formulas are hardest to decide there\n(a method from statistical physics puts the threshold at $4.2667$, \\citealp{Mertens2006Threshold}). The sizes are chosen so that the\nsmallest ones are where the title's crossing is ($n = 50$) and the\nlargest are where a CDCL solver on random formulas begins to take\nseconds. These are random instances only; nothing is claimed about\nformulas with structure.\n\n\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL}, a CDCL solver,\nthrough python-sat 1.9.dev15 \\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT}, one\n\\texttt{solve()} call, for the yes-or-no answer; Ganak 2.8.0\n\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, an exact model counter, through the pyganak binding (which runs a simplification pass called Arjun and then the counter), for the exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0 \\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC,\nSoos2019BIRD}, an approximate counter, at its defaults $\\varepsilon =\n0.8$, $\\delta = 0.2$ (an answer within a factor $1.8$ of the truth with\nprobability $0.8$), with the count taken over all variables (its ``projection set''); and,\nat $n \\le 30$ only, exact counting by listing every solution with\nCaDiCaL, one at a time, which is not a counter at any larger size (its\ntime grows with the count, and the count grows exponentially in $n$)\nand is here as the independent check of the two counters where it can\nreach. Ganak and ApproxMC were installed from PyPI for this section; no\n\\texttt{sharpSAT}, \\texttt{d4} or other counter binary is on the host.\n\n\\paragraph{Protocol.} Each call runs in its own interpreter under\n\\texttt{nice -n 19}, at most eight at a time on the thirty-two cores of\nan AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (Linux 7.0.0, Python 3.12.13; the machine was\nshared with other work, load average at the start in\n\\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}), with a wall-clock budget of\n60~s per call. The time recorded is the wall clock (\\texttt{perf\\_counter}) around the\nconstruction of the solver object and the one call, inside the child\nprocess, with the interpreter's start-up and the import outside it. A\ncall over budget is killed and recorded as \\emph{censored}, that is, as\n``more than 60~s''. A size at which a tool finishes fewer than ten of\nits twenty instances ends that tool's climb to larger sizes. The whole\nrun took about twenty minutes (1,172~s).\n\n\\subsection{What came out}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-results}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:practitioner} gives, per size, the number of satisfiable\ninstances, CaDiCaL's median and maximum over the twenty seeds, the\nnumber of instances each counter finished with its median and maximum\n(a median over all twenty with censored runs counted as above the\nbudget, so a median is a number whenever more than ten finished), and\nthe two guarantees in the common scale of the next subsection.\nFigure~\\ref{fig:practitioner} draws the same.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Random 3-CNF at $m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$, twenty seeds per\nsize, one machine, 60~s per call. Columns: $n$ variables; $m$ clauses;\n``sat'', the number of satisfiable instances among those CaDiCaL\nfinished; for each tool, ``fin'', the number of instances it finished\nwithin budget, and ``med'' and ``max'', its median and maximum seconds\nover all twenty seeds with a censored run counted as $> 60$. The last\ntwo columns are the two guarantees as shapes: $2^n$ (trying every\nassignment) and $2^{\\rhon}$ (the verified bound), both with constant\n$1$ and both multiplied by one common factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$ that puts\n$2^n$ through CaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$ ($t_{20} = 1.28 \\times\n10^{-4}$~s). They are shapes, not running times. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{tab:practitioner}\n\\footnotesize\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n\\input{tables/practitioner}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{practitioner.pdf}\n\\caption{The same data on a logarithmic time axis, where each grid line\nis ten times the one below. Solid lines with markers are medians, dotted\nlines maxima, over the twenty seeds; a tool whose median is censored\nstops at the budget line. The two guarantees are drawn as shapes with\nthe common factor of Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner}, so that $2^n$ passes\nthrough the $n = 20$ measurement and $2^{\\rhon}$ crosses it at $n = 50$,\nwhere the verified exponent overtakes $n$\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The left panel keeps the measured\nseconds in view and lets the guarantees leave the frame; the right\npanel is the same picture on the guarantees' own scale. From\n\\texttt{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{fig:practitioner}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\paragraph{The checks.} On every instance where both finished, Ganak's\ncount equals the count by listing (40 instances at $n \\le 30$) and\nCaDiCaL's answer equals ``count $> 0$''; ApproxMC's estimate is within\nits factor $1 + \\varepsilon = 1.8$ of Ganak's on 178 of the 206\ninstances where both finished (it promises that with probability\n$1 - \\delta = 0.8$ per instance). The script exits non-zero if any of\nthese fails.\n\n\\paragraph{What the solvers did.} CaDiCaL's median is under a\nmillisecond up to $n = 80$, 2.5~ms at 100, 58~ms at 200, 1.5~s at 250\nand 3.7~s at 300 (maximum 20.5~s); at 400 it finishes 10 of 20 within\nthe budget and at 500 8 of 20, every finished one satisfiable, which is\nthe known shape of CDCL on random formulas at the threshold (the\nunsatisfiable half is the expensive half). Ganak finishes all 20 up to\n$n = 250$ (median 17.6~s there, maximum 45.7~s), 10 of 20 at 300 and\nnone at 400; its median is flat at 8.3~s and 6.2~s at $n = 100$ and\n150 and lower, 2.4~s, at 200, while its maximum rises throughout. The\nflat stretch looks like a fixed time budget inside the binding's\npreprocessing rather than counting difficulty; we report it as measured\nand tuned nothing (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06d). Where the count is\npositive it is small for the size: at $n = 200$ the fifteen satisfiable\ninstances have between 372 and $2.5 \\times 10^{12}$ solutions out of\n$2^{200}$ assignments. ApproxMC finishes all 20 up to $n = 200$ (median\n0.51~s there), 18 at 250, 11 at 300 and none at 400. The sizes at which\nthe three tools leave the budget, 300 for the counters and 400 for the\ndecision, are where this measurement ends; nothing is claimed beyond\nthem.\n\n\\subsection{The two guarantees beside them}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-guarantees}\n\nThe guarantees are drawn as shapes. Both are taken with constant $1$\n(the polynomial factors, $C$, $A$ and $d$ are all set aside), and both\nare multiplied by the same factor, chosen so that $2^n$ passes through\nCaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$. Because the factor is one number for both\ncurves, their ratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves,\n$2^{\\rhon - n}$: below $1$ for $n < 50$, equal to $1$ at $n = 50$ up to\nrounding, and above $1$ from there (it is $2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at\n$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). Scaling each curve to\nthe $n = 20$ point on its own would instead make the two touch at\n$n = 20$ and put the verified one above from there on; the common\nfactor is the honest choice because it keeps the crossing where the\nstatement puts it (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-scaling};\n\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a).\n\nIn that scale the brute-force guarantee reaches one second near\n$n = 33$ and the verified guarantee near $n = 35$. At $n = 60$, the\nmeasured CaDiCaL median is $3 \\times 10^{-4}$~s, against $1.4 \\times\n10^{8}$~s for $2^n$ and $5.4 \\times 10^{8}$~s for $2^{\\rhon}$: the\nmeasurement is below the two guarantees by factors of $10^{11.6}$ and\n$10^{12.2}$. At $n = 500$ the decision median is above the budget (8 of\n20 finished within 60~s), against $10^{140.6}$~s and $10^{192.2}$~s.\nNothing in those enormous factors is about the verified machine: the\ngap is between what a CDCL solver does on random formulas and what any\nexponential guarantee says, and it would be the same gap for the\nbrute-force guarantee alone. The one fact this section adds to the\ntitle is the order of the three objects: at every size in the table the\nmeasurement is far below $2^n$, and $2^{\\rhon}$ is above $2^n$ from\n$n = 50$ on, so the verified guarantee is the largest of the three at\nevery practical size and the measurement the smallest.\n\nOne remark on what the verified machine's \\emph{running time} would\nhave to be, were it ever run. Nothing in this section bears on it: the\nconstants $C, A, d$ are unknown (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), the\nmachine is a mathematical term, and a statement that something exists\nis not a program. The only values on record are the proof's own,\n$A \\ge 257$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ (Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n\\ref{sec:proof}); a guarantee of $2^{257\\,\\rhon}$ steps is a guarantee\nand not a measurement, and a different proof of the same statement\ncould carry any positive constants.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Timings on one machine, one day, named versions.} The\n  seconds are from one AMD Ryzen 9 9950X shared with other work, under\n  \\texttt{nice -n 19} with eight calls in flight, through Python\n  bindings; they are reproducible in shape and not in value, and the\n  versions are those named in Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner-setup} and\n  in \\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}.\n\\item \\textbf{Random instances only.} Random 3-CNF at the threshold\n  ratio is one distribution; formulas with structure can be harder for\n  CDCL at far smaller $n$ and easier for counters. Nothing is claimed\n  about any other distribution, and nothing about worst-case behaviour.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the verified machine's running time.} It is\n  unknown, in both directions; no implementation exists to run; the\n  guarantees drawn are at constant $1$ and the constants are never\n  computed.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about $k > 3$.} The statement is for every fixed\n  $k$; the measurement is at $k = 3$.\n\\item \\textbf{The counters are reported, not tuned.} Ganak ran through\n  its binding at defaults, preprocessing included; ApproxMC at its\n  default tolerance; a different configuration gives different seconds.\n  The check against listing is at $n \\le 30$ only.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement about which tool a practitioner should use.}\n  The section shows that the verified guarantee is not the binding one;\n  it does not rank solvers.\n\\end{itemize}\n% The statement in our Lean -- drafted by loop0015 item 07 (2026-11-05),\n% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every number here is printed by paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py (the copy, the\n% statement match, the sorry count; log paper3/lean/rediff.log) or by\n% `cd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean` (the axiom audit;\n% log paper3/lean/axiom_check.log). Line numbers in our files are at the\n% repository commit the log names; line numbers in the clone are at 4eaee875.\n\\section{The statement in our Lean}\n\\label{sec:lean}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement} and \\ref{sec:proof} read OpenAI's statement\nin OpenAI's files. This section describes the one thing we wrote in Lean\nourselves: a copy of the statement inside our own library, checked by\nthe same kernel, under our own name. The point is modest. Every sentence\nthis paper says about ``the verified statement'' is pinned to a named\nobject that Lean has accepted, and anyone who wants to use the statement\nas an assumption later can cite that one name. Nothing is proved about\nthe hypothesis here, and nothing is claimed beyond what the copy and its\naudit show.\n\nFour words first, each in one sentence. In Lean a \\emph{Prop} is a\nsentence that may be true or false. A \\emph{def} gives a sentence a name\nwithout asserting it. A \\emph{theorem} asserts a sentence and carries its\nproof. An \\emph{axiom} asserts a sentence without a proof. Our\nrestatement is a def: it names the sentence and asserts nothing.\n\nThe files are \\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.lean}\nand \\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean}, written\non 2026-11-04 for an earlier study of ours on a different question. The\ndirectory carries that study's name. The owner chose to leave the files\nwhere they are rather than move them (\\repofile{paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD},\nS3), and this paper cites nothing from that study: only the copied\nmodel, the restatement, and six small lemmas about them, all listed in\nAppendix~\\ref{app:lean}.\n\n\\subsection{The copy: definitions only}\n\\label{sec:lean-copy}\n\nThe comparator file of family~378,\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean}, has 220\nlines. Lines 1--190 define the objects the theorem speaks about: the\nformulas, their encoding as a string of bits, the multitape machine, its\nstep and its starting configuration, the exponent $\\runtimeScale$ and\nthe bound built from it, the count word, and the bookkeeping that\nrecords which tape cells a run has visited. Lines 191--219 state the\ntheorem \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} and end it with \\texttt{sorry},\nthe placeholder that a solution must replace. Line 220 closes the last namespace (a grouping of names).\n\nOur \\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.lean} is lines 1--190 and 220, and nothing\nelse. After a thirty-line header that records the source, the commit,\nthe file's hash and the Apache licence, the file is the copy\n\\leanref{copy-model}. The header says so, and a script checks it.\n\\repofile{paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py} reads both files with\n\\texttt{open()} only, takes everything in ours from the line\n\\texttt{import Mathlib} on, and compares it byte for byte with the\nclone's lines 1--190 followed by line 220. The diff is empty: 191 lines\non each side, identical. The script also checks the clone file's\nSHA-256 against the hash the header records, and that the clone is at\ncommit \\texttt{4eaee875}. The log is \\repofile{paper3/lean/rediff.log}.\n\nSo the copy holds the theorem's \\emph{vocabulary} and not the theorem.\nThe \\texttt{sorry} at line 218 is outside the copied range, and our file\nhas no \\texttt{sorry} and no axiom declaration anywhere (the same script\ncounts them: zero and zero in each of our two files). Everything in the\ncopy is a definition. A definition can be badly chosen, but it cannot be\nfalse; what the definitions mean is Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s\nsubject, and the copy adds nothing to it.\n\n\\subsection{The restatement}\n\\label{sec:lean-restatement}\n\n\\texttt{Frame.lean} defines a sentence named \\SubexpCount{}, with the\nwidth $k$ as its argument:\n\\[\n  \\SubexpCount\\ (k : \\mathbb{N}) : \\mathrm{Prop}.\n\\]\nIts body is the body of the comparator theorem for width $k$. The\ntheorem's two leading parts are handled thus: $k$ is the argument of the\ndef, and the condition $2 \\le k$ is carried by a second def,\n\\texttt{ExactCountAndSat}, which says ``for every $k \\ge 2$,\n\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\)'' (Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:subexpcount}\nand \\ref{lean:exactcountandsat}). In words, \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) is the\nsentence of Section~\\ref{sec:statement}: one fixed machine, on every\nformula of width at most $k$, writes the exact number of satisfying\nassignments, answers whether there is one, runs within the bound built\nfrom $\\runtimeScale$, and visits only polynomially many cells.\n\nThe same script checks that the body is the comparator's. It takes the\n23 lines after \\texttt{def SubexpCount (k : }$\\mathbb{N}$\\texttt{) : Prop :=}\nin our file (lines 85--107) and the 23 lines after the theorem's leading\nparts in the clone (lines 195--217, with the closing \\texttt{:= by}\nremoved), and compares them in two ways: with every run of white space\ncollapsed to one space, and line by line with the indentation stripped.\nBoth comparisons find the two texts identical, 1,073 characters. The\nonly difference is that each of our lines starts two spaces further\nleft, because a def's body sits two columns inside a theorem's. We call\nthis ``character for character''; it is a textual match, by the same\nmethod as Section~\\ref{sec:proof}'s, and it rests on the file we copied\nfrom being the comparator file at the commit we name.\n\nNeither def is proved here, and neither is an axiom. The truth of\n\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) for $k \\ge 2$ rests on OpenAI's proof and on\nSections~\\ref{sec:proof} and \\ref{sec:replay}, not on anything in our\nlibrary. Our library would be unchanged if the proof were wrong.\n\n\\subsection{What is proved, and what it means in words}\n\\label{sec:lean-lemmas}\n\nSix small lemmas sit beside the defs, each proved in a few lines\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:subexpcount-anti}--\\ref{lean:trace-projection}).\nNone of them says anything about any machine's running time or about\nwhether the hypothesis is true.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{A machine for width $k$ is a machine for every smaller\n  width.} \\texttt{SubexpCount.anti}: if \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) holds and\n  $k' \\le k$ then \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k'\\) holds, with the same machine and\n  the same constants, because a formula whose clauses have at most $k'$\n  members has at most $k$. \\texttt{ExactCountAndSat.subexpCount} unpacks\n  the ``for every $k \\ge 2$'' form.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is what it is.} \\texttt{runtimeBound\\_eq}: the\n  wrapper \\texttt{runtimeBound} $C\\,A\\,d\\,L\\,n$ unfolds, by definition,\n  to $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\cdot\\rhon}$, the formula\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement} reads. The proof is \\texttt{rfl}: the two\n  sides are the same term.\n\\item \\textbf{The exponent grows more slowly than $n$.}\n  \\texttt{tendsto\\_log\\_sq\\_div\\_sqrt} says $(\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0$\n  as $y \\to \\infty$, and \\texttt{runtimeScale\\_isLittleO} says that\n  $\\rhon/n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, which is written $\\rhon = o(n)$\n  \\leanref{rho-little-o}. This is the limit fact that\n  Sections~\\ref{sec:statement}, \\ref{sec:beyond} and\n  \\ref{sec:consequences} cite: in the limit the exponent is smaller than\n  $n$ by any factor one names. It is true at the same time as the\n  title's fact that $\\rhon > n$ for every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, and the\n  Lean says nothing about where the limit begins to bite.\n  \\texttt{isSubexpExponent\\_runtimeScale} is the same statement for\n  $A \\cdot \\rhon$, for every constant $A$ \\leanref{is-subexp-exponent}.\n\\item \\textbf{The time bound is a bound on the plain machine.}\n  \\texttt{Trace.config\\_of\\_locatedStep}: the comparator states its time bound on the \\emph{located} step, which is the plain step plus the bookkeeping of visited cells beside each configuration. A run of the located step projects to a run of the\n  plain step with the same number of steps, so the bound is a bound on\n  the machine itself \\leanref{trace-projection}. The one-line\n  \\texttt{LocatedCfg.monitor\\_config} is its helper.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{The axiom audit}\n\\label{sec:lean-audit}\n\nLean records, for every proved statement, the axioms its proof uses.\nThree are standard in Mathlib and in every proof of\nSections~\\ref{sec:proof} and \\ref{sec:replay}: \\texttt{propext},\n\\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{Quot.sound}. A fourth,\n\\texttt{sorryAx}, appears exactly when a proof was left out with\n\\texttt{sorry}. The audit \\repofile{paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean} prints\nthe axioms of 21 declarations: the ten copied definitions the statement\nrests on, the two defs, and the nine lemmas and helper definitions, in\nthe pattern of our first paper's audit. It is run from our library's\ndirectory as\n\\begin{verbatim}\ncd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean \\\n  > ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.log\n\\end{verbatim}\nand the log is committed. The result: nineteen lines on exactly the\nthree standard axioms; \\texttt{Trace} on no axiom at all (it is defined by construction rules and carries no proof); \\texttt{countWord} on two of the three (a proof may use fewer than the three, and fewer is never a concern); \\texttt{sorryAx} on none. The library it runs against is built with\nLean~4.34.0-rc2 and Mathlib at \\texttt{1192d624}, neither of which is\nwhat the clone pins (Lean~4.34.1, Mathlib \\texttt{d13f23b7},\nSection~\\ref{sec:proof}). That the copied model is accepted unchanged\nby a different Lean version and a different Mathlib revision is small\nevidence that it rests on nothing patched or private, and no more than\nthat. Our whole library builds with no \\texttt{sorry} and no axiom\ndeclaration, which the loop's gate checks after every session.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:lean-notclaim}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) is a def of a Prop, never proved\n  here.} Its truth is OpenAI's theorem, replayed in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:replay}; our library neither proves it nor assumes\n  it. Nothing in this section makes the refutation more or less certain.\n\\item \\textbf{The copy is definitions only.} The proof is not copied,\n  and could not be: it is 3,091 modules (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). What\n  is copied is the vocabulary of the statement, so that the restatement\n  reads as the comparator does.\n\\item \\textbf{No Lean computation evaluates the exponent.}\n  $\\runtimeScale$ is defined over the real numbers with real logarithms\n  and is marked \\texttt{noncomputable}; no \\texttt{\\#eval} can print\n  $\\rho(50)$ or $\\rho(50)/50$. Every number in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement} comes from a Python script, not from\n  Lean, and the Lean proves only the limit.\n\\item \\textbf{The match is textual.} ``Character for character'' means\n  the normalised texts are equal; it is not a check at the level of\n  \\texttt{lean4export}, and it rests on the clone being the repository\n  at the commit named.\n\\item \\textbf{The lemmas are trivial on purpose.} They fix the shape of\n  the bound and the limit of the exponent, and nothing else. No theorem\n  about any machine's running time, about the constants, or about the\n  manuscript's sharper exponents is stated, because none is verified.\n\\item \\textbf{The directory name is not a citation.} The files sit in a\n  directory named for another study of ours; nothing from that study\n  enters this paper, and the owner's decision to leave them in place is\n  recorded, not argued.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\endgroup","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1cad2094-11ee-40b3-a86f-8ba4c86457ea","uuid":"5c4755b6-378f-4899-a01d-69754878277f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:09:42.978Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"% For the practitioner -- drafted by loop0015 item 06 (2026-11-05), rewritten in\n% plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every number of this section is produced by\n%   nice -n 19 python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py --regenerate   (the measurement)\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py                           (table, figure, stdout)\n% from random 3-CNF the script generates; no MOSP or pathwidth instance, nothing\n% under solutions/. Timings are from one machine on one day with named\n% versions (tables/practitioner_meta.json). The two guarantee curves are shapes\n% at constant 1, and nothing here is a running time of the verified machine.\n% The arithmetic of the scaling is in Appendix B.\n\\section{For the practitioner}\n\\label{sec:practitioner}\n\nThe title compares two guarantees. One is the verified bound,\n$C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, with $C$, $A$ and $d$ never computed (comparator\nfile lines 136--139 and 210--211, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The\nother is the trivial bound for trying every assignment, $2^n$ times a\npolynomial. Neither is what a practitioner runs. What runs on a real\nmachine, when the question is whether a formula can be satisfied, is a\n\\emph{SAT solver}; the standard kind is called CDCL, for\nconflict-driven clause learning, a search that learns from each dead\nend it reaches. When the question is how many satisfying assignments\nthere are, what runs is a \\emph{model counter}, exact or approximate.\nNo implementation of the verified machine exists to run beside them.\nThis section measures the tools a practitioner has, on random formulas,\nand draws the two guarantees next to the measurements as shapes, so\nthat the reader can see where each sits. The point is small and the section says it once: the verified guarantee is not the binding one on any machine, because it is above $2^n$ for every $n \\ge 50$\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), while the solvers are many orders of\nmagnitude below $2^n$ at every size they were run at.\n\n\\subsection{Why the comparison is between guarantees and measurements}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-why}\n\nThe verified statement says a machine \\emph{exists} (comparator line 195\nopens with the existence quantifier, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The\nsolution's 3,977 files contain no command to run anything: no\n\\texttt{\\#eval}, no \\texttt{def main} and no input-output (\\texttt{IO})\naction. The\nscript of this section checks that, reading every file of the family\nwith \\texttt{open()} and counting those three patterns: 0, 0, 0. The\nlibrary proves that a machine with the stated bound exists and exhibits\none as a mathematical term, inside a development with 1,048\n\\texttt{noncomputable} declarations and no way to execute anything\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); nothing in the clone runs it on an input, and\nwe found no implementation elsewhere. So the verified algorithm's actual\nrunning time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing says\nit is slow, and nothing says it is fast. What is known is its\n\\emph{guarantee}, and a guarantee is compared with other guarantees. The\nmeasurements below are of other programs entirely. They are in the\nsection because the question a practitioner asks is not ``which\nguarantee is smaller'' but ``what would I run'', and the answer to that\nhas not changed.\n\n\\subsection{What was run}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-setup}\n\n\\paragraph{Instances.} Random 3-CNF on $n$ variables with $m = \\lfloor\n4.26\\,n \\rceil$ clauses, each clause three distinct variables with\nrandom signs, generated by the script from $(n, \\mathrm{seed})$; twenty\nseeds per size; $n = 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300,\n400, 500$. The ratio $4.26$ clauses per variable is where random 3-SAT\nswitches from mostly satisfiable to mostly unsatisfiable\n\\citep{Selman1996Hard}; random formulas are hardest to decide there\n(a method from statistical physics puts the threshold at $4.2667$, \\citealp{Mertens2006Threshold}). The sizes are chosen so that the\nsmallest ones are where the title's crossing is ($n = 50$) and the\nlargest are where a CDCL solver on random formulas begins to take\nseconds. These are random instances only; nothing is claimed about\nformulas with structure.\n\n\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL}, a CDCL solver,\nthrough python-sat 1.9.dev15 \\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT}, one\n\\texttt{solve()} call, for the yes-or-no answer; Ganak 2.8.0\n\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, an exact model counter, through the pyganak binding (which runs a simplification pass called Arjun and then the counter), for the exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0 \\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC,\nSoos2019BIRD}, an approximate counter, at its defaults $\\varepsilon =\n0.8$, $\\delta = 0.2$ (an answer within a factor $1.8$ of the truth with\nprobability $0.8$), with the count taken over all variables (its ``projection set''); and,\nat $n \\le 30$ only, exact counting by listing every solution with\nCaDiCaL, one at a time, which is not a counter at any larger size (its\ntime grows with the count, and the count grows exponentially in $n$)\nand is here as the independent check of the two counters where it can\nreach. Ganak and ApproxMC were installed from PyPI for this section; no\n\\texttt{sharpSAT}, \\texttt{d4} or other counter binary is on the host.\n\n\\paragraph{Protocol.} Each call runs in its own interpreter under\n\\texttt{nice -n 19}, at most eight at a time on the thirty-two cores of\nan AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (Linux 7.0.0, Python 3.12.13; the machine was\nshared with other work, load average at the start in\n\\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}), with a wall-clock budget of\n60~s per call. The time recorded is the wall clock (\\texttt{perf\\_counter}) around the\nconstruction of the solver object and the one call, inside the child\nprocess, with the interpreter's start-up and the import outside it. A\ncall over budget is killed and recorded as \\emph{censored}, that is, as\n``more than 60~s''. A size at which a tool finishes fewer than ten of\nits twenty instances ends that tool's climb to larger sizes. The whole\nrun took about twenty minutes (1,172~s).\n\n\\subsection{What came out}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-results}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:practitioner} gives, per size, the number of satisfiable\ninstances, CaDiCaL's median and maximum over the twenty seeds, the\nnumber of instances each counter finished with its median and maximum\n(a median over all twenty with censored runs counted as above the\nbudget, so a median is a number whenever more than ten finished), and\nthe two guarantees in the common scale of the next subsection.\nFigure~\\ref{fig:practitioner} draws the same.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Random 3-CNF at $m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$, twenty seeds per\nsize, one machine, 60~s per call. Columns: $n$ variables; $m$ clauses;\n``sat'', the number of satisfiable instances among those CaDiCaL\nfinished; for each tool, ``fin'', the number of instances it finished\nwithin budget, and ``med'' and ``max'', its median and maximum seconds\nover all twenty seeds with a censored run counted as $> 60$. The last\ntwo columns are the two guarantees as shapes: $2^n$ (trying every\nassignment) and $2^{\\rhon}$ (the verified bound), both with constant\n$1$ and both multiplied by one common factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$ that puts\n$2^n$ through CaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$ ($t_{20} = 1.28 \\times\n10^{-4}$~s). They are shapes, not running times. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{tab:practitioner}\n\\footnotesize\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n\\input{tables/practitioner}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{practitioner.pdf}\n\\caption{The same data on a logarithmic time axis, where each grid line\nis ten times the one below. Solid lines with markers are medians, dotted\nlines maxima, over the twenty seeds; a tool whose median is censored\nstops at the budget line. The two guarantees are drawn as shapes with\nthe common factor of Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner}, so that $2^n$ passes\nthrough the $n = 20$ measurement and $2^{\\rhon}$ crosses it at $n = 50$,\nwhere the verified exponent overtakes $n$\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The left panel keeps the measured\nseconds in view and lets the guarantees leave the frame; the right\npanel is the same picture on the guarantees' own scale. From\n\\texttt{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{fig:practitioner}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\paragraph{The checks.} On every instance where both finished, Ganak's\ncount equals the count by listing (40 instances at $n \\le 30$) and\nCaDiCaL's answer equals ``count $> 0$''; ApproxMC's estimate is within\nits factor $1 + \\varepsilon = 1.8$ of Ganak's on 178 of the 206\ninstances where both finished (it promises that with probability\n$1 - \\delta = 0.8$ per instance). The script exits non-zero if any of\nthese fails.\n\n\\paragraph{What the solvers did.} CaDiCaL's median is under a\nmillisecond up to $n = 80$, 2.5~ms at 100, 58~ms at 200, 1.5~s at 250\nand 3.7~s at 300 (maximum 20.5~s); at 400 it finishes 10 of 20 within\nthe budget and at 500 8 of 20, every finished one satisfiable, which is\nthe known shape of CDCL on random formulas at the threshold (the\nunsatisfiable half is the expensive half). Ganak finishes all 20 up to\n$n = 250$ (median 17.6~s there, maximum 45.7~s), 10 of 20 at 300 and\nnone at 400; its median is flat at 8.3~s and 6.2~s at $n = 100$ and\n150 and lower, 2.4~s, at 200, while its maximum rises throughout. The\nflat stretch looks like a fixed time budget inside the binding's\npreprocessing rather than counting difficulty; we report it as measured\nand tuned nothing (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06d). Where the count is\npositive it is small for the size: at $n = 200$ the fifteen satisfiable\ninstances have between 372 and $2.5 \\times 10^{12}$ solutions out of\n$2^{200}$ assignments. ApproxMC finishes all 20 up to $n = 200$ (median\n0.51~s there), 18 at 250, 11 at 300 and none at 400. The sizes at which\nthe three tools leave the budget, 300 for the counters and 400 for the\ndecision, are where this measurement ends; nothing is claimed beyond\nthem.\n\n\\subsection{The two guarantees beside them}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-guarantees}\n\nThe guarantees are drawn as shapes. Both are taken with constant $1$\n(the polynomial factors, $C$, $A$ and $d$ are all set aside), and both\nare multiplied by the same factor, chosen so that $2^n$ passes through\nCaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$. Because the factor is one number for both\ncurves, their ratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves,\n$2^{\\rhon - n}$: below $1$ for $n < 50$, equal to $1$ at $n = 50$ up to\nrounding, and above $1$ from there (it is $2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at\n$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). Scaling each curve to\nthe $n = 20$ point on its own would instead make the two touch at\n$n = 20$ and put the verified one above from there on; the common\nfactor is the honest choice because it keeps the crossing where the\nstatement puts it (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-scaling};\n\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a).\n\nIn that scale the brute-force guarantee reaches one second near\n$n = 33$ and the verified guarantee near $n = 35$. At $n = 60$, the\nmeasured CaDiCaL median is $3 \\times 10^{-4}$~s, against $1.4 \\times\n10^{8}$~s for $2^n$ and $5.4 \\times 10^{8}$~s for $2^{\\rhon}$: the\nmeasurement is below the two guarantees by factors of $10^{11.6}$ and\n$10^{12.2}$. At $n = 500$ the decision median is above the budget (8 of\n20 finished within 60~s), against $10^{140.6}$~s and $10^{192.2}$~s.\nNothing in those enormous factors is about the verified machine: the\ngap is between what a CDCL solver does on random formulas and what any\nexponential guarantee says, and it would be the same gap for the\nbrute-force guarantee alone. The one fact this section adds to the\ntitle is the order of the three objects: at every size in the table the\nmeasurement is far below $2^n$, and $2^{\\rhon}$ is above $2^n$ from\n$n = 50$ on, so the verified guarantee is the largest of the three at\nevery practical size and the measurement the smallest.\n\nOne remark on what the verified machine's \\emph{running time} would\nhave to be, were it ever run. Nothing in this section bears on it: the\nconstants $C, A, d$ are unknown (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), the\nmachine is a mathematical term, and a statement that something exists\nis not a program. The only values on record are the proof's own,\n$A \\ge 257$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ (Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n\\ref{sec:proof}); a guarantee of $2^{257\\,\\rhon}$ steps is a guarantee\nand not a measurement, and a different proof of the same statement\ncould carry any positive constants.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Timings on one machine, one day, named versions.} The\n  seconds are from one AMD Ryzen 9 9950X shared with other work, under\n  \\texttt{nice -n 19} with eight calls in flight, through Python\n  bindings; they are reproducible in shape and not in value, and the\n  versions are those named in Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner-setup} and\n  in \\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}.\n\\item \\textbf{Random instances only.} Random 3-CNF at the threshold\n  ratio is one distribution; formulas with structure can be harder for\n  CDCL at far smaller $n$ and easier for counters. Nothing is claimed\n  about any other distribution, and nothing about worst-case behaviour.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the verified machine's running time.} It is\n  unknown, in both directions; no implementation exists to run; the\n  guarantees drawn are at constant $1$ and the constants are never\n  computed.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about $k > 3$.} The statement is for every fixed\n  $k$; the measurement is at $k = 3$.\n\\item \\textbf{The counters are reported, not tuned.} Ganak ran through\n  its binding at defaults, preprocessing included; ApproxMC at its\n  default tolerance; a different configuration gives different seconds.\n  The check against listing is at $n \\le 30$ only.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement about which tool a practitioner should use.}\n  The section shows that the verified guarantee is not the binding one;\n  it does not rank solvers.\n\\end{itemize}\n% The statement in our Lean -- drafted by loop0015 item 07 (2026-11-05),\n% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every number here is printed by paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py (the copy, the\n% statement match, the sorry count; log paper3/lean/rediff.log) or by\n% `cd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean` (the axiom audit;\n% log paper3/lean/axiom_check.log). Line numbers in our files are at the\n% repository commit the log names; line numbers in the clone are at 4eaee875.\n\\section{The statement in our Lean}\n\\label{sec:lean}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement} and \\ref{sec:proof} read OpenAI's statement\nin OpenAI's files. This section describes the one thing we wrote in Lean\nourselves: a copy of the statement inside our own library, checked by\nthe same kernel, under our own name. The point is modest. Every sentence\nthis paper says about ``the verified statement'' is pinned to a named\nobject that Lean has accepted, and anyone who wants to use the statement\nas an assumption later can cite that one name. Nothing is proved about\nthe hypothesis here, and nothing is claimed beyond what the copy and its\naudit show.\n\nFour words first, each in one sentence. In Lean a \\emph{Prop} is a\nsentence that may be true or false. A \\emph{def} gives a sentence a name\nwithout asserting it. A \\emph{theorem} asserts a sentence and carries its\nproof. An \\emph{axiom} asserts a sentence without a proof. Our\nrestatement is a def: it names the sentence and asserts nothing.\n\nThe files are \\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.lean}\nand \\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean}, written\non 2026-11-04 for an earlier study of ours on a different question. The\ndirectory carries that study's name. The owner chose to leave the files\nwhere they are rather than move them (\\repofile{paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD},\nS3), and this paper cites nothing from that study: only the copied\nmodel, the restatement, and six small lemmas about them, all listed in\nAppendix~\\ref{app:lean}.\n\n\\subsection{The copy: definitions only}\n\\label{sec:lean-copy}\n\nThe comparator file of family~378,\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean}, has 220\nlines. Lines 1--190 define the objects the theorem speaks about: the\nformulas, their encoding as a string of bits, the multitape machine, its\nstep and its starting configuration, the exponent $\\runtimeScale$ and\nthe bound built from it, the count word, and the bookkeeping that\nrecords which tape cells a run has visited. Lines 191--219 state the\ntheorem \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} and end it with \\texttt{sorry},\nthe placeholder that a solution must replace. Line 220 closes the last namespace (a grouping of names).\n\nOur \\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.lean} is lines 1--190 and 220, and nothing\nelse. After a thirty-line header that records the source, the commit,\nthe file's hash and the Apache licence, the file is the copy\n\\leanref{copy-model}. The header says so, and a script checks it.\n\\repofile{paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py} reads both files with\n\\texttt{open()} only, takes everything in ours from the line\n\\texttt{import Mathlib} on, and compares it byte for byte with the\nclone's lines 1--190 followed by line 220. The diff is empty: 191 lines\non each side, identical. The script also checks the clone file's\nSHA-256 against the hash the header records, and that the clone is at\ncommit \\texttt{4eaee875}. The log is \\repofile{paper3/lean/rediff.log}.\n\nSo the copy holds the theorem's \\emph{vocabulary} and not the theorem.\nThe \\texttt{sorry} at line 218 is outside the copied range, and our file\nhas no \\texttt{sorry} and no axiom declaration anywhere (the same script\ncounts them: zero and zero in each of our two files). Everything in the\ncopy is a definition. A definition can be badly chosen, but it cannot be\nfalse; what the definitions mean is Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s\nsubject, and the copy adds nothing to it.\n\n\\subsection{The restatement}\n\\label{sec:lean-restatement}\n\n\\texttt{Frame.lean} defines a sentence named \\SubexpCount{}, with the\nwidth $k$ as its argument:\n\\[\n  \\SubexpCount\\ (k : \\mathbb{N}) : \\mathrm{Prop}.\n\\]\nIts body is the body of the comparator theorem for width $k$. The\ntheorem's two leading parts are handled thus: $k$ is the argument of the\ndef, and the condition $2 \\le k$ is carried by a second def,\n\\texttt{ExactCountAndSat}, which says ``for every $k \\ge 2$,\n\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\)'' (Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:subexpcount}\nand \\ref{lean:exactcountandsat}). In words, \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) is the\nsentence of Section~\\ref{sec:statement}: one fixed machine, on every\nformula of width at most $k$, writes the exact number of satisfying\nassignments, answers whether there is one, runs within the bound built\nfrom $\\runtimeScale$, and visits only polynomially many cells.\n\nThe same script checks that the body is the comparator's. It takes the\n23 lines after \\texttt{def SubexpCount (k : }$\\mathbb{N}$\\texttt{) : Prop :=}\nin our file (lines 85--107) and the 23 lines after the theorem's leading\nparts in the clone (lines 195--217, with the closing \\texttt{:= by}\nremoved), and compares them in two ways: with every run of white space\ncollapsed to one space, and line by line with the indentation stripped.\nBoth comparisons find the two texts identical, 1,073 characters. The\nonly difference is that each of our lines starts two spaces further\nleft, because a def's body sits two columns inside a theorem's. We call\nthis ``character for character''; it is a textual match, by the same\nmethod as Section~\\ref{sec:proof}'s, and it rests on the file we copied\nfrom being the comparator file at the commit we name.\n\nNeither def is proved here, and neither is an axiom. The truth of\n\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) for $k \\ge 2$ rests on OpenAI's proof and on\nSections~\\ref{sec:proof} and \\ref{sec:replay}, not on anything in our\nlibrary. Our library would be unchanged if the proof were wrong.\n\n\\subsection{What is proved, and what it means in words}\n\\label{sec:lean-lemmas}\n\nSix small lemmas sit beside the defs, each proved in a few lines\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:subexpcount-anti}--\\ref{lean:trace-projection}).\nNone of them says anything about any machine's running time or about\nwhether the hypothesis is true.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{A machine for width $k$ is a machine for every smaller\n  width.} \\texttt{SubexpCount.anti}: if \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) holds and\n  $k' \\le k$ then \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k'\\) holds, with the same machine and\n  the same constants, because a formula whose clauses have at most $k'$\n  members has at most $k$. \\texttt{ExactCountAndSat.subexpCount} unpacks\n  the ``for every $k \\ge 2$'' form.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is what it is.} \\texttt{runtimeBound\\_eq}: the\n  wrapper \\texttt{runtimeBound} $C\\,A\\,d\\,L\\,n$ unfolds, by definition,\n  to $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\cdot\\rhon}$, the formula\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement} reads. The proof is \\texttt{rfl}: the two\n  sides are the same term.\n\\item \\textbf{The exponent grows more slowly than $n$.}\n  \\texttt{tendsto\\_log\\_sq\\_div\\_sqrt} says $(\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0$\n  as $y \\to \\infty$, and \\texttt{runtimeScale\\_isLittleO} says that\n  $\\rhon/n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, which is written $\\rhon = o(n)$\n  \\leanref{rho-little-o}. This is the limit fact that\n  Sections~\\ref{sec:statement}, \\ref{sec:beyond} and\n  \\ref{sec:consequences} cite: in the limit the exponent is smaller than\n  $n$ by any factor one names. It is true at the same time as the\n  title's fact that $\\rhon > n$ for every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$, and the\n  Lean says nothing about where the limit begins to bite.\n  \\texttt{isSubexpExponent\\_runtimeScale} is the same statement for\n  $A \\cdot \\rhon$, for every constant $A$ \\leanref{is-subexp-exponent}.\n\\item \\textbf{The time bound is a bound on the plain machine.}\n  \\texttt{Trace.config\\_of\\_locatedStep}: the comparator states its time bound on the \\emph{located} step, which is the plain step plus the bookkeeping of visited cells beside each configuration. A run of the located step projects to a run of the\n  plain step with the same number of steps, so the bound is a bound on\n  the machine itself \\leanref{trace-projection}. The one-line\n  \\texttt{LocatedCfg.monitor\\_config} is its helper.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{The axiom audit}\n\\label{sec:lean-audit}\n\nLean records, for every proved statement, the axioms its proof uses.\nThree are standard in Mathlib and in every proof of\nSections~\\ref{sec:proof} and \\ref{sec:replay}: \\texttt{propext},\n\\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{Quot.sound}. A fourth,\n\\texttt{sorryAx}, appears exactly when a proof was left out with\n\\texttt{sorry}. The audit \\repofile{paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean} prints\nthe axioms of 21 declarations: the ten copied definitions the statement\nrests on, the two defs, and the nine lemmas and helper definitions, in\nthe pattern of our first paper's audit. It is run from our library's\ndirectory as\n\\begin{verbatim}\ncd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean \\\n  > ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.log\n\\end{verbatim}\nand the log is committed. The result: nineteen lines on exactly the\nthree standard axioms; \\texttt{Trace} on no axiom at all (it is defined by construction rules and carries no proof); \\texttt{countWord} on two of the three (a proof may use fewer than the three, and fewer is never a concern); \\texttt{sorryAx} on none. The library it runs against is built with\nLean~4.34.0-rc2 and Mathlib at \\texttt{1192d624}, neither of which is\nwhat the clone pins (Lean~4.34.1, Mathlib \\texttt{d13f23b7},\nSection~\\ref{sec:proof}). That the copied model is accepted unchanged\nby a different Lean version and a different Mathlib revision is small\nevidence that it rests on nothing patched or private, and no more than\nthat. Our whole library builds with no \\texttt{sorry} and no axiom\ndeclaration, which the loop's gate checks after every session.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:lean-notclaim}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{\\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\) is a def of a Prop, never proved\n  here.} Its truth is OpenAI's theorem, replayed in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:replay}; our library neither proves it nor assumes\n  it. Nothing in this section makes the refutation more or less certain.\n\\item \\textbf{The copy is definitions only.} The proof is not copied,\n  and could not be: it is 3,091 modules (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). What\n  is copied is the vocabulary of the statement, so that the restatement\n  reads as the comparator does.\n\\item \\textbf{No Lean computation evaluates the exponent.}\n  $\\runtimeScale$ is defined over the real numbers with real logarithms\n  and is marked \\texttt{noncomputable}; no \\texttt{\\#eval} can print\n  $\\rho(50)$ or $\\rho(50)/50$. Every number in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement} comes from a Python script, not from\n  Lean, and the Lean proves only the limit.\n\\item \\textbf{The match is textual.} ``Character for character'' means\n  the normalised texts are equal; it is not a check at the level of\n  \\texttt{lean4export}, and it rests on the clone being the repository\n  at the commit named.\n\\item \\textbf{The lemmas are trivial on purpose.} They fix the shape of\n  the bound and the limit of the exponent, and nothing else. No theorem\n  about any machine's running time, about the constants, or about the\n  manuscript's sharper exponents is stated, because none is verified.\n\\item \\textbf{The directory name is not a citation.} The files sit in a\n  directory named for another study of ours; nothing from that study\n  enters this paper, and the owner's decision to leave them in place is\n  recorded, not argued.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\endgroup","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015PseLQVCJJExUCzxA1nH1N","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgjMwu1i7iUY4qPK6rqYq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JBP6tfXu8pJrW12QxKLte1","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat latex/sec9_related.tex latex/sec11_data.tex latex/sec12_ack.tex latex/secA_appendix_lean.tex","description":"Read Section 9, 11, 12 and Appendix A"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2025,"cache_read_input_tokens":104084,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2025,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1cad2094-11ee-40b3-a86f-8ba4c86457ea","uuid":"7afdfdb9-a706-4736-800e-88df151b94d0","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:09:44.274Z","request_id":"req_011CgjMwtgMuVb1Aw2PeDuTW","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01JBP6tfXu8pJrW12QxKLte1":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3 && cat latex/sec9_related.tex latex/sec11_data.tex latex/sec12_ack.tex latex/secA_appendix_lean.tex","description":"Read Section 9, 11, 12 and Appendix A"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01JBP6tfXu8pJrW12QxKLte1":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JBP6tfXu8pJrW12QxKLte1","type":"tool_result","content":"% Related work and context -- drafted by loop0015 item 08 (2026-11-05).\n% Every quotation below is located by paper3/scripts/related_work.py\n% (python3 -I; --check verifies without writing), which reads the fetched\n% texts under paper3/related/fetched/ (hashes in Table 9.1), the clone at\n% 4eaee875, and the manuscript extract reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt.\n% Nothing is quoted from memory; nothing in the clone is run.\n\\section{Related work and context}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nThis section places the paper beside what others have written about the\nsame family and about the mechanism that checked it. There are five\nthings to place it beside: what the collection says about itself; the two\npapers that stated the hypotheses; a published human audit of an earlier\nbatch of OpenAI's results; the checking tool, called the comparator; and\nthe second proof checker, called nanoda, that we ran in\nSection~\\ref{sec:replay}. The section ends by saying where we looked for\nan earlier statement of the title's observation and what we found.\nTable~\\ref{tab:related-sources} lists every source with where it is on\ndisk, when it was fetched, and its hash or commit. We quote only what is\non disk; nothing in this section is quoted from memory or from the press.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The sources of this section. ``Clone'' is the read-only copy of\nthe OpenAI collection at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}; ``fetched'' files were\ndownloaded on 2026-11-05 and are kept under \\texttt{paper3/related/fetched/}\nwith their SHA-256 hashes. Written by\n\\texttt{paper3/scripts/related\\_work.py}.}\n\\label{tab:related-sources}\n\\input{tables/related_sources}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{What the collection says about itself}\n\\label{sec:related-collection}\n\nThe collection keeps a dated log, \\clonefile{history.md} (56 lines). Under\nthe heading ``October 26, 2026'' (line 11), the entry for this family is\none bullet, which we quote in full (line 18):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``\\emph{The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False.} The paper gives a\ndeterministic algorithm that exactly counts satisfying assignments of\nfixed-width CNF formulas in subexponential time and polynomial space. The\ncase of 3-SAT refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis. An independent\nworking group of computer scientists led by Russell Impagliazzo,\nRamamohan Paturi, and Ryan Williams will examine and explain the proof.\nAt the group’s request, we are making the materials public immediately so\nothers can examine them as well.''\n\\end{quote}\nThree things in this sentence matter for us. First, the group it names is\nled by two of the three authors of the papers that stated the hypotheses\n(Section~\\ref{sec:related-hypotheses}). Second, the verb is ``will'': the\nlog announces an examination to come. Nothing from that group is in the\nclone, and we fetched nothing from it, so this paper has no finding of\ntheirs to cite, agree with or disagree with. Whatever that group\npublishes about the proof will carry more weight on the proof than\nSection~\\ref{sec:proof} does, which only inspected and replayed. Third,\nthe log says ``the paper gives \\ldots{} subexponential time'' with no\nexponent; the exponent is in the scope note.\n\nThat scope note is \\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, seventeen lines, quoted\nin full in Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}. It is the collection's own statement\nof the gap between what Lean proves (the exponent with the squared\n$\\log\\log$ factor) and what the manuscript claims (two sharper\nexponents). Its table (line 17) links the result to the one comparator\nstatement, \\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean}.\nSo the collection itself records that the verified exponent is the weaker\none. What it does not record is what the weaker exponent means at any\nsize: that $\\rhon$, the verified exponent, is larger than $n$ for every\n$n$ between $50$ and $e^{5504}$, so that the verified bound with constant\none sits above $2^n$ at every size anyone will ever run\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). That is the title's observation, and the\nnote does not make it.\n\nThe third document is \\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/README.md},\nten lines. It says how the collection's checks are run: ``Install\n\\texttt{comparator}, \\texttt{landrun}, and \\texttt{lean4export}, and make\nthem available on \\texttt{PATH}.'' (line 3), then from the \\texttt{lean/}\ndirectory \\texttt{lake update}, \\texttt{lake exe cache get}, and\n\\texttt{lake env comparator} on a family's JSON (lines 7--9; the example\nis another family's). The JSON for this family has six fields\n(Section~\\ref{sec:related-comparator} reads them).\n\n\\subsection{The two papers that stated the hypotheses}\n\\label{sec:related-hypotheses}\n\nETH, the Exponential Time Hypothesis, says that 3-SAT (deciding whether a\nformula whose clauses have three literals each can be satisfied) has no\nalgorithm whose running time grows slower than every exponential in the\nnumber of variables. SETH, the Strong form, says that as the clause width\ngrows, the best possible base of the exponential tends to $2$, the base of\ntrying every assignment. Both are from \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}; the\nstandard setting of SETH, and the lemma that lets one move between the\nnumber of variables and the number of clauses, are from\n\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}. The manuscript cites both as\n\\texttt{[IP01]} and \\texttt{[IPZ01]} (extract page 66, lines 3733--3736)\nand states each hypothesis in its own words in its \\S1.1.1, which\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences} quotes with page and line. We do not hold\neither paper: \\repofile{literature/} has no file for them, and\n\\repofile{literature/MISSING.md} now records both with their DOIs. Every\nstatement of a hypothesis in this paper is therefore the manuscript's\nstatement of it, not the original's, and we quote nothing from the two\noriginals. The manuscript's own account of what motivated SETH is one\nsentence we can locate (page 4, lines 170--171): the improvements of the\nknown algorithms ``over exhaustive search diminish as $k$ grows [IP01,\nOpen Problems, item 2]''. Exhaustive search is the ``trying all $2^n$\nassignments'' of the title.\n\n\\subsection{A human audit of an earlier batch of results}\n\\label{sec:related-audit}\n\n\\citet{SienickiSienicki2026} is a published human audit of OpenAI's\nAI-generated proofs. We fetched its arXiv abstract page and API record\n(v1 of 3 August 2026, v2 of 25 August, v3 of 9 September 2026; 13 pages;\nprimary class cs.AI). It is not about this family. Its abstract says\nwhat it covers: ``We assess 18 chapter-specific reviews of the ten\nmathematical results announced by OpenAI on 1 August 2026, alongside\nreview standards, Lean formalizations, subsequent research, and\nmathematical references.'' The family this paper reads is dated 17\nOctober 2026, revised 25 October, and entered the collection on 26\nOctober (Section~\\ref{sec:related-collection}); it is not one of the ten,\nand the abstract mentions neither it nor ETH (the script checks). So we\ncite the audit for its method of review and for nothing about the proof.\n\nFour sentences of the abstract bear on how this paper was written. On\nrestraint: ``The article audits this review record without claiming a\ncomplete reconstruction of all ten proofs.'' On the finding: ``No\nconfirmed substantive mathematical error in a principal result remains in\nthe examined assessments, although review depth varies and some\ndependencies remain partly checked.'' On a hazard of reading proofs\nthrough text extraction: ``In Chapter 6, an apparent polarity error was\nwithdrawn after an overbar lost during PDF extraction was recovered from\nthe typeset source.'' And on what confidence should rest on: ``We argue\nthat confidence should combine formal checking, human reconstruction,\nindependent mathematical use, and a public record supporting correction\nof both proofs and reviews.''\n\nThe third of these is a warning we took. This paper reads the manuscript\nthrough a \\texttt{pdftotext} extract, the same kind of text in which that\noverbar was lost. That is why every quotation from the manuscript carries\nthe page of the PDF beside the line of the extract, why the Lean is read\nfrom its source files and never from a rendering, and why no claim in\nthis paper rests on a formula read from the extract alone: the formulas\nthat matter are the Lean's, re-typed in Section~\\ref{sec:statement} from\nthe \\texttt{.lean} file and re-checked by the kernel in\nSection~\\ref{sec:lean}. Against the fourth sentence, this paper is one of\nthe four things it lists, formal checking, replayed by us and by a\nsecond kernel, together with a reading of what the checked statement\nsays. It is not a human reconstruction of the proof: Section~\\ref{sec:proof}\ninspected the proof's files and did not re-derive the argument. We\nfetched the abstract only, and quote nothing from the paper's body.\n\n\\subsection{The comparator}\n\\label{sec:related-comparator}\n\nThe comparator is the program the collection used to check that the\nsolution file proves the challenge statement\n\\citep{LeanComparator}. We fetched its README from the\n\\texttt{master} branch of \\texttt{leanprover/comparator} on 2026-11-05\n(latest commit \\texttt{865edc14} of 2026-10-14, ``doc: migration\nnotice''; the repository is archived and under the Apache-2.0 licence).\nIts first lines say the tool has moved: ``This project has been merged\ninto core as of Lean release v4.35.0 in the form of \\texttt{lake\ncomparator} and the accompanying \\texttt{lake check}'' (line 3). The\ncollection pins Lean v4.34.1 (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}), one release\nearlier, so the stand-alone tool is the one its README names. The README\nopens: ``Comparator is a trustworthy judge for Lean proofs.'' (line 8).\n\nWhat it checks is stated as three guarantees for the theorems a JSON\nconfiguration names (lines 52--55):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``All theorems in \\texttt{Solution} that are listed in\n\\texttt{theorem\\_names} are guaranteed to:\\\\\n1. Prove the same statement as provided in \\texttt{Challenge}\\\\\n2. Use no more axioms than listed in \\texttt{permitted\\_axioms}\\\\\n3. Be accepted by the Lean kernel''\n\\end{quote}\nIn plain words: the solution proves exactly the sentence\nthe challenge posed, uses only the agreed axioms (the three standard ones\nin the example configuration, line 27), and passes the kernel, the small\ncore of Lean that checks proofs. How it checks them is in its\n``Internals'' (lines 184--200), six steps: build the challenge inside a\nsandbox; export its declarations with \\texttt{lean4export}; do the same\nfor the solution; ``Verify that all declarations used in the statement of\nall relevant theorems in \\texttt{Challenge} are the same as in the\n\\texttt{Solution} environment'' (lines 191--192); ``Verify that the body\nof all relevant theorems in the \\texttt{Solution} environment only uses\naxioms listed in \\texttt{permitted\\_axioms}'' (lines 195--196); and\n``Replay the \\texttt{Solution} environment into the Lean kernel'' (line\n197). The first guarantee is therefore a match of the statement's\ndeclarations as exported, not a textual comparison of the two files.\nThat is the check Section~\\ref{sec:proof} says we did not reproduce: our\nstatement match is textual (Table~\\ref{tab:proof-trust}, row (iv)).\n\nThe guarantees hold under six stated assumptions (lines 34--45). The one\nthat concerns the kernel is the fifth: ``The Lean kernel is correct (with\n\\texttt{external\\_kernels} this can be reduced to `At least one of the\nLean kernel or the \\texttt{external\\_kernels} is correct')'' (lines\n43--44). The sandbox is \\texttt{landrun} (line 10), a tool that confines\nthe build so that a malicious solution file cannot tamper with the\nchecker; the README notes that its trusted base ``naturally includes the\noperating system and hardware it is running on, plus its sandboxing\nmechanism'' (line 58). External kernels are optional: ``Comparator can\nadditionally check solutions with external kernels'' (line 70), and ``For\nbackwards compatibility reasons users may instead set\n\\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: true} to obtain a config that calls\n\\texttt{nanoda\\_bin}'' (lines 86--87). The tool ``was originally\ndeveloped by Lean FRO, with feedback from the AIMO team, in support of\nthe AIMO series of competitions'' (lines 207--208).\n\nFor this family the configuration has six fields\n(\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}): the\nchallenge module; the solution module,\n\\path{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}; one theorem name,\n\\path{SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat}; an empty\n\\texttt{definition\\_names}; the three standard axioms as\n\\texttt{permitted\\_axioms}; and \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false}. There is\nno \\texttt{external\\_kernels} field. So the check the collection\nconfigured for this family is the Lean kernel alone, under the fifth\nassumption in its unweakened form. Section~\\ref{sec:replay} is the\nweakened form, supplied by us: the same theorem's dependencies through a\nsecond kernel. Our \\texttt{bwrap} sandbox there played the role\n\\texttt{landrun} plays here, for the same reason, and we did not run the\ncomparator itself.\n\n\\subsection{nanoda}\n\\label{sec:related-nanoda}\n\nnanoda is the second checker \\citep{Nanoda}. Its README, fetched from\nthe \\texttt{master} branch of \\texttt{ammkrn/nanoda\\_lib} on 2026-11-05,\nsays what it is in one sentence (line 3): ``This is an external type\nchecker for the Lean 4 programming language and theorem prover.'' The\nrepository's description line is ``Library implementing type\ninference/checking functionality based on the Lean theorem prover''; the\nlicence is Apache-2.0. It reads the file \\texttt{lean4export} writes:\n``Export files can be created with lean4export'' (line 13), and ``Given\nthat lean4export already uses json as its export format, adding a json\nparser as a dependency doesn't increase the overall complexity of the\nproject'' (line 23). Its axiom rule is the comparator's: ``The average\nLean user will want to permit the three semi-official axioms, which are\n\\texttt{Quot.sound}, \\texttt{Classical.choice}, and \\texttt{propext}, and\nmost export files will also rely on \\texttt{Lean.trustCompiler}'' (line\n33); the export we checked contains the first three and no fourth\n(Section~\\ref{sec:replay}). Two kernel extensions for arithmetic on\nliterals are off by default and ``enable or disable the Nat and String\nkernel extensions'' (line 39); the replay turned both on.\n\nThe latest commit of the repository at the time of the fetch is\n\\texttt{8de7e0c4} of 2026-10-29, and that is the commit the replay built\nand ran (version 0.4.31; the script checks the replay's log against the\nfetched record). The checker is written in Rust and shares no code with\nthe Lean kernel, which is what makes it a second opinion. Its author's\nbook on external type checkers, linked from line 3 of the README, was not\nfetched.\n\n\\subsection{Where we looked for an earlier statement of the title's observation}\n\\label{sec:related-search}\n\nThe title says the verified guarantee is weaker than trying all $2^n$\nassignments at every practical size. We looked for an earlier statement\nof that observation in the texts this paper rests on, and in no others:\nthe collection's \\clonefile{history.md}, its scope note\n\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, the abstract of\n\\citet{SienickiSienicki2026}, the comparator README, the nanoda README,\nand the manuscript extract. The script searches each for the words such\na statement would be likely to use (``practical'', ``brute'', ``trying\nall'', ``exhaustive'', ``$2^n$'', ``constants are'', ``concrete''). Outside\nthe manuscript, none occurs. In the manuscript, ``practical'' occurs once\n(page 4, line 153), in a sentence about the practical value of SAT\nsolvers on industrial formulas, a different sense; ``exhaustive'' occurs\neleven times, as the motivation of SETH quoted above (line 170) and as the\nproof's own fallback for small inputs (lines 2480 and 2487, where the\nalgorithm uses ``exhaustive search'' below the cutoff\n$n \\ge 20$). The nearest earlier statements are the manuscript's own\nsentence that Lean verifies ``the weaker exponent'' (page 2, lines\n63--67; Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) and the scope note: both say the\nverified exponent is the weaker one, and neither says at what sizes the\nverified bound exceeds $2^n$, which is the title's point. Within these\ntexts we found no prior statement of it. The search is bounded by that\nlist: we did not search the literature at large, the working group's\noutput, or the press.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:related-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the working group's findings.} The\n  collection's log says the group ``will examine and explain the\n  proof''; we hold nothing it has written, and we neither anticipate\n  nor contradict it.\n\\item \\textbf{The human audit does not cover this family.} It is cited\n  for its method and its stated lesson about PDF extraction; its body\n  was not fetched, and no sentence of it beyond the abstract is quoted.\n\\item \\textbf{The two hypothesis papers were not read.} They are cited\n  as the manuscript cites them, and the hypotheses are stated as the\n  manuscript states them (Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}).\n\\item \\textbf{We did not run the comparator.} What we reproduced is\n  stated in Section~\\ref{sec:proof}'s Table~\\ref{tab:proof-trust};\n  the comparator's statement match by exported declarations is not among\n  it.\n\\item \\textbf{No claim that landrun, lean4export or nanoda is correct.}\n  Each is quoted for what its README says it does; the replay's\n  evidence is that two independent checkers agreed on this export, and\n  the trust left in \\texttt{lean4export} is stated in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:replay}.\n\\item \\textbf{``No prior statement'' is bounded by the list above.} Six\n  texts, read with a script; not a literature search.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\endgroup\n% The data, code and proofs statement (item 11 completes it), as in paper 1.\n\\section*{Data, code and proofs}\n\\label{sec:data}\n\\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Data, code and proofs}\n\n\\placeholder{Item 11 completes this statement: the repository, the scripts\nunder \\repofile{paper3/scripts/} that regenerate every number, the replay's\ncommands and log hashes under \\repofile{paper3/replay/}, the Lean names and\nthe axiom audit under \\repofile{paper3/lean/}, and the read-only clone of\n\\url{https://github.com/openai/math} at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}.}\n% Acknowledgements, with the disclosure of AI use in paper 1's form (item 11).\n\\section*{Acknowledgements}\n\\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Acknowledgements}\n\n\\placeholder{Item 11 writes the acknowledgements, with the disclosure of AI\nuse in the form of \\repofile{paper1/latex/sec8_ack.tex}.}\n% Appendix A: formal names, one row per result, as in paper 1\n% (\\leanentry{key}{result} \\leannames{...}); rows written by item 07 (2026-11-05).\n\\section{Formal names}\n\\label{app:lean}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nEvery Lean name the paper cites, with its file under\n\\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/} (the files are left where\nloop0014 wrote them, by the owner's decision recorded in\n\\repofile{paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, S3); the axiom audit is\n\\repofile{paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean} with its committed log\n\\repofile{paper3/lean/axiom_check.log}, and the byte-level re-diff of the\ncopy against the comparator file is \\repofile{paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py}\nwith its log \\repofile{paper3/lean/rediff.log}. Every row's declarations are\non the three standard axioms \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Classical.choice},\n\\texttt{Quot.sound} or fewer; none depends on \\texttt{sorryAx}. Nothing from\nthe other study that shares the directory is cited.\n\n\\begin{longtable}{@{}p{0.07\\linewidth}p{0.43\\linewidth}p{0.44\\linewidth}@{}}\n\\toprule\n & result & Lean names \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\endhead\n\\leangroup{The copy (\\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.lean}, lines 31--221 = comparator lines 1--190 and 220, byte-identical; definitions only, the namespaces of the source kept)}\n\\leanentry{copy-model}{The comparator's vocabulary: formulas and their satisfying-assignment count, the canonical bit encoding and its length, the multitape machine with its step and start, the trace, the running-time scale \\(\\rhon\\) and the bound built from it, the count word, the located step and the visited space} \\leannames{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SAT.count, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Encoding.encodeCNF, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Encoding.formulaBitLength, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Computation.PrimitiveMachine.step, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Computation.Trace, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLog.Statement.runtimeScale, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLog.Statement.runtimeBound, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.countWord, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.locatedStep, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.visitedSpace} \\\\\n\\leangroup{The restatement (\\texttt{Frame.lean}): two \\texttt{def}s of a \\texttt{Prop}, never proved, never axioms}\n\\leanentry{subexpcount}{The comparator statement for width \\(k\\) as a named \\texttt{Prop}, \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\): its body is the comparator theorem's body, identical after whitespace normalisation (1,073 characters, 23 lines)} \\leannames{MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.SubexpCount} \\\\\n\\leanentry{exactcountandsat}{The comparator theorem in full: for every \\(k \\ge 2\\), \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\)} \\leannames{MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.ExactCountAndSat} \\\\\n\\leangroup{What is proved about them (\\texttt{Frame.lean})}\n\\leanentry{subexpcount-anti}{A machine for width \\(k\\) is a machine for every width \\(k' \\le k\\), with the same constants; and the unpacking of the ``for every \\(k \\ge 2\\)'' form} \\leannames{MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.SubexpCount.anti, MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.ExactCountAndSat.subexpCount} \\\\\n\\leanentry{runtimebound-eq}{The bound unfolds, by definition, to \\(C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\cdot\\rhon}\\)} \\leannames{MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.runtimeBound_eq} \\\\\n\\leanentry{rho-little-o}{\\(\\rhon = o(n)\\): the verified exponent is subexponential in the limit, from \\((\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0\\)} \\leannames{MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.tendsto_log_sq_div_sqrt, MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.runtimeScale_isLittleO} \\\\\n\\leanentry{is-subexp-exponent}{``\\(f\\) is a subexponential exponent'' means \\(f = o(n)\\); \\(A \\cdot \\rhon\\) is one for every constant \\(A\\)} \\leannames{MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.IsSubexpExponent, MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.isSubexpExponent_runtimeScale} \\\\\n\\leanentry{trace-projection}{A run of the located (cell-monitoring) step projects to a run of the plain step with the same number of steps, so the comparator's time bound is a bound on the machine itself} \\leannames{MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.LocatedCfg.monitor_config, MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.Trace.config_of_locatedStep} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{longtable}\n\\endgroup","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1cad2094-11ee-40b3-a86f-8ba4c86457ea","uuid":"3c0ca26b-f1a0-4d3b-9f06-39fe309b1232","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:09:44.284Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"% Related work and context -- drafted by loop0015 item 08 (2026-11-05).\n% Every quotation below is located by paper3/scripts/related_work.py\n% (python3 -I; --check verifies without writing), which reads the fetched\n% texts under paper3/related/fetched/ (hashes in Table 9.1), the clone at\n% 4eaee875, and the manuscript extract reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt.\n% Nothing is quoted from memory; nothing in the clone is run.\n\\section{Related work and context}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nThis section places the paper beside what others have written about the\nsame family and about the mechanism that checked it. There are five\nthings to place it beside: what the collection says about itself; the two\npapers that stated the hypotheses; a published human audit of an earlier\nbatch of OpenAI's results; the checking tool, called the comparator; and\nthe second proof checker, called nanoda, that we ran in\nSection~\\ref{sec:replay}. The section ends by saying where we looked for\nan earlier statement of the title's observation and what we found.\nTable~\\ref{tab:related-sources} lists every source with where it is on\ndisk, when it was fetched, and its hash or commit. We quote only what is\non disk; nothing in this section is quoted from memory or from the press.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The sources of this section. ``Clone'' is the read-only copy of\nthe OpenAI collection at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}; ``fetched'' files were\ndownloaded on 2026-11-05 and are kept under \\texttt{paper3/related/fetched/}\nwith their SHA-256 hashes. Written by\n\\texttt{paper3/scripts/related\\_work.py}.}\n\\label{tab:related-sources}\n\\input{tables/related_sources}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{What the collection says about itself}\n\\label{sec:related-collection}\n\nThe collection keeps a dated log, \\clonefile{history.md} (56 lines). Under\nthe heading ``October 26, 2026'' (line 11), the entry for this family is\none bullet, which we quote in full (line 18):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``\\emph{The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False.} The paper gives a\ndeterministic algorithm that exactly counts satisfying assignments of\nfixed-width CNF formulas in subexponential time and polynomial space. The\ncase of 3-SAT refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis. An independent\nworking group of computer scientists led by Russell Impagliazzo,\nRamamohan Paturi, and Ryan Williams will examine and explain the proof.\nAt the group’s request, we are making the materials public immediately so\nothers can examine them as well.''\n\\end{quote}\nThree things in this sentence matter for us. First, the group it names is\nled by two of the three authors of the papers that stated the hypotheses\n(Section~\\ref{sec:related-hypotheses}). Second, the verb is ``will'': the\nlog announces an examination to come. Nothing from that group is in the\nclone, and we fetched nothing from it, so this paper has no finding of\ntheirs to cite, agree with or disagree with. Whatever that group\npublishes about the proof will carry more weight on the proof than\nSection~\\ref{sec:proof} does, which only inspected and replayed. Third,\nthe log says ``the paper gives \\ldots{} subexponential time'' with no\nexponent; the exponent is in the scope note.\n\nThat scope note is \\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, seventeen lines, quoted\nin full in Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}. It is the collection's own statement\nof the gap between what Lean proves (the exponent with the squared\n$\\log\\log$ factor) and what the manuscript claims (two sharper\nexponents). Its table (line 17) links the result to the one comparator\nstatement, \\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean}.\nSo the collection itself records that the verified exponent is the weaker\none. What it does not record is what the weaker exponent means at any\nsize: that $\\rhon$, the verified exponent, is larger than $n$ for every\n$n$ between $50$ and $e^{5504}$, so that the verified bound with constant\none sits above $2^n$ at every size anyone will ever run\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). That is the title's observation, and the\nnote does not make it.\n\nThe third document is \\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/README.md},\nten lines. It says how the collection's checks are run: ``Install\n\\texttt{comparator}, \\texttt{landrun}, and \\texttt{lean4export}, and make\nthem available on \\texttt{PATH}.'' (line 3), then from the \\texttt{lean/}\ndirectory \\texttt{lake update}, \\texttt{lake exe cache get}, and\n\\texttt{lake env comparator} on a family's JSON (lines 7--9; the example\nis another family's). The JSON for this family has six fields\n(Section~\\ref{sec:related-comparator} reads them).\n\n\\subsection{The two papers that stated the hypotheses}\n\\label{sec:related-hypotheses}\n\nETH, the Exponential Time Hypothesis, says that 3-SAT (deciding whether a\nformula whose clauses have three literals each can be satisfied) has no\nalgorithm whose running time grows slower than every exponential in the\nnumber of variables. SETH, the Strong form, says that as the clause width\ngrows, the best possible base of the exponential tends to $2$, the base of\ntrying every assignment. Both are from \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}; the\nstandard setting of SETH, and the lemma that lets one move between the\nnumber of variables and the number of clauses, are from\n\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}. The manuscript cites both as\n\\texttt{[IP01]} and \\texttt{[IPZ01]} (extract page 66, lines 3733--3736)\nand states each hypothesis in its own words in its \\S1.1.1, which\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences} quotes with page and line. We do not hold\neither paper: \\repofile{literature/} has no file for them, and\n\\repofile{literature/MISSING.md} now records both with their DOIs. Every\nstatement of a hypothesis in this paper is therefore the manuscript's\nstatement of it, not the original's, and we quote nothing from the two\noriginals. The manuscript's own account of what motivated SETH is one\nsentence we can locate (page 4, lines 170--171): the improvements of the\nknown algorithms ``over exhaustive search diminish as $k$ grows [IP01,\nOpen Problems, item 2]''. Exhaustive search is the ``trying all $2^n$\nassignments'' of the title.\n\n\\subsection{A human audit of an earlier batch of results}\n\\label{sec:related-audit}\n\n\\citet{SienickiSienicki2026} is a published human audit of OpenAI's\nAI-generated proofs. We fetched its arXiv abstract page and API record\n(v1 of 3 August 2026, v2 of 25 August, v3 of 9 September 2026; 13 pages;\nprimary class cs.AI). It is not about this family. Its abstract says\nwhat it covers: ``We assess 18 chapter-specific reviews of the ten\nmathematical results announced by OpenAI on 1 August 2026, alongside\nreview standards, Lean formalizations, subsequent research, and\nmathematical references.'' The family this paper reads is dated 17\nOctober 2026, revised 25 October, and entered the collection on 26\nOctober (Section~\\ref{sec:related-collection}); it is not one of the ten,\nand the abstract mentions neither it nor ETH (the script checks). So we\ncite the audit for its method of review and for nothing about the proof.\n\nFour sentences of the abstract bear on how this paper was written. On\nrestraint: ``The article audits this review record without claiming a\ncomplete reconstruction of all ten proofs.'' On the finding: ``No\nconfirmed substantive mathematical error in a principal result remains in\nthe examined assessments, although review depth varies and some\ndependencies remain partly checked.'' On a hazard of reading proofs\nthrough text extraction: ``In Chapter 6, an apparent polarity error was\nwithdrawn after an overbar lost during PDF extraction was recovered from\nthe typeset source.'' And on what confidence should rest on: ``We argue\nthat confidence should combine formal checking, human reconstruction,\nindependent mathematical use, and a public record supporting correction\nof both proofs and reviews.''\n\nThe third of these is a warning we took. This paper reads the manuscript\nthrough a \\texttt{pdftotext} extract, the same kind of text in which that\noverbar was lost. That is why every quotation from the manuscript carries\nthe page of the PDF beside the line of the extract, why the Lean is read\nfrom its source files and never from a rendering, and why no claim in\nthis paper rests on a formula read from the extract alone: the formulas\nthat matter are the Lean's, re-typed in Section~\\ref{sec:statement} from\nthe \\texttt{.lean} file and re-checked by the kernel in\nSection~\\ref{sec:lean}. Against the fourth sentence, this paper is one of\nthe four things it lists, formal checking, replayed by us and by a\nsecond kernel, together with a reading of what the checked statement\nsays. It is not a human reconstruction of the proof: Section~\\ref{sec:proof}\ninspected the proof's files and did not re-derive the argument. We\nfetched the abstract only, and quote nothing from the paper's body.\n\n\\subsection{The comparator}\n\\label{sec:related-comparator}\n\nThe comparator is the program the collection used to check that the\nsolution file proves the challenge statement\n\\citep{LeanComparator}. We fetched its README from the\n\\texttt{master} branch of \\texttt{leanprover/comparator} on 2026-11-05\n(latest commit \\texttt{865edc14} of 2026-10-14, ``doc: migration\nnotice''; the repository is archived and under the Apache-2.0 licence).\nIts first lines say the tool has moved: ``This project has been merged\ninto core as of Lean release v4.35.0 in the form of \\texttt{lake\ncomparator} and the accompanying \\texttt{lake check}'' (line 3). The\ncollection pins Lean v4.34.1 (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}), one release\nearlier, so the stand-alone tool is the one its README names. The README\nopens: ``Comparator is a trustworthy judge for Lean proofs.'' (line 8).\n\nWhat it checks is stated as three guarantees for the theorems a JSON\nconfiguration names (lines 52--55):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``All theorems in \\texttt{Solution} that are listed in\n\\texttt{theorem\\_names} are guaranteed to:\\\\\n1. Prove the same statement as provided in \\texttt{Challenge}\\\\\n2. Use no more axioms than listed in \\texttt{permitted\\_axioms}\\\\\n3. Be accepted by the Lean kernel''\n\\end{quote}\nIn plain words: the solution proves exactly the sentence\nthe challenge posed, uses only the agreed axioms (the three standard ones\nin the example configuration, line 27), and passes the kernel, the small\ncore of Lean that checks proofs. How it checks them is in its\n``Internals'' (lines 184--200), six steps: build the challenge inside a\nsandbox; export its declarations with \\texttt{lean4export}; do the same\nfor the solution; ``Verify that all declarations used in the statement of\nall relevant theorems in \\texttt{Challenge} are the same as in the\n\\texttt{Solution} environment'' (lines 191--192); ``Verify that the body\nof all relevant theorems in the \\texttt{Solution} environment only uses\naxioms listed in \\texttt{permitted\\_axioms}'' (lines 195--196); and\n``Replay the \\texttt{Solution} environment into the Lean kernel'' (line\n197). The first guarantee is therefore a match of the statement's\ndeclarations as exported, not a textual comparison of the two files.\nThat is the check Section~\\ref{sec:proof} says we did not reproduce: our\nstatement match is textual (Table~\\ref{tab:proof-trust}, row (iv)).\n\nThe guarantees hold under six stated assumptions (lines 34--45). The one\nthat concerns the kernel is the fifth: ``The Lean kernel is correct (with\n\\texttt{external\\_kernels} this can be reduced to `At least one of the\nLean kernel or the \\texttt{external\\_kernels} is correct')'' (lines\n43--44). The sandbox is \\texttt{landrun} (line 10), a tool that confines\nthe build so that a malicious solution file cannot tamper with the\nchecker; the README notes that its trusted base ``naturally includes the\noperating system and hardware it is running on, plus its sandboxing\nmechanism'' (line 58). External kernels are optional: ``Comparator can\nadditionally check solutions with external kernels'' (line 70), and ``For\nbackwards compatibility reasons users may instead set\n\\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: true} to obtain a config that calls\n\\texttt{nanoda\\_bin}'' (lines 86--87). The tool ``was originally\ndeveloped by Lean FRO, with feedback from the AIMO team, in support of\nthe AIMO series of competitions'' (lines 207--208).\n\nFor this family the configuration has six fields\n(\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}): the\nchallenge module; the solution module,\n\\path{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}; one theorem name,\n\\path{SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat}; an empty\n\\texttt{definition\\_names}; the three standard axioms as\n\\texttt{permitted\\_axioms}; and \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false}. There is\nno \\texttt{external\\_kernels} field. So the check the collection\nconfigured for this family is the Lean kernel alone, under the fifth\nassumption in its unweakened form. Section~\\ref{sec:replay} is the\nweakened form, supplied by us: the same theorem's dependencies through a\nsecond kernel. Our \\texttt{bwrap} sandbox there played the role\n\\texttt{landrun} plays here, for the same reason, and we did not run the\ncomparator itself.\n\n\\subsection{nanoda}\n\\label{sec:related-nanoda}\n\nnanoda is the second checker \\citep{Nanoda}. Its README, fetched from\nthe \\texttt{master} branch of \\texttt{ammkrn/nanoda\\_lib} on 2026-11-05,\nsays what it is in one sentence (line 3): ``This is an external type\nchecker for the Lean 4 programming language and theorem prover.'' The\nrepository's description line is ``Library implementing type\ninference/checking functionality based on the Lean theorem prover''; the\nlicence is Apache-2.0. It reads the file \\texttt{lean4export} writes:\n``Export files can be created with lean4export'' (line 13), and ``Given\nthat lean4export already uses json as its export format, adding a json\nparser as a dependency doesn't increase the overall complexity of the\nproject'' (line 23). Its axiom rule is the comparator's: ``The average\nLean user will want to permit the three semi-official axioms, which are\n\\texttt{Quot.sound}, \\texttt{Classical.choice}, and \\texttt{propext}, and\nmost export files will also rely on \\texttt{Lean.trustCompiler}'' (line\n33); the export we checked contains the first three and no fourth\n(Section~\\ref{sec:replay}). Two kernel extensions for arithmetic on\nliterals are off by default and ``enable or disable the Nat and String\nkernel extensions'' (line 39); the replay turned both on.\n\nThe latest commit of the repository at the time of the fetch is\n\\texttt{8de7e0c4} of 2026-10-29, and that is the commit the replay built\nand ran (version 0.4.31; the script checks the replay's log against the\nfetched record). The checker is written in Rust and shares no code with\nthe Lean kernel, which is what makes it a second opinion. Its author's\nbook on external type checkers, linked from line 3 of the README, was not\nfetched.\n\n\\subsection{Where we looked for an earlier statement of the title's observation}\n\\label{sec:related-search}\n\nThe title says the verified guarantee is weaker than trying all $2^n$\nassignments at every practical size. We looked for an earlier statement\nof that observation in the texts this paper rests on, and in no others:\nthe collection's \\clonefile{history.md}, its scope note\n\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, the abstract of\n\\citet{SienickiSienicki2026}, the comparator README, the nanoda README,\nand the manuscript extract. The script searches each for the words such\na statement would be likely to use (``practical'', ``brute'', ``trying\nall'', ``exhaustive'', ``$2^n$'', ``constants are'', ``concrete''). Outside\nthe manuscript, none occurs. In the manuscript, ``practical'' occurs once\n(page 4, line 153), in a sentence about the practical value of SAT\nsolvers on industrial formulas, a different sense; ``exhaustive'' occurs\neleven times, as the motivation of SETH quoted above (line 170) and as the\nproof's own fallback for small inputs (lines 2480 and 2487, where the\nalgorithm uses ``exhaustive search'' below the cutoff\n$n \\ge 20$). The nearest earlier statements are the manuscript's own\nsentence that Lean verifies ``the weaker exponent'' (page 2, lines\n63--67; Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) and the scope note: both say the\nverified exponent is the weaker one, and neither says at what sizes the\nverified bound exceeds $2^n$, which is the title's point. Within these\ntexts we found no prior statement of it. The search is bounded by that\nlist: we did not search the literature at large, the working group's\noutput, or the press.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:related-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the working group's findings.} The\n  collection's log says the group ``will examine and explain the\n  proof''; we hold nothing it has written, and we neither anticipate\n  nor contradict it.\n\\item \\textbf{The human audit does not cover this family.} It is cited\n  for its method and its stated lesson about PDF extraction; its body\n  was not fetched, and no sentence of it beyond the abstract is quoted.\n\\item \\textbf{The two hypothesis papers were not read.} They are cited\n  as the manuscript cites them, and the hypotheses are stated as the\n  manuscript states them (Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}).\n\\item \\textbf{We did not run the comparator.} What we reproduced is\n  stated in Section~\\ref{sec:proof}'s Table~\\ref{tab:proof-trust};\n  the comparator's statement match by exported declarations is not among\n  it.\n\\item \\textbf{No claim that landrun, lean4export or nanoda is correct.}\n  Each is quoted for what its README says it does; the replay's\n  evidence is that two independent checkers agreed on this export, and\n  the trust left in \\texttt{lean4export} is stated in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:replay}.\n\\item \\textbf{``No prior statement'' is bounded by the list above.} Six\n  texts, read with a script; not a literature search.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\endgroup\n% The data, code and proofs statement (item 11 completes it), as in paper 1.\n\\section*{Data, code and proofs}\n\\label{sec:data}\n\\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Data, code and proofs}\n\n\\placeholder{Item 11 completes this statement: the repository, the scripts\nunder \\repofile{paper3/scripts/} that regenerate every number, the replay's\ncommands and log hashes under \\repofile{paper3/replay/}, the Lean names and\nthe axiom audit under \\repofile{paper3/lean/}, and the read-only clone of\n\\url{https://github.com/openai/math} at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}.}\n% Acknowledgements, with the disclosure of AI use in paper 1's form (item 11).\n\\section*{Acknowledgements}\n\\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Acknowledgements}\n\n\\placeholder{Item 11 writes the acknowledgements, with the disclosure of AI\nuse in the form of \\repofile{paper1/latex/sec8_ack.tex}.}\n% Appendix A: formal names, one row per result, as in paper 1\n% (\\leanentry{key}{result} \\leannames{...}); rows written by item 07 (2026-11-05).\n\\section{Formal names}\n\\label{app:lean}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nEvery Lean name the paper cites, with its file under\n\\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/} (the files are left where\nloop0014 wrote them, by the owner's decision recorded in\n\\repofile{paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, S3); the axiom audit is\n\\repofile{paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean} with its committed log\n\\repofile{paper3/lean/axiom_check.log}, and the byte-level re-diff of the\ncopy against the comparator file is \\repofile{paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py}\nwith its log \\repofile{paper3/lean/rediff.log}. Every row's declarations are\non the three standard axioms \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Classical.choice},\n\\texttt{Quot.sound} or fewer; none depends on \\texttt{sorryAx}. Nothing from\nthe other study that shares the directory is cited.\n\n\\begin{longtable}{@{}p{0.07\\linewidth}p{0.43\\linewidth}p{0.44\\linewidth}@{}}\n\\toprule\n & result & Lean names \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\endhead\n\\leangroup{The copy (\\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.lean}, lines 31--221 = comparator lines 1--190 and 220, byte-identical; definitions only, the namespaces of the source kept)}\n\\leanentry{copy-model}{The comparator's vocabulary: formulas and their satisfying-assignment count, the canonical bit encoding and its length, the multitape machine with its step and start, the trace, the running-time scale \\(\\rhon\\) and the bound built from it, the count word, the located step and the visited space} \\leannames{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SAT.count, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Encoding.encodeCNF, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Encoding.formulaBitLength, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Computation.PrimitiveMachine.step, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Computation.Trace, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLog.Statement.runtimeScale, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLog.Statement.runtimeBound, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.countWord, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.locatedStep, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.visitedSpace} \\\\\n\\leangroup{The restatement (\\texttt{Frame.lean}): two \\texttt{def}s of a \\texttt{Prop}, never proved, never axioms}\n\\leanentry{subexpcount}{The comparator statement for width \\(k\\) as a named \\texttt{Prop}, \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\): its body is the comparator theorem's body, identical after whitespace normalisation (1,073 characters, 23 lines)} \\leannames{MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.SubexpCount} \\\\\n\\leanentry{exactcountandsat}{The comparator theorem in full: for every \\(k \\ge 2\\), \\(\\SubexpCount\\ k\\)} \\leannames{MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.ExactCountAndSat} \\\\\n\\leangroup{What is proved about them (\\texttt{Frame.lean})}\n\\leanentry{subexpcount-anti}{A machine for width \\(k\\) is a machine for every width \\(k' \\le k\\), with the same constants; and the unpacking of the ``for every \\(k \\ge 2\\)'' form} \\leannames{MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.SubexpCount.anti, MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.ExactCountAndSat.subexpCount} \\\\\n\\leanentry{runtimebound-eq}{The bound unfolds, by definition, to \\(C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\cdot\\rhon}\\)} \\leannames{MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.runtimeBound_eq} \\\\\n\\leanentry{rho-little-o}{\\(\\rhon = o(n)\\): the verified exponent is subexponential in the limit, from \\((\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0\\)} \\leannames{MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.tendsto_log_sq_div_sqrt, MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.runtimeScale_isLittleO} \\\\\n\\leanentry{is-subexp-exponent}{``\\(f\\) is a subexponential exponent'' means \\(f = o(n)\\); \\(A \\cdot \\rhon\\) is one for every constant \\(A\\)} \\leannames{MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.IsSubexpExponent, MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.isSubexpExponent_runtimeScale} \\\\\n\\leanentry{trace-projection}{A run of the located (cell-monitoring) step projects to a run of the plain step with the same number of steps, so the comparator's time bound is a bound on the machine itself} \\leannames{MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.LocatedCfg.monitor_config, MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.Trace.config_of_locatedStep} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{longtable}\n\\endgroup","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JBP6tfXu8pJrW12QxKLte1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgjMwu1i7iUY4qPK6rqYq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017QF12Xg2zk8cyVRyYDMiKp","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat README.md && echo ---- && sed -n 1,120p ../reports/paper3_plan.md","description":"Read paper3 README and plan §0"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2025,"cache_read_input_tokens":104084,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2025,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1cad2094-11ee-40b3-a86f-8ba4c86457ea","uuid":"35c4c86a-de5e-4751-a6e2-ea5993be9daf","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:09:45.341Z","request_id":"req_011CgjMwtgMuVb1Aw2PeDuTW","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_017QF12Xg2zk8cyVRyYDMiKp":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3 && cat README.md && echo ---- && sed -n 1,120p ../reports/paper3_plan.md","description":"Read paper3 README and plan §0"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_017QF12Xg2zk8cyVRyYDMiKp":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017QF12Xg2zk8cyVRyYDMiKp","type":"tool_result","content":"Exponential time hypothesis is false... but the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size.\n\n*(The title above is fixed by the owner and is used verbatim, including\nthe ellipsis and the final period, everywhere: here, in `latex/main.tex`,\nin `reports/paper3_plan.md`, in `Ralph_Loops/loop0015/TASK.md` and in\nevery commit the loop makes. Questions about its wording go to\n`ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD`, never into the title.)*\n\n**This paper is about the limitation of the ETH refutation and nothing\nelse.** It is not concerned with graphs, pathwidth, treewidth, MOSP or any\nwidth parameter.\n\n## What the paper is\n\nA reading of OpenAI's family 378, *The Exponential Time Hypothesis is\nFalse* (`github.com/openai/math`, 2026-10-25; cloned read-only under\n`~/dev/untrusted/openai-math`, commit `4eaee875`), by people who checked:\n\n- **what the Lean-verified statement says exactly** (`lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean`\n  in the clone): for each `k ≥ 2` there *exist* a finite multitape machine\n  and constants `C, A, S, d, e` such that on every width-`≤ k` CNF, in a\n  canonical encoding of bit length `L`, the machine halts with the exact\n  count within `C (L + 1)^d · 2^{A · ρ(n)}` steps,\n  `ρ(n) = n (ln ln(n + 16))² / √ln(n + 16)`. A **guarantee**, not a\n  running time; the constants are never computed; the exponent is `ρ(n)`\n  only — the manuscript's sharper `n log log n / √log n` and `n / √log n`\n  are not in the Lean library. The factor `ρ(n)/n` exceeds 1 for every\n  `50 ≤ n < e^{5504}` (peak `16/e² ≈ 2.17` near `n ≈ 10^{23.7}`), so with\n  `A = 1` the verified bound is above `2^n` at every practical size: the\n  verified guarantee is weaker than the trivial `2^n · poly` guarantee of\n  trying all assignments. The statement does refute ETH as stated.\n- **an independent inspection and replay of the proof**: the closure of\n  3,091 modules with no `axiom`, `sorry`, `native_decide`,\n  `implemented_by`, `unsafe` or `partial`, 33 statement definitions\n  identical to the comparator's, the compile lemmas concluding in the\n  comparator's own `Trace`; and (item 01) a build of that closure in an\n  isolated `bwrap` sandbox with `#print axioms` recorded, since the\n  repository holds no recorded axiom audit, no CI and no second-kernel\n  (nanoda) replay for this family.\n- **what follows**: ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, #ETH and their randomised forms\n  refuted as the statement supports; lower bounds derived from them lose\n  their premise while the statements they concluded are not themselves\n  refuted (the manuscript's own §1.1.3).\n- **what it changes for a practitioner**: nothing on any machine — a\n  measurement of a SAT solver and a model counter on random 3-CNF against\n  `2^{ρ(n)}` and `2^n`.\n\nThe two audits the paper rests on are `reports/scratch/eth_audit/\nstatement_audit.md` and `proof_audit.md` (2026-11-05). The plan is\n`reports/paper3_plan.md`; the loop that writes the paper is\n`Ralph_Loops/loop0015/`.\n\n## Layout\n\n| path | content |\n|---|---|\n| `latex/` | the paper: `main.tex` (title verbatim, abstract, section inputs), `sec*.tex` (Sections 2–8 rewritten in plain English by item 08x on 2026-11-05; the dense passages they cite live in `secB_appendix_technical.tex`, Appendix B, with every line number and formula unchanged), `refs.bib`, `Makefile` (`make`, `make check`), `figures/`, `tables/` |\n| `scripts/` | every number's regenerate script (`runtime_scale.py`, `practitioner.py`, …), written by the items |\n| `replay/` | the sandboxed replay: `README.md` with the exact commands, `logs/` (full logs git-ignored, hashes committed) |\n| `lean/` | `README.md` pointing at the companion Lean (`lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/{PrimitiveMachine,Frame}.lean`, left in place by the owner's decision S3), `axiom_check.lean` with its committed `axiom_check.log`, and `rediff.log` (the byte-level re-diff of the copy, written by `scripts/lean_rediff.py`) |\n| `related/` | the four texts Section 9 quotes, fetched on 2026-11-05 (`fetched/`, with their API records), and a `README.md` of files, URLs and what was not fetched |\n| `refuter/` | item 10's refuter report |\n| `ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD` | every question the loop could not settle, one entry each, for the owner |\n\n## How to build\n\n```bash\ncd paper3/latex\nmake          # latexmk -pdf -> main.pdf\nmake check    # fails on any undefined reference or citation; prints the page count\nmake clean\n```\n\nThe gate (`python3 Ralph_Loops/loop0015/gate.py`) runs `make check` here,\nchecks the title in `main.tex` verbatim, and fails if anything under\n`solutions/` changed.\n\n## How to regenerate every number\n\n*(Filled in by the items, section by section. Until an item fills its\nsection: the closure counts come from `python3 -I\nreports/scratch/eth_audit/closure.py` and `defdiff.py`, read-only on the\nclone.)*\n\n- **Section 2, the statement** (item 02): `python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py` prints the `ρ(n)/n` table (Table 2.1),\n  the first integer above 1 (`n = 50`: `ρ(49)/49 = 0.9994`,\n  `ρ(50)/50 = 1.0027`), the peak (`16/e² = 2.1654` at `ln(n+16) = e⁴`,\n  `n ≈ 10^23.71`) and the return below 1 (`ln(n+16) = 5503.7`,\n  `n ≈ e^5504 ≈ 10^2390`), and writes `latex/tables/runtime_scale.tex` and\n  `latex/figures/runtime_scale.{pdf,png}` (Figure 2.2). `python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/rho_over_n.py` draws the central figure (Figure 2.1, two\n  panels, with the manuscript's two unverified exponents beneath) and\n  writes `latex/tables/rho_over_n.csv`. The encoding lengths quoted in\n  §2.1 (`L = 1,006,223` at `n, m, k = 125, 500, 4`; `47.9 M` at\n  `1000, 4000, 3`) come from `python3 -I\n  reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py`. Every comparator line\n  number is at clone commit `4eaee875`; every manuscript quote gives the\n  page of `paper.pdf` and the line of `reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt`.\n- **Section 3, what the manuscript claims beyond the statement** (item 03):\n  `python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py` checks every page, line and\n  quoted fragment of the section against the extract (page = form feeds\n  before the line + 1; `paper.pdf` has 67 pages) and every Lean location\n  against the read-only clone at `4eaee875` (`docs/378.md` lines 9 and 11,\n  the five theorems of `SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean` at lines 10, 74, 104, 149,\n  184 with their `#print axioms` at 197–201, the base-2 `rho` of\n  `Asymptotics.lean` 10–11, `runtimeScale_le_four_rho` at 190–191, the\n  sparsification parameters at 14–20, `MainTheorem` of\n  `SqrtLog/StatementCanonical.lean` line 37), searches the family's 3,977\n  `.lean` files for corollary and tensor-network identifiers (none), lists\n  the files outside the family that mention ETH/SETH (two `ParityLifts`\n  files, not in the closure), prints the three exponents of Theorem 1.1\n  divided by `n` at constant 1 (the general one peaks at `2/e = 0.736` at\n  `n = e^{e²} ≈ 1,618`; the fixed-density one is `0.578` at `n = 20` and\n  falls), and writes `latex/tables/beyond_claims.tex` (Table 3.2) and\n  `latex/tables/beyond_exponents.tex` (Table 3.1). Exit status non-zero if\n  anything has moved; `--check` verifies without writing.\n- **Section 4, the proof inspected** (item 04): `python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py` re-runs the two audit scripts\n  `reports/scratch/eth_audit/closure.py` (the import closure of\n  `OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main`: 3,091 modules, 550,551 raw\n  lines, 50 Mathlib modules + `Lean.Elab.Tactic.Omega`, the keyword\n  counts with comments stripped) and `defdiff.py` (32 declarations\n  `SAME`), both under `python3 -I` as subprocesses and read-only on the\n  clone, and then checks what they do not: the four declarations the\n  diff cannot match by itself (`countWord` identical, `runtimeScale` and\n  `runtimeBound` identical up to `noncomputable def` / `noncomputable\n  section`, the theorem statement identical, 1,124 characters normalised\n  and 1,353 raw, challenge line 194 = `Final.lean` line 74), the 3,977\n  family files and 886 outside the closure (no `sorry`/`axiom`/\n  `native_decide`/`admit`, raw or stripped), the nine `opaque`s with\n  bodies, the two `quotPrecheck false`, the `#print axioms` placement\n  (8 closure files / 19 commands; 17 / 35 over the family directory;\n  the replay's 19 in-build axiom lines all three axioms), the absence of\n  any recorded `depends on axioms` output in the clone, the challenge\n  JSONs (418; `enable_nanoda` false 413, true 2, absent 3), the single\n  CI workflow, the toolchain, the Mathlib pin, the 42 manifest packages\n  and 23 patches (none for Mathlib), the lakefile hooks, and about 60\n  line citations of the proof's spine (`Final.lean`, `GuardedCompiler`,\n  `CompileSpace`, `NativeOuter`, `NativeLargeEntry`, `NativeEntry`,\n  `NativeOuterBudgets`, `NativeLeafContract`, `BitProgram`, the\n  challenge file, `docs/378.md`, the preprint's date line, `history.md`).\n  Writes `latex/tables/proof_inspection.tex` (Table 4.1) and\n  `latex/tables/proof_inspection.json`; exit status non-zero if anything\n  has moved; `--check` verifies without writing. Table 4.2 (the trust\n  boundary) is prose from Sections 4 and 5, not generated.\n- **Section 6, what follows in general terms** (item 05): `python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/consequences.py` checks every page, line and quoted\n  fragment of the section against the extract (the hypotheses as the\n  manuscript states them: ETH p. 4 lines 165–166, SETH 166–169, Gap-ETH\n  172–174 and its randomised form 175–176, NSETH 181–183, the randomised\n  ETH and SETH by citation at 190–191, #ETH 204–205; the refutation\n  paragraph 187–191 and its two context sentences 191–194; the #ETH\n  sentence p. 5 211–212; §1.1.3's \"removes one basis\" 226–227, OVC\n  229–232, the unrestricted-width hypothesis 233–236, \"a reduction in the\n  other direction\" 237–238, Set Splitting 239–243; the transfer caveat\n  p. 2 95–99), the manuscript's bibliography entries that `refs.bib`\n  copies for the hypotheses' sources (extract lines 3686–3811), the\n  comparator lines Section 6 relies on (38, 47, 132, 147, 194, 203–211 at\n  `4eaee875`, read with `open()` only), prints the clause-count bound\n  `Σ_{j≤k} C(2n, j)` (2,604,376 at `n = 125`, `k = 3`) and `ρ(n)/n` at a\n  few sizes, and writes `latex/tables/hypotheses.tex` (Table 6.1). Exit\n  status non-zero if anything has moved; `--check` verifies without\n  writing. No other number in Section 6 (the `A ≥ 257`, the `50 ≤ n <\n  e^5504` range) is Section 6's own; each is Section 2's.\n- **Section 7, for the practitioner** (item 06): `nice -n 19 python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/practitioner.py --regenerate` runs the measurement\n  (about twenty minutes of wall clock on the machine of record, 1,172 s on\n  2026-11-05): random 3-CNF at `m = round(4.26 n)`, `n = 20, 30, 40, 50,\n  60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 400, 500`, twenty seeds per size,\n  generated by the script from `(n, seed)`; CaDiCaL 1.9.5 through\n  python-sat 1.9.dev15 (`cadical195`) for the decision bit, Ganak 2.8.0\n  (pyganak, Arjun preprocessing included) for the exact count, ApproxMC\n  4.4.0 (pyapproxmc, `ε = 0.8`, `δ = 0.2`, projection = all variables)\n  for the approximate count, and blocking-clause enumeration over CaDiCaL\n  at `n ≤ 30` as the independent check; each call in its own interpreter\n  under `nice -n 19`, eight at a time, 60 s per call, censored above it; a\n  tool that finishes fewer than ten of twenty at a size stops escalating.\n  Writes `latex/tables/practitioner_raw.csv` (every call) and\n  `latex/tables/practitioner_meta.json` (versions, CPU, load, date).\n  `python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py` (seconds) rebuilds\n  `latex/tables/practitioner.tex` (Table 7.1) and\n  `latex/figures/practitioner.{pdf,png}` (Figure 7.1) from the CSV,\n  prints every number Section 7 quotes (`t20 = 1.28e−4` s and the common\n  factor `t20 / 2^20`; the 1 s crossings of the two scaled guarantees at\n  `n = 33` and `35`; `2^(ρ(n) − n)` at 50, 100, 500; the decades between\n  CaDiCaL's median and each guarantee at 60, 100, 200, 500), runs the\n  checks (Ganak = enumeration on 40 instances at `n ≤ 30`; CaDiCaL's bit\n  = `[count > 0]` everywhere both finished; ApproxMC within `1.8×` of\n  Ganak on 178 of 206; the clone read with `open()` only: 3,977 family\n  files, no `#eval`, no `def main`, no `IO` action, the comparator\n  theorem existential in the machine at line 195) and exits non-zero if\n  any fails; `--check` verifies without writing. No MOSP or pathwidth\n  instance; nothing under `solutions/`. The two counters were installed\n  from PyPI for this section (`pip install pyganak`; `pyapproxmc` was\n  already present); no counter binary is on the host.\n\n- **Section 8, the statement in our Lean** (item 07): `python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py` (from the repository root; `--check`\n  verifies without writing) re-diffs the copy — our\n  `lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.lean` lines 31–221\n  against the clone's comparator file lines 1–190 followed by 220, byte for\n  byte (identical, 191 lines each side), with the clone's SHA-256\n  (`4183b205…`) and commit (`4eaee875`) — and the restatement — the body of\n  `SubexpCount` (Frame.lean 85–107) against the comparator theorem's body\n  (clone 195–217): identical after whitespace normalisation (1,073\n  characters) and line by line after stripping indentation (23 lines) — and\n  counts `sorry` and `axiom` declarations in our two files (zero and zero;\n  the clone's one `sorry` at line 218 is outside the copied range); log\n  `paper3/lean/rediff.log`. The axiom audit is `cd lean && lake env lean\n  ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean > ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.log` (21\n  declarations: 19 on exactly `[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]`,\n  `Trace` on none, `countWord` on two of the three, `sorryAx` on none; Lean\n  4.34.0-rc2, Mathlib `1192d624`, our library's toolchain). `paper3/lean/README.md`\n  lists the files, the line of every cited name and both logs.\n\n- **Section 9, related work and context** (item 08): `python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/related_work.py` (from the repository root; `--check`\n  verifies without writing) locates every quotation of the section — in\n  the four texts item 08 fetched on 2026-11-05 into `paper3/related/fetched/`\n  (the arXiv API record and abstract page of 2608.14673; the `master`\n  READMEs of `leanprover/comparator`, commit `865edc14`, and\n  `ammkrn/nanoda_lib`, commit `8de7e0c4`, with both repositories' API\n  records), in the clone at `4eaee875` (`history.md` line 18, `lean/docs/378.md`,\n  `lean/ComparatorChallenges/README.md` and `SubexponentialSAT.json`) and in\n  the manuscript extract (lines 170–171, 3733–3736) — checks that\n  `literature/` holds none of the cited papers, that the replay's nanoda\n  commit equals the repository's latest, runs the prior-statement word\n  search over the six texts (none outside the manuscript; in it,\n  \"practical\" once at line 153 and \"exhaustive\" eleven times), prints the\n  SHA-256 of every fetched file and writes `latex/tables/related_sources.tex`\n  (Table 9.1). `paper3/related/README.md` lists the files and URLs.\n\n## The replay\n\nDone (item 01, 2026-11-05): `replay/README.md` has the exact scripts,\nthe timeline, every count and hash. In one paragraph: a fresh copy of\nthe clone (commit `4eaee875`) was built inside a `bwrap` sandbox\n(read-only system directories, a private home, the repository\ninvisible, PID/IPC/UTS namespaces, eight cores at `nice -n 19`, network\non for the lakefile's dependency clones and the Mathlib cache) at the\npinned `leanprover/lean4:v4.34.1` with upstream Mathlib\n`d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612`: `lake build\nOAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main` compiled **3,091 modules** (the\naudit's closure exactly) in **33 min** with **0 `error` lines and 0\n`sorry`** lines, and `#print axioms` of\n`SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat` printed\n`[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]`. A first run (01:03) failed\nat once on a `lake build -j 8` option this Lake does not have; the\nsecond (01:16) is the one reported. Then a **second kernel**:\n`lean4export` (tag `v4.34.0`, built with `v4.34.1`) exported the theorem\nand its dependencies (2.36 GB, 43.6 M lines) and `nanoda_lib`\n(commit `8de7e0c4`, v0.4.31) checked them: `Checked 95176 declarations\nwith no errors`, three axioms, exit 0, in 110 s. Regenerate the counts\nwith `bash paper3/replay/counts.sh`; the full build log and the export\nstay in `~/dev/untrusted/replay/logs/` (hashes committed under\n`replay/logs/run2/` and `replay/logs/nanoda/`).\n\n## Open questions for the owner\n\nSee `ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD`; item 11 lists the entries here by number.\n\n## The CLAUDE.md paragraph\n\n*(Item 11 drafts it here. Not applied to `CLAUDE.md`; the owner folds it.)*\n----\n# Paper 3 — *Exponential time hypothesis is false... but the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size.* — plan for Ralph loop0015\n\n*Written 2026-11-05, after the two audits of OpenAI's family 378\n(`reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement_audit.md`, `proof_audit.md`) and\nafter loop0014's items 01–02 fixed what the verified statement says and\nrestated it in Lean. The title is fixed by the owner and is used verbatim,\nincluding the ellipsis and the final period, everywhere. The loop\ndirectory is `Ralph_Loops/loop0015/` (`TASK.md`, `iterations.md`,\n`PROGRESS.md`, `loop.py`, `gate.py`, `knobs.json`, `allowed_sorries.txt`,\n`budget_watch.py`), cloned from loop0014's. Not launched.*\n\n**This paper is about the limitation of the ETH refutation and nothing\nelse.** (Owner, 2026-11-05.) It is not concerned with graphs, pathwidth,\ntreewidth, MOSP or any width parameter.\n\n## 0. The question and the stance\n\n### 0.1 The question\n\nOn 2026-10-25 OpenAI's manuscript collection (`github.com/openai/math`,\nApache-2.0; cloned read-only under `~/dev/untrusted/openai-math` at commit\n`4eaee875`) added family 378, *The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False*:\na deterministic algorithm that counts the satisfying assignments of any\n`k`-CNF on `n` variables in time subexponential in `n`, with a Lean proof\nchecked by the collection's comparator mechanism against a 220-line\nstatement. The paper asks one question and answers it by reading, not by\nopinion:\n\n> **Q.** What exactly is verified, what is only in the manuscript, what\n> follows from the verified part, and what does it change for anyone who\n> solves or counts CNF formulas on a machine?\n\n### 0.2 The stance, and the title's claim made precise\n\nThe two audits of 2026-11-05 found **no flaw**: the comparator statement\nmeans what the manuscript's verified form says, and the proof's import\nclosure shows no unsoundness by inspection. The paper is therefore **a\ncareful reading by people who checked, not a debunking**, and every\nsentence is to be written so that a reader who admires the result finds\nnothing unfair in it. What the title asserts, made precise:\n\n1. **What is verified is a bound, a guarantee**: for each `k ≥ 2` there\n   *exist* a finite multitape machine and constants `C, A, S > 0`,\n   `d, e ≥ 1` such that on every width-`≤ k` CNF the machine halts with\n   the exact count within `C (L + 1)^d · 2^{A · ρ(n)}` steps, where `L`\n   is the encoding's bit length and\n   `ρ(n) = n (ln ln(n + 16))² / √ln(n + 16)`. The constants are\n   existential: the proof constructs the machine from an explicit program\n   but **no value of `C, A, S, d, e` or of the state count is computed**\n   anywhere in the library.\n2. **The exponent is `ρ(n)`, and only `ρ(n)`.** The manuscript's Theorem\n   1.1 states the sharper `n log log n / √log n` and, at fixed clause\n   density, `n / √log n`; the Lean library carries neither\n   (`lean/docs/378.md` says so). Those exponents are **unverified**.\n3. **The factor `ρ(n)/n` exceeds 1 for every `50 ≤ n < e^{5504}`**: it\n   is 0.9994 at `n = 49`, 1.0027 at 50, 1.11 at 100, 1.42 at 10³, 1.85 at\n   10⁶, peaks at `16/e² ≈ 2.17` near `n ≈ 10^{23.7}`, and falls below 1\n   again only past `n ≈ 10^{2390}`. So even with `A = 1` the verified\n   bound is above `2^n` at every practical size, and `A` is unknown.\n   **The verified guarantee is weaker than the trivial guarantee\n   `2^n · poly` of trying all assignments at every practical size.**\n4. **The machine's actual running time is unknown.** A bound above `2^n`\n   does not say the machine is slow; it says nothing about its speed\n   below `e^{5504}` variables. No implementation exists to time.\n5. **The verified statement does refute ETH** as stated — a deterministic\n   `2^{o(n)} · poly(n + m)` #k-SAT counter in the standard sense — and\n   with it SETH, Gap-ETH, #ETH and their randomised forms, as the\n   manuscript's §1.1.1 lists. Lower bounds *derived* from those\n   hypotheses lose their premise; the statements they concluded are not\n   themselves refuted, as the manuscript's own §1.1.3 explains.\n\n### 0.3 What is on record (nothing to redo)\n\n- `reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement_audit.md` (2026-11-05): the\n  comparator statement read field by field — `PrimitiveMachine` is\n  finite, `Trace` counts exactly the steps, the encoding is `O(k·n·m)`\n  bits and unary in `n`, the quantifier order puts the machine and the\n  five constants after `k` and before `n` and `F`, `runtimeScale` is\n  `ρ(n)` in natural logarithms, the output is the count in binary on a\n  named tape and the decision in the halting state; verdict \"not\n  refuted\", with the `ρ(n)/n` numbers and the companion-claims list\n  (what the statement supports, what is paper-only). Scripts beside it:\n  `check_encoding.py`, `VarCheck.lean`.\n- `reports/scratch/eth_audit/proof_audit.md` (2026-11-05): the closure\n  of the solution (3,091 modules, 550,551 lines; zero `axiom`, `sorry`,\n  `admit`, `native_decide`, `ofReduceBool`, `implemented_by`, `extern`,\n  `unsafe`, `partial`; nine bodied `opaque`s; two `quotPrecheck false`),\n  the 33 statement definitions textually identical, the 1,124-character\n  statement identical, the compile lemmas concluding in the comparator's\n  own `Trace`, Mathlib `d13f23b7` unpatched, toolchain `v4.34.1`; and the\n  evidential gap: no recorded `#print axioms`, no CI, `enable_nanoda:\n  false`, the comparator run not reproduced. Scripts: `closure.py`,\n  `defdiff.py`; data `closure_modules.json`, `hits.json`; the committed\n  `pdftotext` extract `paper.txt`.\n- `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md` §1.0 and §1.3.5–1.3.7 (the verified\n  exponent named; the manuscript's caveats quoted with lines) and\n  §2.1–2.2 (the machine model copied verbatim with attribution;\n  `SubexpCount k` character for character; `runtimeScale_isLittleO`).\n  Nothing else of that report is in scope.\n- `lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.lean` (the\n  verbatim copy of the comparator's definitions, Apache-2.0 header) and\n  `Frame.lean` lines 1–165 (`SubexpCount`, `ExactCountAndSat`,\n  `SubexpCount.anti`, `runtimeBound_eq`, `runtimeScale_isLittleO`): no\n  `sorry`, no `axiom`, axiom-audited in loop0014 item 02. Item 07 moves\n  the ETH-only part to a neutral path (§0.4.7).\n- The collection itself: the manuscript (64 pages), the comparator file\n  and JSON, the solution tree, `lean/docs/378.md`, `history.md` (the\n  working-group sentence), `lean/lakefile.lean` with its configuration-time\n  `run_cmd` (line ~290) and `post_update` hooks.\n- `paper1/latex/`: the house LaTeX setup (`make check`, `plainnat`,\n  Appendix A of Lean names, figures as PDFs beside their scripts).\n\n### 0.4 Design decisions (fixed)\n\n1. **The title is verbatim everywhere** — `\\title` and `pdftitle` in\n   `paper3/latex/main.tex` (`$2^n$` and `\\ldots` its only LaTeX), the\n   first line of `paper3/README.md`, this plan, `TASK.md`, every commit\n   body (the driver writes it). The gate checks `main.tex`. No session\n   rewords it; questions about it go to `paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD`.\n2. **The `ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD` protocol.** No item stops on a question.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1cad2094-11ee-40b3-a86f-8ba4c86457ea","uuid":"90a64cb5-fe0f-44a2-b160-02c703d44803","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:09:45.356Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Exponential time hypothesis is false... but the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size.\n\n*(The title above is fixed by the owner and is used verbatim, including\nthe ellipsis and the final period, everywhere: here, in `latex/main.tex`,\nin `reports/paper3_plan.md`, in `Ralph_Loops/loop0015/TASK.md` and in\nevery commit the loop makes. Questions about its wording go to\n`ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD`, never into the title.)*\n\n**This paper is about the limitation of the ETH refutation and nothing\nelse.** It is not concerned with graphs, pathwidth, treewidth, MOSP or any\nwidth parameter.\n\n## What the paper is\n\nA reading of OpenAI's family 378, *The Exponential Time Hypothesis is\nFalse* (`github.com/openai/math`, 2026-10-25; cloned read-only under\n`~/dev/untrusted/openai-math`, commit `4eaee875`), by people who checked:\n\n- **what the Lean-verified statement says exactly** (`lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean`\n  in the clone): for each `k ≥ 2` there *exist* a finite multitape machine\n  and constants `C, A, S, d, e` such that on every width-`≤ k` CNF, in a\n  canonical encoding of bit length `L`, the machine halts with the exact\n  count within `C (L + 1)^d · 2^{A · ρ(n)}` steps,\n  `ρ(n) = n (ln ln(n + 16))² / √ln(n + 16)`. A **guarantee**, not a\n  running time; the constants are never computed; the exponent is `ρ(n)`\n  only — the manuscript's sharper `n log log n / √log n` and `n / √log n`\n  are not in the Lean library. The factor `ρ(n)/n` exceeds 1 for every\n  `50 ≤ n < e^{5504}` (peak `16/e² ≈ 2.17` near `n ≈ 10^{23.7}`), so with\n  `A = 1` the verified bound is above `2^n` at every practical size: the\n  verified guarantee is weaker than the trivial `2^n · poly` guarantee of\n  trying all assignments. The statement does refute ETH as stated.\n- **an independent inspection and replay of the proof**: the closure of\n  3,091 modules with no `axiom`, `sorry`, `native_decide`,\n  `implemented_by`, `unsafe` or `partial`, 33 statement definitions\n  identical to the comparator's, the compile lemmas concluding in the\n  comparator's own `Trace`; and (item 01) a build of that closure in an\n  isolated `bwrap` sandbox with `#print axioms` recorded, since the\n  repository holds no recorded axiom audit, no CI and no second-kernel\n  (nanoda) replay for this family.\n- **what follows**: ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, #ETH and their randomised forms\n  refuted as the statement supports; lower bounds derived from them lose\n  their premise while the statements they concluded are not themselves\n  refuted (the manuscript's own §1.1.3).\n- **what it changes for a practitioner**: nothing on any machine — a\n  measurement of a SAT solver and a model counter on random 3-CNF against\n  `2^{ρ(n)}` and `2^n`.\n\nThe two audits the paper rests on are `reports/scratch/eth_audit/\nstatement_audit.md` and `proof_audit.md` (2026-11-05). The plan is\n`reports/paper3_plan.md`; the loop that writes the paper is\n`Ralph_Loops/loop0015/`.\n\n## Layout\n\n| path | content |\n|---|---|\n| `latex/` | the paper: `main.tex` (title verbatim, abstract, section inputs), `sec*.tex` (Sections 2–8 rewritten in plain English by item 08x on 2026-11-05; the dense passages they cite live in `secB_appendix_technical.tex`, Appendix B, with every line number and formula unchanged), `refs.bib`, `Makefile` (`make`, `make check`), `figures/`, `tables/` |\n| `scripts/` | every number's regenerate script (`runtime_scale.py`, `practitioner.py`, …), written by the items |\n| `replay/` | the sandboxed replay: `README.md` with the exact commands, `logs/` (full logs git-ignored, hashes committed) |\n| `lean/` | `README.md` pointing at the companion Lean (`lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/{PrimitiveMachine,Frame}.lean`, left in place by the owner's decision S3), `axiom_check.lean` with its committed `axiom_check.log`, and `rediff.log` (the byte-level re-diff of the copy, written by `scripts/lean_rediff.py`) |\n| `related/` | the four texts Section 9 quotes, fetched on 2026-11-05 (`fetched/`, with their API records), and a `README.md` of files, URLs and what was not fetched |\n| `refuter/` | item 10's refuter report |\n| `ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD` | every question the loop could not settle, one entry each, for the owner |\n\n## How to build\n\n```bash\ncd paper3/latex\nmake          # latexmk -pdf -> main.pdf\nmake check    # fails on any undefined reference or citation; prints the page count\nmake clean\n```\n\nThe gate (`python3 Ralph_Loops/loop0015/gate.py`) runs `make check` here,\nchecks the title in `main.tex` verbatim, and fails if anything under\n`solutions/` changed.\n\n## How to regenerate every number\n\n*(Filled in by the items, section by section. Until an item fills its\nsection: the closure counts come from `python3 -I\nreports/scratch/eth_audit/closure.py` and `defdiff.py`, read-only on the\nclone.)*\n\n- **Section 2, the statement** (item 02): `python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py` prints the `ρ(n)/n` table (Table 2.1),\n  the first integer above 1 (`n = 50`: `ρ(49)/49 = 0.9994`,\n  `ρ(50)/50 = 1.0027`), the peak (`16/e² = 2.1654` at `ln(n+16) = e⁴`,\n  `n ≈ 10^23.71`) and the return below 1 (`ln(n+16) = 5503.7`,\n  `n ≈ e^5504 ≈ 10^2390`), and writes `latex/tables/runtime_scale.tex` and\n  `latex/figures/runtime_scale.{pdf,png}` (Figure 2.2). `python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/rho_over_n.py` draws the central figure (Figure 2.1, two\n  panels, with the manuscript's two unverified exponents beneath) and\n  writes `latex/tables/rho_over_n.csv`. The encoding lengths quoted in\n  §2.1 (`L = 1,006,223` at `n, m, k = 125, 500, 4`; `47.9 M` at\n  `1000, 4000, 3`) come from `python3 -I\n  reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py`. Every comparator line\n  number is at clone commit `4eaee875`; every manuscript quote gives the\n  page of `paper.pdf` and the line of `reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt`.\n- **Section 3, what the manuscript claims beyond the statement** (item 03):\n  `python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py` checks every page, line and\n  quoted fragment of the section against the extract (page = form feeds\n  before the line + 1; `paper.pdf` has 67 pages) and every Lean location\n  against the read-only clone at `4eaee875` (`docs/378.md` lines 9 and 11,\n  the five theorems of `SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean` at lines 10, 74, 104, 149,\n  184 with their `#print axioms` at 197–201, the base-2 `rho` of\n  `Asymptotics.lean` 10–11, `runtimeScale_le_four_rho` at 190–191, the\n  sparsification parameters at 14–20, `MainTheorem` of\n  `SqrtLog/StatementCanonical.lean` line 37), searches the family's 3,977\n  `.lean` files for corollary and tensor-network identifiers (none), lists\n  the files outside the family that mention ETH/SETH (two `ParityLifts`\n  files, not in the closure), prints the three exponents of Theorem 1.1\n  divided by `n` at constant 1 (the general one peaks at `2/e = 0.736` at\n  `n = e^{e²} ≈ 1,618`; the fixed-density one is `0.578` at `n = 20` and\n  falls), and writes `latex/tables/beyond_claims.tex` (Table 3.2) and\n  `latex/tables/beyond_exponents.tex` (Table 3.1). Exit status non-zero if\n  anything has moved; `--check` verifies without writing.\n- **Section 4, the proof inspected** (item 04): `python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py` re-runs the two audit scripts\n  `reports/scratch/eth_audit/closure.py` (the import closure of\n  `OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main`: 3,091 modules, 550,551 raw\n  lines, 50 Mathlib modules +