ec:practitioner-results}\npaper3/latex/sec7_practitioner.tex: \\label{tab:practitioner}\npaper3/latex/sec7_practitioner.tex: \\label{fig:practitioner}\npaper3/latex/sec7_practitioner.tex: \\label{sec:practitioner-guarantees}\npaper3/latex/sec7_practitioner.tex: \\label{sec:practitioner-not}\npaper3/latex/sec6_consequences.tex: \\label{sec:consequences}\npaper3/latex/sec6_consequences.tex: \\label{sec:consequences-property}\npaper3/latex/sec6_consequences.tex: \\label{sec:consequences-hypotheses}\npaper3/latex/sec6_consequences.tex: \\label{tab:hypotheses}\npaper3/latex/sec6_consequences.tex: \\label{sec:consequences-lower-bounds}\npaper3/latex/sec6_consequences.tex: \\label{sec:consequences-not}\npaper3/latex/sec8_lean.tex: \\label{sec:lean}\npaper3/latex/sec8_lean.tex: \\label{sec:lean-copy}\npaper3/latex/sec8_lean.tex: \\label{sec:lean-restatement}\npaper3/latex/sec8_lean.tex: \\label{sec:lean-lemmas}\npaper3/latex/sec8_lean.tex: \\label{sec:lean-audit}\npaper3/latex/sec8_lean.tex: \\label{sec:lean-notclaim}\npaper3/latex/sec5_replay.tex: \\label{sec:replay}\npaper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex: \\label{sec:proof}\npaper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex: \\label{sec:proof-method}\npaper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex: \\label{sec:proof-closure}\npaper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex: \\label{sec:proof-keywords}\npaper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex: \\label{tab:proof-inspection}\npaper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex: \\label{sec:proof-match}\npaper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex: \\label{sec:proof-spine}\npaper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex: \\label{sec:proof-build}\npaper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex: \\label{sec:proof-missing}\npaper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex: \\label{sec:proof-trust}\npaper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex: \\label{tab:proof-trust}\n----\n\\begin{abstract}\n\\noindent\nIn 2001 we listed twelve problems from operations research, VLSI\ndesign and graph theory, the minimization of open stacks problem (MOSP) among\nthem, as equal ``plus or minus one'', and gave no proofs\n\\citep{Linhares2001,LY2002}. We now correct and extend the table, proving formally in Lean that most of\nthem are, mathematically, one quantity, \\emph{graph pathwidth}, studied\nindependently by communities that rarely cite one another. Eight rows are exact,\ncounting pathwidth itself: each equals pathwidth or pathwidth plus one. Two lie\nin \\emph{bands}: they stay within one or two of pathwidth, but not at a fixed\noffset. Two are false as originally stated. We add three members the table missed:\n(i) the maximum wavefront of sparse matrix elimination, which brings a fourth\ndiscipline, numerical linear algebra, into the family; (ii) progressive\npebbling; and (iii) the vertex search of Bienstock, Robertson, Seymour and\nThomas. We call this cluster the\n\\emph{pathwidth complex}, as the literature uses the name \\emph{pathwidth} more\nthan three times as often as the other eleven names combined. Since 2005, the\nnext most active name is MOSP, and the open-stacks community produced, by way\nof that year's Constraint Modelling Challenge, the best exact algorithm for\nMOSP: the \\emph{customer search} of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, which branches on the order in which customers' stacks close (also a\npathwidth algorithm). We prove in Lean that two of its published dominance theorems,\nrules that discard a branch as no better than another, are \\emph{not true},\nand that on a 34-customer counter-example instance their algorithm, with its better move off, reports an\noptimum of 7 where the optimum is 6. Our corrected search (and a SAT solver with a checked DRAT proof) confirms the 6. Our corrected definite move,\nthe first of the two, was also found by Tamaki as a lemma\n\\citep[Lemma~1]{Kitsunai2016} in the pathwidth literature; we prove our\ncorrected search sound, formally, in Lean. We re-prove all 115 benchmark\noptima that rested on Chu and Stuckey's algorithm, changing none. Ported to graphs, our corrected search proves the pathwidth of all 11,534\nRome graphs, a standard graph-drawing benchmark of 10 to 110 vertices (97.1\\% of them within 10 minutes each), and, on every benchmark set with published\nexact counts, as many graphs or more than the published branch and bound of\nCoudert, Mazauric and Nisse (2016). In the other direction, \\emph{from pathwidth to MOSP}, a lower bound from graph\nminors, proved in Lean, settles in seconds instances whose refutation took\ncore-days, and closes the Mycielski graph $M_7$. All of our proofs and solvers are open source.\nWe also release a dataset of 17,714 graphs, distinct up to isomorphism, from\ntwenty benchmark collections for pathwidth and the problems equal to it, the\nMOSP instances and the Rome graphs among them, 16,634 of them with a certified\npathwidth, with independent checkers. We believe ours is currently the\nstrongest exact pathwidth solver openly available.\n\\end{abstract}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1cad2094-11ee-40b3-a86f-8ba4c86457ea","uuid":"2e541fdc-a59d-4741-84ea-8a6731639f6c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:09:58.245Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"35:  -j 8` (the 3,091-module closure only, never the default target that\n108:  (log-`n` axis from 10 to 10^{2500}, the line `ρ(n)/n`, the level 1,\n132:  `latex/figures/rho_over_n_practical.pdf` (`n = 10 … 10⁶` with the values\n162:  (3,091 modules, 550,551 lines, 50 Mathlib modules + `Lean.Elab.Tactic.Omega`,\n265:  Audit of OpenAI's AI-Generated Mathematical Proofs*, v3 2026-09-09 —\n303:- [ ] **09 The abstract, introduction and conclusion.**\n317:- [ ] **10 Full build and refuter pass.**\n333:- [ ] **11 The write-up around the paper.**\n344:- [ ] **12 Reserve.** The nanoda replay if item 01 could not do it (the\n----\npaper3/latex/sec10_conclusion.tex: \\label{sec:conclusion}\npaper3/latex/sec11_data.tex: \\label{sec:data}\npaper3/latex/sec1_intro.tex: \\label{sec:intro}\npaper3/latex/secA_appendix_lean.tex: \\label{app:lean}\npaper3/latex/secB_appendix_technical.tex: \\label{app:tech}\npaper3/latex/secB_appendix_technical.tex: \\label{app:tech-definiti