{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PstKSgxL5vGhWv3J7HavU6","type":"tool_result","content":"% The proof, inspected -- drafted by loop0015 item 04 (2026-11-05), rewritten\n% in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05). Every number of this section is\n% printed, and every line citation re-checked against the read-only clone at\n% commit 4eaee875, by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py\n% which re-runs the two audit scripts of reports/scratch/eth_audit/\n% (closure.py, defdiff.py) as subprocesses, performs the remaining checks\n% itself, writes latex/tables/proof_inspection.tex, and exits non-zero if\n% anything has moved. Unqualified Lean paths below are relative to\n% lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/ in the clone; the challenge file is\n% lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean. Nothing in the clone was\n% built, imported or run for this section; the build is Section 5's. The\n% line-by-line spine and the keyword details are in Appendix B.\n\\section{The proof, inspected}\n\\label{sec:proof}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement} read the theorem. This section reads the\nproof, as far as reading can go. The question is not whether the\nmathematics is right. The kernel decides that, and\nSection~\\ref{sec:replay} reports what the kernel said when we ran it.\nThe question is whether anything in the source could make the kernel's\nacceptance mean less than it appears to mean. There are known ways for\nthat to happen: an added axiom; a \\texttt{sorry}, which is Lean's word\nfor a proof left out; a step proved by trusting a compiled program\nrather than the kernel; a patched copy of the mathematics library; a\nstatement in the solution that differs from the statement in the\nchallenge; or a running-time bound proved against a cost model of the\nproof's own choosing. We found none of these. What we did find is that\nthe repository offers no recorded evidence that the proof type-checks at all, that is, that Lean accepts it: no saved output of the axiom check, no automated build, and no\nsecond checker for this family. That is why Section~\\ref{sec:replay}\nexists. The last subsection joins the two.\n\n\\subsection{Method}\n\\label{sec:proof-method}\n\nThe clone of \\texttt{openai/math} at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} is treated\nas untrusted data. Every file was read with \\texttt{cat}, \\texttt{sed},\n\\texttt{grep} and \\texttt{python3 -I}, and nothing in it was built,\nimported or executed for this section. Two scripts from the audit of\n2026-11-05 do the mechanical part. \\texttt{closure.py} follows the\n\\texttt{import} lines from the solution's main module, strips comments,\nand counts how often each keyword of interest occurs in what remains.\n\\texttt{defdiff.py} compares each definition in the challenge file with\nthe library's definition of the same name, ignoring differences in\nspacing. Both were re-run for this section, and every number below is\nfrom that fresh run; \\repofile{paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py}\nre-runs them, performs the remaining checks, and regenerates\nTable~\\ref{tab:proof-inspection}. The inspection reads the spine of the\nproof, that is, the final file, the compiler, the entry lemmas and the\nconstants, and sweeps the whole closure by keyword. The arithmetic\nbeneath the spine was not read (Section~\\ref{sec:proof-not}).\n\n\\subsection{The closure}\n\\label{sec:proof-closure}\n\nThe \\emph{closure} of a module is the set of all files it depends on,\ndirectly or through other files. The comparator's JSON names one\nsolution module, \\path{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, a\nsixteen-line file that imports \\path{SqrtLog.ExecutionNativeFinal} and\n\\path{SqrtLogSpace.Final} and declares nothing (\\clonefile{Main.lean}). Its closure inside the repository is\n\\textbf{3,091 modules}, every one in the family's own directory,\ntotalling \\textbf{550,551} lines; the largest file is 2,873 lines\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparsePriorityProgram.lean}).\nOutside the family, the closure reaches \\textbf{50} modules of Mathlib\nand one module of Lean's own library, \\path{Lean.Elab.Tactic.Omega},\nimported by \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.lean} (line~14) and\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparseCharging.lean} (line~5).\nThat one module is a proof tactic, a tool for writing proofs; the\nclosure defines no tactic of its own (Section~\\ref{sec:proof-keywords}).\nNothing is imported from any other family of the collection or from\nany of the repository's other dependencies.\n\nThe family's directory holds \\textbf{3,977} \\texttt{.lean} files, so\n\\textbf{886} are outside the closure. They are not reachable from the\ncollection's root module either: \\clonefile{lean/OAI.lean} imports the\nfamily only at its line~49, and the build's default target is that root\n(\\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}, line~89,\n\\texttt{@[default\\_target] lean\\_lib OAI}). They are never built by the\ndefault target and bear on nothing: dead files, not a hole. For\ncompleteness, a raw search of the 886 for \\texttt{sorry},\n\\texttt{axiom}, \\texttt{native\\_decide} and \\texttt{admit} finds\nnothing, with or without comments.\n\n\\subsection{The keyword sweep}\n\\label{sec:proof-keywords}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:proof-inspection} gives the counts over the 3,091 files\nwith comments stripped. The ones that matter are the zeros, and each\nzero rules out one way of weakening a verified proof.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item No \\texttt{axiom}, no \\texttt{sorry}, no \\texttt{admit}: nothing\n  is assumed and no proof is left out.\n\\item No \\texttt{native\\_decide} and no \\texttt{ofReduceBool}: nothing is\n  proved by trusting a compiled program's answer instead of the kernel.\n\\item No \\texttt{implemented\\_by}, \\texttt{extern}, \\texttt{unsafe} or\n  \\texttt{partial}: no definition has a hidden implementation, and every\n  recursion is one the kernel checks for termination.\n\\item No \\texttt{elab}, \\texttt{macro}, \\texttt{syntax} or\n  \\texttt{simproc}: no custom programming of Lean itself that could\n  rewrite a goal outside the kernel's view.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThe non-zero counts are ordinary. Nine declarations are \\texttt{opaque},\na keyword that hides a definition's body from the proof-writing layer;\nevery one of the nine has a body, and the kernel checks that body\nagainst its type, so nothing is assumed. The 1,048 \\texttt{noncomputable}\ndeclarations, the eight \\texttt{open Classical} and the four explicit\nuses each of \\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{propext} are the\nclassical reasoning the comparator permits. The 148 instances and 40 local instances are standard bookkeeping declarations (``typeclass instances'') that tell Lean, for example, that a type is finite or a property is decidable; they are harmless because such a declaration can only make a false claim by way of an axiom, and there is none. The only options set in the closure switch off a check on notation in two files and have no bearing on soundness.\nAppendix~\\ref{app:tech-keywords} lists every one of these with its file\nand line.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The inspection in numbers. Each row is one count over the\n3,091 files of the closure, comments stripped where a keyword is\ncounted; the script column says which script produced it. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/proof\\_inspection.py}.}\n\\label{tab:proof-inspection}\n\\input{tables/proof_inspection}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{The statement is the challenge's}\n\\label{sec:proof-match}\n\nThe solution does not import the challenge file. Instead the library\ndeclares again, under the same names, every definition the statement\nuses, spread over seven files. So one has to check that the two copies\nagree. The definition diff compares \\textbf{35} declarations:\n\\textbf{33} are identical character for character once spacing is\nignored, and the remaining two, \\texttt{runtimeScale} and\n\\texttt{runtimeBound}, differ only in that the challenge marks each\ndefinition \\texttt{noncomputable} by itself where the library marks a\nwhole section so (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean}, line~15), a\ndifference the kernel does not see. The theorem statement itself, from\nits first word to the start of its proof, is \\textbf{identical} in the\ntwo files, 1,353 characters raw and 1,124 after normalisation, at\nline~194 of the challenge and line~74 of\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}; the challenge's proof is the single\nword \\texttt{sorry} at its line~218. Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-spine} names\nthe seven files.\n\nThis textual match is a sanity check and not the guarantee. The\ncomparator \\citep{LeanComparator} compares the two statements after\nexporting them from Lean, with every definition the statement mentions\nmatched by name and content, so a library definition that differed from\nthe challenge's under the same name would make the comparator fail\nrather than pass silently. What the text confirms is that there is no\nsuch difference to find, and that the shape Section~\\ref{sec:statement}\nread is the shape the library proves.\n\n\\subsection{The spine of the proof}\n\\label{sec:proof-spine}\n\nWe followed the proof from its last file down to the lemmas that connect\nit to the machine of the statement. Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-spine} gives\nevery name and line; here is what the spine shows.\n\n\\paragraph{The machine is a concrete table.} The theorem's machine is\ncompiled from a program over 80 one-bit registers and 1,220 Boolean\nstacks, with twelve kinds of instruction\n(\\clonefile{BitProgram.lean}, lines 10--26). The compiler\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~131) builds the\nmachine's transition table case by case from the program and then renames the states to numbers by an appeal to a choice principle that names no particular numbering. The theorem only says such a machine exists, so this non-constructive step (one that proves something exists without producing it) costs it nothing; it is also the reason no running time can be read off the\nstatement (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\n\\paragraph{The constants, as far as the proof pins them.} For inputs\nabove a cutoff, the main run supplies a trace within the bound with\n$1 \\le C$ and $1 \\le A$ (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean},\nline~12). Its exponent constant is \\texttt{256 + At} (line~51), where\n\\texttt{At} is at least $1$ and comes from a contract on the leaf\nprogram (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, line~793).\nThe cutoff itself is \\texttt{max 20 (max Nleaf Ns)} (line~50), with \\texttt{Ns} obtained from a statement that something holds for all large enough $n$, which yields no particular number, and never computed. Below the cutoff, a finite scanner gives\n$\\mathtt{time} \\le 28700\\,(L+1)^2$\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeEntry.lean}, line~92). The two branches are folded together, and the final theorem's constants are those of the two branches added up:\n\\texttt{C + 28700, A + 1, S + 5022741, d + 2, e + 2}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~41). So the proof as written\nhas an exponent constant of at least $258$ in front of $\\rhon$, a\npolynomial degree that depends on $k$, and a leading constant of at\nleast $28{,}701$. None of these is in the statement, which asserts only\nthat they are positive, and none of the underlying quantities is\ncomputed anywhere. This confirms, from the proof side, what\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement} said from the statement side: the constants\nare unknown, and the \\emph{smallest} they could be is already far from\n$1$.\n\n\\paragraph{Where a hidden cost model would live, and why it cannot.}\nA proof of a running-time bound for a machine that is compiled from a\nhigher-level program could, in principle, prove the bound against a cost\nfunction of its own and leave the kernel to check only that function's\narithmetic. That is not what happens here. The lemmas that connect the\nprogram to the machine conclude in the challenge's own words: a source\nrun of $n$ steps becomes a run of the compiled machine of $7n$ steps,\nwith the overhead $7$ proved as a theorem\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, lines 152--162), and the\nspace lemma (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/CompileSpace.lean}, lines 125--143)\nproduces a trace of the challenge's own step function with its visited\nspace bounded on every prefix. The \\texttt{time} in the final theorem is\nliterally the length of a trace of the challenge's step function (its\nline~201). No cost function of the compiler's appears in the statement;\na wrong cost model would make these lemmas unprovable, not the theorem\nfalse. This is the structural point on which the rest of the paper\nleans: whatever the proof's internal accounting, the number the theorem\nbounds is the number of steps of the comparator's machine.\n\n\\paragraph{Correctness of the count.} The outer lemma\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 50--51) yields the\ncount word and the answer bit from the canonical search, and a transport\nlemma (line~73) carries both to the machine's tapes. These are theorems\nabout the concrete step function. The arithmetic that makes them true,\nthe sparsification, the ring and moment computations, the search, is the\ncontent of the other three thousand files, and the only claim made about it here is the one the kernel makes: Lean accepts it (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n\n\\subsection{Toolchain, Mathlib, and the build hooks}\n\\label{sec:proof-build}\n\nThe Lean version is fixed (\\clonefile{lean/lean-toolchain}:\n\\texttt{leanprover/lean4:v4.34.1}; \\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}\nline~8: \\texttt{fixedToolchain := true}). The manifest (the list of the packages the build depends on, each at an exact version) lists 42 packages; Mathlib is pinned to upstream revision\n\\texttt{d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612} at\n\\path{leanprover-community/mathlib4}, and the audit of 2026-11-05\nconfirmed that revision on GitHub as the upstream commit that moves to\n\\texttt{v4.34.1}. The repository carries 23 compatibility patches under\n\\clonefile{lean/patches/}, \\textbf{none for Mathlib}; every one is for a\nthird-party package that this closure does not import. The build file has two hooks, pieces of code that run automatically when the build tool configures or updates the package: one clones and patches eleven dependencies, the\nother verifies or applies the patches for the rest\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-keywords}). They touch only the dependency\ndirectory, and only non-Mathlib packages. They are one reason the\ninspection did not build, and the reason the replay of\nSection~\\ref{sec:replay} runs inside a sandbox with the network on.\n\n\\subsection{What the repository does not record}\n\\label{sec:proof-missing}\n\nThree things one would expect beside a verified refutation of a\nforty-year-old hypothesis are absent. Their absence is a fact about\nevidence, not about the proof.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No saved axiom check.} \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}\n  ends with five \\texttt{\\#print axioms} commands (lines 197--201), and\n  seven other files of the closure carry fourteen more (nineteen\n  commands in eight files; the family's directory holds seventeen such\n  files, but nine of them are among the 886 never built). Their output\n  exists only while the build runs and is recorded nowhere: a search of\n  the whole clone for the string \\texttt{depends on axioms} finds\n  nothing.\n\\item \\textbf{No automated build.} \\clonefile{.github/workflows/} holds\n  one file, \\texttt{lock-prs.yml}, which locks pull-request\n  conversations. Nothing builds or checks the Lean.\n\\item \\textbf{No second checker for this family.} The comparator's JSON\n  (\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}) names\n  the one theorem, an empty \\texttt{definition\\_names} list, the three permitted\n  axioms \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Quot.sound},\n  \\texttt{Classical.choice}, and \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false}. Nanoda\n  is an independent checker written in another language\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}); with it off, the collection's own check of\n  this family rests on one implementation of the kernel. Of the\n  collection's 418 challenge JSONs, 413 set \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda} to\n  \\texttt{false}, two to \\texttt{true}, and three omit the key. The\n  comparator's README describes the check as: build challenge and\n  solution in a sandbox, export both, match each named theorem's\n  statement with every declaration it uses, confine the proof to the\n  permitted axioms, and have the Lean kernel accept it; with nanoda on,\n  the exported environment is replayed through the independent checker\n  as well \\citep{Nanoda}. Section~\\ref{sec:related} records the\n  versions.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nTwo smaller consistency facts, for the record. The manuscript's source\nis dated ``17 October 2026, revised 25 October 2026''\n(\\clonefile{preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/build/main_full_version.tex},\nline~25); the collection's history file mentions the family once, in the\nsentence that names the working group at whose request the materials\nwere released (\\clonefile{history.md}, line~18), and its only\n``Formalization Fix'' entry (line~7) concerns another family. And the\nscope note \\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md} states the verified exponent as\n$n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ (line~9), which is what the library\nproves and what Section~\\ref{sec:beyond} compares with the manuscript's.\n\n\\subsection{The trust boundary, joined with the replay}\n\\label{sec:proof-trust}\n\nBefore anything was run, the proof's trust rested on five things:\n(i) OpenAI's unpublished build, since nothing showed the closure\ntype-checks; (ii) the absence of any recorded axiom output; (iii) the\nLean kernel of \\texttt{v4.34.1} and Mathlib \\texttt{d13f23b7} being what\nupstream publishes; (iv) the comparator run, not reproduced; (v) no\nsecond checker for this family. Table~\\ref{tab:proof-trust} says which\nof the five Section~\\ref{sec:replay} moved.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The trust boundary before and after the replay of\nSection~\\ref{sec:replay}. ``Moved'' means the item no longer has to be\ntaken on trust; ``not moved'' means it still does.}\n\\label{tab:proof-trust}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.30\\linewidth}p{0.64\\linewidth}@{}}\n\\toprule\nitem & status after the replay \\\\\n\\midrule\n(i) the build & \\textbf{moved}: the 3,091-module closure built from source in our sandbox at the pinned toolchain, exit~0, no \\texttt{error}, no \\texttt{sorry}. \\\\\n(ii) the axiom output & \\textbf{moved}: \\texttt{\\#print axioms} of the comparator's theorem printed \\texttt{[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]}, and the nineteen in-build axiom lines say the same; the output is on record. \\\\\n(iii) kernel and Mathlib & \\textbf{moved in part}: Mathlib was fetched from upstream at the pinned revision and not patched, so ``as upstream'' is now observed rather than assumed; the Lean kernel of \\texttt{v4.34.1} is still trusted as upstream, now beside a second checker (v). \\\\\n(iv) the comparator run & \\textbf{not moved}: the statement-level match through \\texttt{lean4export} was not performed; the match this paper relies on is the textual one of Section~\\ref{sec:proof-match}. \\\\\n(v) a second checker & \\textbf{moved}: \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} checked the exported dependencies of the theorem, 95,176 declarations, with no errors and the three axioms; what this adds and what it does not is stated in Section~\\ref{sec:replay}. \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAfter the replay, then, the proof's trust rests on four things: the\nLean kernel of \\texttt{v4.34.1} \\emph{and} nanoda agreeing on the\nexported dependencies of the theorem; the export tool \\texttt{lean4export} writing out what the built environment (the set of all checked declarations) holds; upstream\nMathlib at the pinned revision; and the textual, not export-level, match\nbetween the solution's statement and the challenge's. Everything the\ninspection could find by reading, the build and the two checkers\nconfirmed. Nothing the inspection could not settle by reading has been\nsettled by anything else.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:proof-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{The arithmetic was not read.} The inspection followed the\n  spine from \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} down to the compile\n  lemmas and swept the whole closure by keyword; the content of the\n  other three thousand files was not read. The argument above is that\n  its correctness is enforced by the kernel, not that we verified it.\n\\item \\textbf{A text search is necessary, not sufficient.} Zero\n  \\texttt{sorry} and zero \\texttt{axiom} by text search cannot show that\n  a file type-checks; only the build does (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n  And a keyword count is over source text at one commit; a proof can be\n  weakened only through the mechanisms searched for, and we searched for\n  the ones we know.\n\\item \\textbf{The textual statement match is not the comparator's.} The\n  comparator matches exported constants; we matched source text after\n  normalising spacing and found it identical. A difference the\n  normalisation hides would have to be invisible to the kernel as well,\n  but that is an argument, not a run.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the constants' values.} The bounds\n  $A \\ge 258$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ are what the proof's witnesses are\n  built from; they are lower bounds on what this proof exhibits, not\n  statements about any machine's running time, and a different proof of\n  the same statement could exhibit different ones.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the Lean kernel, Mathlib or the toolchain.}\n  All three are trusted as upstream at the pinned versions; the second\n  checker of Section~\\ref{sec:replay} is evidence about the first, not a\n  proof of either.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about what the theorem says.} That is\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement}; a sound proof of a weak bound is a sound\n  proof of a weak bound.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\par\\endgroup\n% Independent replay -- drafted by loop0015 item 01 (2026-11-05), rewritten in\n% plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every number here is read from paper3/replay/logs/ (regenerate: re-run\n% ~/dev/untrusted/replay/run_sandbox.sh as paper3/replay/README.md records;\n% the counts below come from `grep -c` on the copied logs, see\n% paper3/replay/logs/run2/counts.txt).\n\\section{Independent replay}\n\\label{sec:replay}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nThe repository holds no saved output of the axiom check for the family,\nno automated build of its Lean, and \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false} in the\ncomparator's settings (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). We therefore built the\nproof ourselves, in isolation, at the Lean version the repository pins,\nand recorded what the kernel reports. This section says exactly what was\nrun, where, and what came out. The scripts are quoted verbatim in\n\\repofile{paper3/replay/README.md}, and the logs are under\n\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/}.\n\n\\subsection{What was run, and where}\n\nA fresh copy of the clone (commit \\texttt{4eaee875}) was built inside a\nsandbox on the project's machine. The sandbox tool is \\texttt{bwrap}\n(bubblewrap). Inside it, the system directories are read-only; the home\ndirectory is a private, empty one (a \\texttt{tmpfs}), so nothing of the real home or of\nthis repository is visible; processes, inter-process communication and host names are kept apart from the host's (separate ``namespaces''); the whole sandbox is confined to\neight cores (\\texttt{taskset -c 0-7}) at the lowest scheduling\npriority; and the network is on, because the repository's build file\n(its \\texttt{lakefile}) clones its 41 dependencies at build time and the compiled Mathlib comes\nfrom a download cache. Inside the sandbox, the Lean installer\n\\texttt{elan} was installed into the private home and fetched the\npinned Lean version from the repository's own \\texttt{lean-toolchain}\nfile. Then, in the clone's \\texttt{lean/} directory, three commands\nran: \\texttt{lake exe cache get}, which downloads the compiled Mathlib;\n\\texttt{lake build OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, which builds\nthe solution's 3,091 files and not the collection's default target,\nwhich would build all 721 families; and \\texttt{\\#print axioms} of the\none theorem the comparator names,\n\\path{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat},\nthrough \\texttt{lake env lean}. Only text left the sandbox; nothing\nbuilt inside it was executed outside it.\n\nTwo runs were needed. The first, started at 01:03 on 2026-11-05, failed\nat once at the build step because its command line carried\n\\texttt{-j 8}, an option this version of the build tool does not have","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1cad2094-11ee-40b3-a86f-8ba4c86457ea","uuid":"ff1148fc-845b-401d-a23b-7f578a6ab75c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:10:02.200Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"% The proof, inspected -- drafted by loop0015 item 04 (2026-11-05), rewritten\n% in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05). Every number of this section is\n% printed, and every line citation re-checked against the read-only clone at\n% commit 4eaee875, by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py\n% which re-runs the two audit scripts of reports/scratch/eth_audit/\n% (closure.py, defdiff.py) as subprocesses, performs the remaining checks\n% itself, writes latex/tables/proof_inspection.tex, and exits non-zero if\n% anything has moved. Unqualified Lean paths below are relative to\n% lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/ in the clone; the challenge file is\n% lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean. Nothing in the clone was\n% built, imported or run for this section; the build is Section 5's. The\n% line-by-line spine and the keyword details are in Appendix B.\n\\section{The proof, inspected}\n\\label{sec:proof}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement} read the theorem. This section reads the\nproof, as far as reading can go. The question is not whether the\nmathematics is right. The kernel decides that, and\nSection~\\ref{sec:replay} reports what the kernel said when we ran it.\nThe question is whether anything in the source could make the kernel's\nacceptance mean less than it appears to mean. There are known ways for\nthat to happen: an added axiom; a \\texttt{sorry}, which is Lean's word\nfor a proof left out; a step proved by trusting a compiled program\nrather than the kernel; a patched copy of the mathematics library; a\nstatement in the solution that differs from the statement in the\nchallenge; or a running-time bound proved against a cost model of the\nproof's own choosing. We found none of these. What we did find is that\nthe repository offers no recorded evidence that the proof type-checks at all, that is, that Lean accepts it: no saved output of the axiom check, no automated build, and no\nsecond checker for this family. That is why Section~\\ref{sec:replay}\nexists. The last subsection joins the two.\n\n\\subsection{Method}\n\\label{sec:proof-method}\n\nThe clone of \\texttt{openai/math} at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} is treated\nas untrusted data. Every file was read with \\texttt{cat}, \\texttt{sed},\n\\texttt{grep} and \\texttt{python3 -I}, and nothing in it was built,\nimported or executed for this section. Two scripts from the audit of\n2026-11-05 do the mechanical part. \\texttt{closure.py} follows the\n\\texttt{import} lines from the solution's main module, strips comments,\nand counts how often each keyword of interest occurs in what remains.\n\\texttt{defdiff.py} compares each definition in the challenge file with\nthe library's definition of the same name, ignoring differences in\nspacing. Both were re-run for this section, and every number below is\nfrom that fresh run; \\repofile{paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py}\nre-runs them, performs the remaining checks, and regenerates\nTable~\\ref{tab:proof-inspection}. The inspection reads the spine of the\nproof, that is, the final file, the compiler, the entry lemmas and the\nconstants, and sweeps the whole closure by keyword. The arithmetic\nbeneath the spine was not read (Section~\\ref{sec:proof-not}).\n\n\\subsection{The closure}\n\\label{sec:proof-closure}\n\nThe \\emph{closure} of a module is the set of all files it depends on,\ndirectly or through other files. The comparator's JSON names one\nsolution module, \\path{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, a\nsixteen-line file that imports \\path{SqrtLog.ExecutionNativeFinal} and\n\\path{SqrtLogSpace.Final} and declares nothing (\\clonefile{Main.lean}). Its closure inside the repository is\n\\textbf{3,091 modules}, every one in the family's own directory,\ntotalling \\textbf{550,551} lines; the largest file is 2,873 lines\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparsePriorityProgram.lean}).\nOutside the family, the closure reaches \\textbf{50} modules of Mathlib\nand one module of Lean's own library, \\path{Lean.Elab.Tactic.Omega},\nimported by \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.lean} (line~14) and\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparseCharging.lean} (line~5).\nThat one module is a proof tactic, a tool for writing proofs; the\nclosure defines no tactic of its own (Section~\\ref{sec:proof-keywords}).\nNothing is imported from any other family of the collection or from\nany of the repository's other dependencies.\n\nThe family's directory holds \\textbf{3,977} \\texttt{.lean} files, so\n\\textbf{886} are outside the closure. They are not reachable from the\ncollection's root module either: \\clonefile{lean/OAI.lean} imports the\nfamily only at its line~49, and the build's default target is that root\n(\\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}, line~89,\n\\texttt{@[default\\_target] lean\\_lib OAI}). They are never built by the\ndefault target and bear on nothing: dead files, not a hole. For\ncompleteness, a raw search of the 886 for \\texttt{sorry},\n\\texttt{axiom}, \\texttt{native\\_decide} and \\texttt{admit} finds\nnothing, with or without comments.\n\n\\subsection{The keyword sweep}\n\\label{sec:proof-keywords}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:proof-inspection} gives the counts over the 3,091 files\nwith comments stripped. The ones that matter are the zeros, and each\nzero rules out one way of weakening a verified proof.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item No \\texttt{axiom}, no \\texttt{sorry}, no \\texttt{admit}: nothing\n  is assumed and no proof is left out.\n\\item No \\texttt{native\\_decide} and no \\texttt{ofReduceBool}: nothing is\n  proved by trusting a compiled program's answer instead of the kernel.\n\\item No \\texttt{implemented\\_by}, \\texttt{extern}, \\texttt{unsafe} or\n  \\texttt{partial}: no definition has a hidden implementation, and every\n  recursion is one the kernel checks for termination.\n\\item No \\texttt{elab}, \\texttt{macro}, \\texttt{syntax} or\n  \\texttt{simproc}: no custom programming of Lean itself that could\n  rewrite a goal outside the kernel's view.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThe non-zero counts are ordinary. Nine declarations are \\texttt{opaque},\na keyword that hides a definition's body from the proof-writing layer;\nevery one of the nine has a body, and the kernel checks that body\nagainst its type, so nothing is assumed. The 1,048 \\texttt{noncomputable}\ndeclarations, the eight \\texttt{open Classical} and the four explicit\nuses each of \\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{propext} are the\nclassical reasoning the comparator permits. The 148 instances and 40 local instances are standard bookkeeping declarations (``typeclass instances'') that tell Lean, for example, that a type is finite or a property is decidable; they are harmless because such a declaration can only make a false claim by way of an axiom, and there is none. The only options set in the closure switch off a check on notation in two files and have no bearing on soundness.\nAppendix~\\ref{app:tech-keywords} lists every one of these with its file\nand line.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The inspection in numbers. Each row is one count over the\n3,091 files of the closure, comments stripped where a keyword is\ncounted; the script column says which script produced it. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/proof\\_inspection.py}.}\n\\label{tab:proof-inspection}\n\\input{tables/proof_inspection}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{The statement is the challenge's}\n\\label{sec:proof-match}\n\nThe solution does not import the challenge file. Instead the library\ndeclares again, under the same names, every definition the statement\nuses, spread over seven files. So one has to check that the two copies\nagree. The definition diff compares \\textbf{35} declarations:\n\\textbf{33} are identical character for character once spacing is\nignored, and the remaining two, \\texttt{runtimeScale} and\n\\texttt{runtimeBound}, differ only in that the challenge marks each\ndefinition \\texttt{noncomputable} by itself where the library marks a\nwhole section so (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean}, line~15), a\ndifference the kernel does not see. The theorem statement itself, from\nits first word to the start of its proof, is \\textbf{identical} in the\ntwo files, 1,353 characters raw and 1,124 after normalisation, at\nline~194 of the challenge and line~74 of\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}; the challenge's proof is the single\nword \\texttt{sorry} at its line~218. Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-spine} names\nthe seven files.\n\nThis textual match is a sanity check and not the guarantee. The\ncomparator \\citep{LeanComparator} compares the two statements after\nexporting them from Lean, with every definition the statement mentions\nmatched by name and content, so a library definition that differed from\nthe challenge's under the same name would make the comparator fail\nrather than pass silently. What the text confirms is that there is no\nsuch difference to find, and that the shape Section~\\ref{sec:statement}\nread is the shape the library proves.\n\n\\subsection{The spine of the proof}\n\\label{sec:proof-spine}\n\nWe followed the proof from its last file down to the lemmas that connect\nit to the machine of the statement. Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-spine} gives\nevery name and line; here is what the spine shows.\n\n\\paragraph{The machine is a concrete table.} The theorem's machine is\ncompiled from a program over 80 one-bit registers and 1,220 Boolean\nstacks, with twelve kinds of instruction\n(\\clonefile{BitProgram.lean}, lines 10--26). The compiler\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~131) builds the\nmachine's transition table case by case from the program and then renames the states to numbers by an appeal to a choice principle that names no particular numbering. The theorem only says such a machine exists, so this non-constructive step (one that proves something exists without producing it) costs it nothing; it is also the reason no running time can be read off the\nstatement (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\n\\paragraph{The constants, as far as the proof pins them.} For inputs\nabove a cutoff, the main run supplies a trace within the bound with\n$1 \\le C$ and $1 \\le A$ (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean},\nline~12). Its exponent constant is \\texttt{256 + At} (line~51), where\n\\texttt{At} is at least $1$ and comes from a contract on the leaf\nprogram (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, line~793).\nThe cutoff itself is \\texttt{max 20 (max Nleaf Ns)} (line~50), with \\texttt{Ns} obtained from a statement that something holds for all large enough $n$, which yields no particular number, and never computed. Below the cutoff, a finite scanner gives\n$\\mathtt{time} \\le 28700\\,(L+1)^2$\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeEntry.lean}, line~92). The two branches are folded together, and the final theorem's constants are those of the two branches added up:\n\\texttt{C + 28700, A + 1, S + 5022741, d + 2, e + 2}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~41). So the proof as written\nhas an exponent constant of at least $258$ in front of $\\rhon$, a\npolynomial degree that depends on $k$, and a leading constant of at\nleast $28{,}701$. None of these is in the statement, which asserts only\nthat they are positive, and none of the underlying quantities is\ncomputed anywhere. This confirms, from the proof side, what\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement} said from the statement side: the constants\nare unknown, and the \\emph{smallest} they could be is already far from\n$1$.\n\n\\paragraph{Where a hidden cost model would live, and why it cannot.}\nA proof of a running-time bound for a machine that is compiled from a\nhigher-level program could, in principle, prove the bound against a cost\nfunction of its own and leave the kernel to check only that function's\narithmetic. That is not what happens here. The lemmas that connect the\nprogram to the machine conclude in the challenge's own words: a source\nrun of $n$ steps becomes a run of the compiled machine of $7n$ steps,\nwith the overhead $7$ proved as a theorem\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, lines 152--162), and the\nspace lemma (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/CompileSpace.lean}, lines 125--143)\nproduces a trace of the challenge's own step function with its visited\nspace bounded on every prefix. The \\texttt{time} in the final theorem is\nliterally the length of a trace of the challenge's step function (its\nline~201). No cost function of the compiler's appears in the statement;\na wrong cost model would make these lemmas unprovable, not the theorem\nfalse. This is the structural point on which the rest of the paper\nleans: whatever the proof's internal accounting, the number the theorem\nbounds is the number of steps of the comparator's machine.\n\n\\paragraph{Correctness of the count.} The outer lemma\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 50--51) yields the\ncount word and the answer bit from the canonical search, and a transport\nlemma (line~73) carries both to the machine's tapes. These are theorems\nabout the concrete step function. The arithmetic that makes them true,\nthe sparsification, the ring and moment computations, the search, is the\ncontent of the other three thousand files, and the only claim made about it here is the one the kernel makes: Lean accepts it (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n\n\\subsection{Toolchain, Mathlib, and the build hooks}\n\\label{sec:proof-build}\n\nThe Lean version is fixed (\\clonefile{lean/lean-toolchain}:\n\\texttt{leanprover/lean4:v4.34.1}; \\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}\nline~8: \\texttt{fixedToolchain := true}). The manifest (the list of the packages the build depends on, each at an exact version) lists 42 packages; Mathlib is pinned to upstream revision\n\\texttt{d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612} at\n\\path{leanprover-community/mathlib4}, and the audit of 2026-11-05\nconfirmed that revision on GitHub as the upstream commit that moves to\n\\texttt{v4.34.1}. The repository carries 23 compatibility patches under\n\\clonefile{lean/patches/}, \\textbf{none for Mathlib}; every one is for a\nthird-party package that this closure does not import. The build file has two hooks, pieces of code that run automatically when the build tool configures or updates the package: one clones and patches eleven dependencies, the\nother verifies or applies the patches for the rest\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-keywords}). They touch only the dependency\ndirectory, and only non-Mathlib packages. They are one reason the\ninspection did not build, and the reason the replay of\nSection~\\ref{sec:replay} runs inside a sandbox with the network on.\n\n\\subsection{What the repository does not record}\n\\label{sec:proof-missing}\n\nThree things one would expect beside a verified refutation of a\nforty-year-old hypothesis are absent. Their absence is a fact about\nevidence, not about the proof.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No saved axiom check.} \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}\n  ends with five \\texttt{\\#print axioms} commands (lines 197--201), and\n  seven other files of the closure carry fourteen more (nineteen\n  commands in eight files; the family's directory holds seventeen such\n  files, but nine of them are among the 886 never built). Their output\n  exists only while the build runs and is recorded nowhere: a search of\n  the whole clone for the string \\texttt{depends on axioms} finds\n  nothing.\n\\item \\textbf{No automated build.} \\clonefile{.github/workflows/} holds\n  one file, \\texttt{lock-prs.yml}, which locks pull-request\n  conversations. Nothing builds or checks the Lean.\n\\item \\textbf{No second checker for this family.} The comparator's JSON\n  (\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}) names\n  the one theorem, an empty \\texttt{definition\\_names} list, the three permitted\n  axioms \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Quot.sound},\n  \\texttt{Classical.choice}, and \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false}. Nanoda\n  is an independent checker written in another language\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}); with it off, the collection's own check of\n  this family rests on one implementation of the kernel. Of the\n  collection's 418 challenge JSONs, 413 set \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda} to\n  \\texttt{false}, two to \\texttt{true}, and three omit the key. The\n  comparator's README describes the check as: build challenge and\n  solution in a sandbox, export both, match each named theorem's\n  statement with every declaration it uses, confine the proof to the\n  permitted axioms, and have the Lean kernel accept it; with nanoda on,\n  the exported environment is replayed through the independent checker\n  as well \\citep{Nanoda}. Section~\\ref{sec:related} records the\n  versions.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nTwo smaller consistency facts, for the record. The manuscript's source\nis dated ``17 October 2026, revised 25 October 2026''\n(\\clonefile{preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/build/main_full_version.tex},\nline~25); the collection's history file mentions the family once, in the\nsentence that names the working group at whose request the materials\nwere released (\\clonefile{history.md}, line~18), and its only\n``Formalization Fix'' entry (line~7) concerns another family. And the\nscope note \\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md} states the verified exponent as\n$n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ (line~9), which is what the library\nproves and what Section~\\ref{sec:beyond} compares with the manuscript's.\n\n\\subsection{The trust boundary, joined with the replay}\n\\label{sec:proof-trust}\n\nBefore anything was run, the proof's trust rested on five things:\n(i) OpenAI's unpublished build, since nothing showed the closure\ntype-checks; (ii) the absence of any recorded axiom output; (iii) the\nLean kernel of \\texttt{v4.34.1} and Mathlib \\texttt{d13f23b7} being what\nupstream publishes; (iv) the comparator run, not reproduced; (v) no\nsecond checker for this family. Table~\\ref{tab:proof-trust} says which\nof the five Section~\\ref{sec:replay} moved.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The trust boundary before and after the replay of\nSection~\\ref{sec:replay}. ``Moved'' means the item no longer has to be\ntaken on trust; ``not moved'' means it still does.}\n\\label{tab:proof-trust}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.30\\linewidth}p{0.64\\linewidth}@{}}\n\\toprule\nitem & status after the replay \\\\\n\\midrule\n(i) the build & \\textbf{moved}: the 3,091-module closure built from source in our sandbox at the pinned toolchain, exit~0, no \\texttt{error}, no \\texttt{sorry}. \\\\\n(ii) the axiom output & \\textbf{moved}: \\texttt{\\#print axioms} of the comparator's theorem printed \\texttt{[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]}, and the nineteen in-build axiom lines say the same; the output is on record. \\\\\n(iii) kernel and Mathlib & \\textbf{moved in part}: Mathlib was fetched from upstream at the pinned revision and not patched, so ``as upstream'' is now observed rather than assumed; the Lean kernel of \\texttt{v4.34.1} is still trusted as upstream, now beside a second checker (v). \\\\\n(iv) the comparator run & \\textbf{not moved}: the statement-level match through \\texttt{lean4export} was not performed; the match this paper relies on is the textual one of Section~\\ref{sec:proof-match}. \\\\\n(v) a second checker & \\textbf{moved}: \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} checked the exported dependencies of the theorem, 95,176 declarations, with no errors and the three axioms; what this adds and what it does not is stated in Section~\\ref{sec:replay}. \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAfter the replay, then, the proof's trust rests on four things: the\nLean kernel of \\texttt{v4.34.1} \\emph{and} nanoda agreeing on the\nexported dependencies of the theorem; the export tool \\texttt{lean4export} writing out what the built environment (the set of all checked declarations) holds; upstream\nMathlib at the pinned revision; and the textual, not export-level, match\nbetween the solution's statement and the challenge's. Everything the\ninspection could find by reading, the build and the two checkers\nconfirmed. Nothing the inspection could not settle by reading has been\nsettled by anything else.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:proof-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{The arithmetic was not read.} The inspection followed the\n  spine from \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} down to the compile\n  lemmas and swept the whole closure by keyword; the content of the\n  other three thousand files was not read. The argument above is that\n  its correctness is enforced by the kernel, not that we verified it.\n\\item \\textbf{A text search is necessary, not sufficient.} Zero\n  \\texttt{sorry} and zero \\texttt{axiom} by text search cannot show that\n  a file type-checks; only the build does (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n  And a keyword count is over source text at one commit; a proof can be\n  weakened only through the mechanisms searched for, and we searched for\n  the ones we know.\n\\item \\textbf{The textual statement match is not the comparator's.} The\n  comparator matches exported constants; we matched source text after\n  normalising spacing and found it identical. A difference the\n  normalisation hides would have to be invisible to the kernel as well,\n  but that is an argument, not a run.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the constants' values.} The bounds\n  $A \\ge 258$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ are what the proof's witnesses are\n  built from; they are lower bounds on what this proof exhibits, not\n  statements about any machine's running time, and a different proof of\n  the same statement could exhibit different ones.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the Lean kernel, Mathlib or the toolchain.}\n  All three are trusted as upstream at the pinned versions; the second\n  checker of Section~\\ref{sec:replay} is evidence about the first, not a\n  proof of either.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about what the theorem says.} That is\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement}; a sound proof of a weak bound is a sound\n  proof of a weak bound.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\par\\endgroup\n% Independent replay -- drafted by loop0015 item 01 (2026-11-05), rewritten in\n% plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05).\n% Every number here is read from paper3/replay/logs/ (regenerate: re-run\n% ~/dev/untrusted/replay/run_sandbox.sh as paper3/replay/README.md records;\n% the counts below come from `grep -c` on the copied logs, see\n% paper3/replay/logs/run2/counts.txt).\n\\section{Independent replay}\n\\label{sec:replay}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nThe repository holds no saved output of the axiom check for the family,\nno automated build of its Lean, and \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false} in the\ncomparator's settings (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). We therefore built the\nproof ourselves, in isolation, at the Lean version the repository pins,\nand recorded what the kernel reports. This section says exactly what was\nrun, where, and what came out. The scripts are quoted verbatim in\n\\repofile{paper3/replay/README.md}, and the logs are under\n\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/}.\n\n\\subsection{What was run, and where}\n\nA fresh copy of the clone (commit \\texttt{4eaee875}) was built inside a\nsandbox on the project's machine. The sandbox tool is \\texttt{bwrap}\n(bubblewrap). Inside it, the system directories are read-only; the home\ndirectory is a private, empty one (a \\texttt{tmpfs}), so nothing of the real home or of\nthis repository is visible; processes, inter-process communication and host names are kept apart from the host's (separate ``namespaces''); the whole sandbox is confined to\neight cores (\\texttt{taskset -c 0-7}) at the lowest scheduling\npriority; and the network is on, because the repository's build file\n(its \\texttt{lakefile}) clones its 41 dependencies at build time and the compiled Mathlib comes\nfrom a download cache. Inside the sandbox, the Lean installer\n\\texttt{elan} was installed into the private home and fetched the\npinned Lean version from the repository's own \\texttt{lean-toolchain}\nfile. Then, in the clone's \\texttt{lean/} directory, three commands\nran: \\texttt{lake exe cache get}, which downloads the compiled Mathlib;\n\\texttt{lake build OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, which builds\nthe solution's 3,091 files and not the collection's default target,\nwhich would build all 721 families; and \\texttt{\\#print axioms} of the\none theorem the comparator names,\n\\path{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat},\nthrough \\texttt{lake env lean}. Only text left the sandbox; nothing\nbuilt inside it was executed outside it.\n\nTwo runs were needed. The first, started at 01:03 on 2026-11-05, failed\nat once at the build step because its command line carried\n\\texttt{-j 8}, an option this version of the build tool does not have","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PstKSgxL5vGhWv3J7HavU6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
