{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgjNMeDNyrSRDPEJt3jZL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016MUxV5ajhLXXT16TH3tG5L","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex && pdftoppm -f 1 -l 2 -r 70 -png main.pdf /tmp/p3page && ls /tmp/p3page*; pdftotext -f 1 -l 1 main.pdf - | head -60","description":"Render the first two pages and extract page 1 text"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1935,"cache_read_input_tokens":224980,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1935,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1cad2094-11ee-40b3-a86f-8ba4c86457ea","uuid":"f235a48b-a097-46ba-8a1d-0f1d8bba5529","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:15:23.398Z","request_id":"req_011CgjNMdkMYfbqwfTmmMCGE","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_016MUxV5ajhLXXT16TH3tG5L":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex && pdftoppm -f 1 -l 2 -r 70 -png main.pdf /tmp/p3page && ls /tmp/p3page*; pdftotext -f 1 -l 1 main.pdf - | head -60","description":"Render the first two pages and extract page 1 text"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016MUxV5ajhLXXT16TH3tG5L","type":"tool_result","content":"/tmp/p3page-01.png\n/tmp/p3page-02.png\n\n/tmp/p3pages:\npg-38.png\npg-39.png\npg-40.png\npg-41.png\npg-42.png\npg-43.png\ntbl-39.png\nv2-42.png\nExponential time hypothesis is false. . . but the verified\nalgorithm is slower than trying all 2n assignments at every\npractical size.\nAlex Linhares\nArgo Lab AGI; on leave from the Getulio Vargas Foundation\nalex@argolab.org ORCID 0000-0002-4227-6879\n\nNovember 5, 2026\nAbstract\nIn October 2026 OpenAI published a theorem, checked by the Lean proof assistant, that refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis: the forty-year-old conjecture that deciding whether a\nformula with three variables per clause can be satisfied needs time exponential in the number\nof variables. We read the verified statement exactly, rebuilt its proof in isolation, re-checked\nit with a second, independent proof checker, and found no flaw. The theorem does refute the\nhypothesis. What we add is exactness about what is verified. The theorem is a guarantee, not\na running time: it says that a machine and some constants exist, and never computes them. Its\nexponent is the one the manuscript itself calls weaker; the sharper exponents the manuscript\nstates are not in the Lean library. With every constant set to one, the verified exponent exceeds\nthe number of variables at every size from 50 variables up to a number with 2,391 digits. So\nat every practical size the verified guarantee is weaker than the guarantee of trying all 2n assignments. We say what follows for the hypotheses, for the lower bounds derived from them,\nand for anyone who runs a SAT solver: nothing changes on any machine.\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nOn 25 October 2026, OpenAI’s collection of machine-checked mathematics added a result it\ncalls family 378, The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False (OpenAI, 2026). The Exponential Time\nHypothesis, ETH, is a conjecture from 2001 (Impagliazzo and Paturi, 2001): that deciding whether\na formula with three variables per clause can be satisfied needs time that grows exponentially in\nthe number of variables. The result comes with a 67-page manuscript and a proof checked by the\nLean proof assistant, a program that verifies every step of a proof and rejects a proof with a gap.\nThe verified theorem does refute the hypothesis. The title of this paper says what else is true of it,\nand the four clauses of that sentence are these. First, what is verified is a guarantee, not a running\ntime: the theorem says that a machine halts within a stated bound, and it says nothing about\nhow long the machine actually takes on any input. Second, the bound contains constants that\nthe theorem only says exist; no value of any of them is computed anywhere, and the one place\nthe proof writes a value down gives the exponent’s constant as at least 257 (Section 2). Third,\nwith every constant set to one, the exponent in that bound is larger than the number of variables\nat every size from 50 variables up to a number with 2,391 digits (Section 2, Figure 2.1). Over that\n1","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1cad2094-11ee-40b3-a86f-8ba4c86457ea","uuid":"b4ebbb58-fe25-4961-a3b2-198b000e58dc","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:15:23.550Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/tmp/p3page-01.png\n/tmp/p3page-02.png\n\n/tmp/p3pages:\npg-38.png\npg-39.png\npg-40.png\npg-41.png\npg-42.png\npg-43.png\ntbl-39.png\nv2-42.png\nExponential time hypothesis is false. . . but the verified\nalgorithm is slower than trying all 2n assignments at every\npractical size.\nAlex Linhares\nArgo Lab AGI; on leave from the Getulio Vargas Foundation\nalex@argolab.org ORCID 0000-0002-4227-6879\n\nNovember 5, 2026\nAbstract\nIn October 2026 OpenAI published a theorem, checked by the Lean proof assistant, that refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis: the forty-year-old conjecture that deciding whether a\nformula with three variables per clause can be satisfied needs time exponential in the number\nof variables. We read the verified statement exactly, rebuilt its proof in isolation, re-checked\nit with a second, independent proof checker, and found no flaw. The theorem does refute the\nhypothesis. What we add is exactness about what is verified. The theorem is a guarantee, not\na running time: it says that a machine and some constants exist, and never computes them. Its\nexponent is the one the manuscript itself calls weaker; the sharper exponents the manuscript\nstates are not in the Lean library. With every constant set to one, the verified exponent exceeds\nthe number of variables at every size from 50 variables up to a number with 2,391 digits. So\nat every practical size the verified guarantee is weaker than the guarantee of trying all 2n assignments. We say what follows for the hypotheses, for the lower bounds derived from them,\nand for anyone who runs a SAT solver: nothing changes on any machine.\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nOn 25 October 2026, OpenAI’s collection of machine-checked mathematics added a result it\ncalls family 378, The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False (OpenAI, 2026). The Exponential Time\nHypothesis, ETH, is a conjecture from 2001 (Impagliazzo and Paturi, 2001): that deciding whether\na formula with three variables per clause can be satisfied needs time that grows exponentially in\nthe number of variables. The result comes with a 67-page manuscript and a proof checked by the\nLean proof assistant, a program that verifies every step of a proof and rejects a proof with a gap.\nThe verified theorem does refute the hypothesis. The title of this paper says what else is true of it,\nand the four clauses of that sentence are these. First, what is verified is a guarantee, not a running\ntime: the theorem says that a machine halts within a stated bound, and it says nothing about\nhow long the machine actually takes on any input. Second, the bound contains constants that\nthe theorem only says exist; no value of any of them is computed anywhere, and the one place\nthe proof writes a value down gives the exponent’s constant as at least 257 (Section 2). Third,\nwith every constant set to one, the exponent in that bound is larger than the number of variables\nat every size from 50 variables up to a number with 2,391 digits (Section 2, Figure 2.1). Over that\n1","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016MUxV5ajhLXXT16TH3tG5L","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
