{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EaUK7gwzAo14YCCw9poYYH","type":"tool_result","content":"Makefile\nfigures\nmain.aux\nmain.bbl\nmain.blg\nmain.fdb_latexmk\nmain.fls\nmain.log\nmain.out\nmain.pdf\nmain.tex\nrefs.bib\nsec10_conclusion.tex\nsec11_data.tex\nsec12_ack.tex\nsec1_intro.tex\nsec2_statement.tex\nsec3_beyond.tex\nsec4_proof.tex\nsec5_replay.tex\nsec6_consequences.tex\nsec7_practitioner.tex\nsec8_lean.tex\nsec9_related.tex\nsecA_appendix_lean.tex\nsecB_appendix_technical.tex\ntables\n----\n\\begin{abstract}\n\\noindent\n% Item 09 (2026-11-05). Under 200 words; the only numbers are 50 and\n% the 2,391 digits of e^5504, both printed by paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py\n% (Section 2); the only formula is 2^n (owner's rule of 2026-11-05 05:30).\nIn October 2026 OpenAI published a theorem, checked by the Lean proof\nassistant, that refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis: the\nforty-year-old conjecture that deciding whether a formula with three\nvariables per clause can be satisfied needs time exponential in the\nnumber of variables. We read the verified statement exactly, rebuilt its\nproof in isolation, re-checked it with a second, independent proof\nchecker, and found no flaw. The theorem does refute the hypothesis. What\nwe add is exactness about \\emph{what} is verified. The theorem is a\nguarantee, not a running time: it says that a machine and some constants\nexist, and never computes them. Its exponent is the one the manuscript\nitself calls weaker; the sharper exponents the manuscript states are not\nin the Lean library. With every constant set to one, the verified\nexponent exceeds the number of variables at every size from 50 variables\nup to a number with 2,391 digits. So at every practical size the\nverified guarantee is weaker than the guarantee of trying all $2^n$\nassignments. We say what follows for the hypotheses, for the lower\nbounds derived from them, and for anyone who runs a SAT solver: nothing\nchanges on any machine.\n\\end{abstract}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LUW2a8rbbvHhsaADMWYwFV","agent_id":"a5dd7e393af2824fa","session_id":"1cad2094-11ee-40b3-a86f-8ba4c86457ea","uuid":"ff9a9346-4270-42e2-a78c-2bba1cc7cb65","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:15:46.523Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EaUK7gwzAo14YCCw9poYYH","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Blind read of intro and conclusion"}
