{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015tnbFcjbFthh9JxgEFYtbw","type":"tool_result","content":"% Introduction -- drafted by loop0015 item 09 (2026-11-05), under the owner's\n% plain-English rule of 2026-11-05 05:30: no formula beyond 2^n in this\n% section, every term of art glossed in one clause on first use, every\n% number with the section that regenerates it. The title's four clauses are\n% made precise in the first paragraph; the symbols (the exponent, the\n% constants) are introduced in Section 2 and used from there on.\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nOn 25 October 2026, OpenAI's collection of machine-checked mathematics\nadded a result it calls family 378, \\emph{The Exponential Time\nHypothesis is False}\n\\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}.\nThe Exponential Time Hypothesis, ETH, is a conjecture from 2001\n\\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}: that deciding whether a formula with\nthree variables per clause can be satisfied needs time that grows\nexponentially in the number of variables. The result comes with a\n67-page manuscript and a proof checked by the Lean proof assistant, a\nprogram that verifies every step of a proof and rejects a proof with a\ngap. The verified theorem does refute the hypothesis. The title of this\npaper says what else is true of it, and the four clauses of that\nsentence are these. First, what is verified is a \\emph{guarantee}, not\na running time: the theorem says that a machine halts within a stated\nbound, and it says nothing about how long the machine actually takes on\nany input. Second, the bound contains constants that the theorem only\nsays \\emph{exist}; no value of any of them is computed anywhere, and the\none place the proof writes a value down gives the exponent's constant\nas at least 257 (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). Third, with every\nconstant set to one, the exponent in that bound is larger than the\nnumber of variables at every size from 50 variables up to a number with\n2,391 digits (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, Figure~\\ref{fig:central}).\nOver that whole range the verified bound is above $2^n$, the cost of\ntrying every one of the $2^n$ assignments of $n$ variables; so the\nverified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size, than the\nguarantee of the method everyone already has. Fourth, the manuscript\nstates two sharper exponents, which would make a different sentence,\nand neither is in the Lean library; the manuscript itself calls the\nverified one ``the weaker exponent'' (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\nThis paper is a careful reading by people who checked, not a debunking.\nWe read the verified statement line by line, inspected the proof's\nsource, rebuilt the proof from source in isolation, re-checked it with\na second proof checker that shares no code with Lean, and found no flaw:\nthe statement means what the manuscript's verified form says, and the\nproof rests on the three standard axioms and nothing else. Every\nsentence that could read as ``wrong'' or ``overclaimed'' is replaced by\nwhat the Lean statement and the manuscript actually say, with the file\nand line, or the page and line, beside it. Nothing is quoted from\nmemory. Every number has a script that regenerates it, named where the\nnumber appears.\n\n\\subsection{What this paper does}\n\\label{sec:intro-summary}\n\nIn one paragraph. The verified statement (Section~\\ref{sec:statement})\nsays that for each fixed clause width there exist a finite machine of\nthe textbook kind, a multitape Turing machine, and five positive\nconstants, such that on every formula of that width the machine halts\nwith the exact number of satisfying assignments written on a tape,\nwithin a bound that is a constant times a power of the input length\ntimes two raised to a constant times the exponent. The exponent is a\nfixed function of the number of variables, written out in\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}; its ratio to the number of variables rises\nabove one at 50, peaks at about 2.17 near a number with 24 digits, and\nfalls back below one only past a number with 2,391 digits. The\nmanuscript claims more than the statement (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}):\ntwo sharper exponents, a circuit lower bound that depends on one of\nthem, and a chapter of proofs by tensor contraction; we tag every claim\nverified, supported on paper by the verified statement, or paper-only.\nWe inspected the proof (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}): all 3,091 source\nfiles it depends on, with no axiom, no omitted proof, no step that\ntrusts a compiled program, no patch to the mathematics library, and a\nstatement identical to the one the checking tool posed. We replayed it\n(Section~\\ref{sec:replay}): the 3,091 files built from source in a\nsandbox at the pinned Lean version in 33 minutes, the kernel reported\nthe three standard axioms, and the independent checker nanoda\nre-checked the theorem's 95,176 exported dependencies with no error. We\nsay what follows (Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}): ETH, its strong form\nSETH, the gap, nondeterministic and counting forms, and their\nrandomised versions are refuted by the statement alone; a lower bound\nthat was proved \\emph{from} one of them loses its premise, and the\nstatement it concluded is not itself refuted. We say what it changes\nfor a practitioner (Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}): nothing on any\nmachine, which we show by timing an ordinary SAT solver and two model\ncounters on random formulas of up to 500 variables and drawing the two\nguarantees beside the measurements. We restate the verified sentence in\nour own Lean library under one name, with its axioms audited\n(Section~\\ref{sec:lean}), and place the paper beside what the\ncollection, the hypotheses' authors, a published human audit, and the\ntwo checking tools say about themselves (Section~\\ref{sec:related}).\n\n\\subsection{The words we use}\n\\label{sec:intro-words}\n\nEach section glosses its own terms where they first appear, so that a\nreader may enter anywhere. The ones the whole paper needs are these,\neach in one sentence.\nA \\emph{formula} is a list of clauses, each clause an ``or'' of a few\nvariables or negated variables, and the formula is \\emph{satisfied} by\na choice of true or false for every variable that makes every clause\ntrue; a \\emph{$k$-CNF} is a formula with at most $k$ variables per\nclause, and 3-SAT is the problem of deciding whether a 3-CNF can be\nsatisfied.\n\\emph{Trying all $2^n$ assignments} is the method of checking every one\nof the $2^n$ ways to set $n$ variables; its cost is the yardstick in the\ntitle.\n\\emph{ETH} is the conjecture that 3-SAT needs time exponential in $n$;\n\\emph{SETH}, its strong form, that as $k$ grows no algorithm for $k$-SAT\ndoes much better than trying all assignments.\n\\emph{Lean} is the proof assistant; its \\emph{kernel} is the small core\nthat does the final checking, and ``verified'' in this paper always\nmeans ``accepted by the kernel''.\nThe \\emph{comparator} is OpenAI's checking tool: it takes a\n\\emph{challenge file} that states a theorem with the proof left blank,\nand a \\emph{solution} that proves it, and checks that both state the\nsame theorem and that the proof uses only three standard axioms, which\nare the three assumptions every ordinary Lean proof makes.\n\\emph{nanoda} is a second, independent checker for Lean proofs, written\nin another language; a \\emph{replay} is a rebuild of a proof from source\non our own machine, so that nothing rests on a build we did not see.\nA \\emph{SAT solver} is the program a practitioner runs to decide a\nformula, and a \\emph{model counter} the one that counts its satisfying\nassignments.\nA \\emph{guarantee} is a bound a theorem promises; a \\emph{running time}\nis what a clock measures; the two are compared only with their own kind.\n\n\\subsection{What is claimed, and what is not}\n\\label{sec:intro-claims}\n\nClaimed, each with the section that establishes it:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item The verified statement is a running-time \\emph{guarantee} with\n  constants that exist and are never computed, for each fixed clause\n  width, with no lower limit on the number of variables\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item With every constant set to one, the verified exponent exceeds the\n  number of variables at every size from 50 up to a number with 2,391\n  digits, so the verified bound is above $2^n$ there; with the true\n  constants, which are unknown and at least 257 in the proof on record,\n  it is higher still (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item The manuscript's two sharper exponents, and the quantitative\n  circuit bound that rests on one of them, are not in the Lean library\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\\item The proof's source shows none of the known ways of weakening a\n  verified proof, and its statement is the challenge's\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); it builds from source at the pinned\n  version with the three standard axioms, and a second checker agrees\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n\\item The verified statement refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, NSETH, \\#ETH\n  and their randomised forms, by the parts of the statement named in\n  each case; a lower bound derived from any of them loses its premise\n  and nothing more (Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}).\n\\item On random formulas, the solvers a practitioner has are many\n  orders of magnitude below $2^n$ at every size measured, and the\n  verified guarantee is above $2^n$ from 50 variables on; the\n  verified result changes nothing that runs\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}).\n\\end{enumerate}\nNot claimed:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item No running time of the verified machine, in either direction. No\n  implementation of it exists; the title compares two guarantees.\n\\item No flaw in the statement, the proof, the Lean kernel, Mathlib, or\n  the collection's checking tool. We found none and assert none.\n\\item No value of any constant beyond what the proof writes down, and\n  nothing about what a different proof of the same statement could\n  achieve.\n\\item No judgement on the manuscript's paper-only proofs, which were\n  located and quoted, not read for correctness.\n\\item No refutation of any lower bound's conclusion, for any problem,\n  and nothing about any specific problem's complexity beyond what the\n  manuscript's own remarks state.\n\\item Nothing about any problem outside satisfiability. This paper is\n  about the limitation of the ETH refutation and nothing else.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{How the paper is organised}\n\\label{sec:intro-organisation}\n\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement} reads the statement; Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}\ndraws the line between it and the manuscript;\nSections~\\ref{sec:proof} and \\ref{sec:replay} inspect and replay the\nproof; Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} says what follows;\nSection~\\ref{sec:practitioner} is for the practitioner;\nSection~\\ref{sec:lean} is our own Lean; Section~\\ref{sec:related} is\nrelated work; Section~\\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes and lists the\nquestions this reading left for its author to decide. Every section\nends with a list headed ``what this does not claim''. Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}\nlists every Lean name the paper cites; Appendix~\\ref{app:tech} holds the\ntechnical detail, the definitions with their line numbers, the theorem\nwith its quantifiers, the spine of the proof, behind the plain text of\nthe body. The OpenAI repository is read throughout at one commit,\n\\texttt{4eaee875}, as untrusted data: with text tools only, never built\nor run in place; the one build is the sandboxed replay.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:intro-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the sections it summarises.} Every\n  sentence of this introduction is a sentence of a later section said\n  shorter; where the two differ, the later section, with its file and\n  line, is the one that counts.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the result's importance.} Whether the\n  refutation matters is not a question this paper asks; what the\n  verified statement says, and at what sizes, is.\n\\item \\textbf{No prior-art claim.} ``We found no earlier statement'' of\n  the title's observation is bounded to the six texts\n  Section~\\ref{sec:related} names; the literature at large was not\n  searched.\n\\end{itemize}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LUW2a8rbbvHhsaADMWYwFV","agent_id":"a5dd7e393af2824fa","session_id":"1cad2094-11ee-40b3-a86f-8ba4c86457ea","uuid":"d48d555b-fc4a-4ccd-a9e0-0c2c8b3606fb","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:15:48.992Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015tnbFcjbFthh9JxgEFYtbw","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Blind read of intro and conclusion"}
