ised NSETH or #ETH is stated.\n- Section 1: nanoda \"shares no code with **Lean**\". Sections 5 and 9: \"shares no code with the **Lean kernel**\" (its input is produced by lean4export, a Lean tool).\n- Section 1: \"re-checked the theorem's 95,176 exported **dependencies**\". Section 5: \"Checked 95176 declarations\" (the file includes the theorem and the three axiom declarations).\n\n**2. Readability (abstract and Section 1)**\n\n- Abstract: \"Lean proof assistant\", \"exponent\", \"SAT solver\", \"lower bounds derived from them\" are used unglossed; the opening sentence is 44 words.\n- Section 1, para 1: \"exponent\" is used unglossed throughout the section (first gloss is Section 2); the \"Second, the bound contains constants...\" sentence is 48 words; \"Over that whole range...\" is 42.\n- Para 2: \"three standard axioms\" before the gloss in 1.2; 57-word sentence.\n- 1.1 (before the glossary in 1.2): \"multitape Turing machine\", \"kernel\", \"nanoda\", \"sandbox\", \"pinned Lean version\", \"circuit lower bound\", \"tensor contraction\", \"nondeterministic\", \"SETH\", \"loses its premise\" — kernel/nanoda/SETH are glossed only later in 1.2, the rest never in Section 1. Sentences of 73, 49, 43, 42, 42, 48, 42, 42 words.\n- 1.2: symbol $k$ (\"$k$-CNF\", \"$k$-SAT\") beyond $2^n$ and $n$; the formula gloss is 63 words, the comparator gloss 53.\n- 1.3: \"Gap-ETH, NSETH, #ETH\", \"quantitative circuit bound\", \"clause width\", \"Mathlib\" (named only here; earlier \"mathematics library\") unglossed; claim 2 is 55 words.\n- 1.4: the organisation sentence is 53 words.\n\n**3. Ledger arithmetic**\n\nThe ledger has 48 entries. Resolved (S1, S3+07a, S4, S6, S7, S8) = 7; procedural (S5, 01a, 03b, 04b, 04d, 04e, 06b, 08xb) = 8; open list names 33 distinct entries. 7 + 8 + 33 = 48; every ledger entry appears exactly once in the section and no number in the section is absent from the ledger. \"Thirteen of the thirty-three\": S2, 02b, 02d, 05c, 06c, 08a, 08b, 08c (8) + 02a