{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgjNuJjVqYw8BAgzBN4UF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JFnmfhi8bcfghUUGgSmDKa","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat > sec1_intro.tex <<'EOF'\n% Introduction -- drafted by loop0015 item 09 (2026-11-05), under the owner's\n% plain-English rule of 2026-11-05 05:30: no formula beyond 2^n in this\n% section, every term of art glossed in one clause on first use, every\n% number with the section that regenerates it. The title's four clauses are\n% made precise in the first paragraph; the symbols (the exponent, the\n% constants) are introduced in Section 2 and used from there on. A blind\n% reader's pass (one subagent, 2026-11-05) moved the glossary ahead of the\n% summary and shortened the long sentences.\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nIn late October 2026, OpenAI's collection of machine-checked mathematics\nadded a result dated 25 October 2026 that it calls family 378,\n\\emph{The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False}\n\\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}.\nThe Exponential Time Hypothesis, ETH, is a conjecture from 2001\n\\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. It says that deciding whether a formula\nwith three variables per clause can be satisfied needs time that grows\nexponentially in the number of variables. The result comes with a\n67-page manuscript and a proof checked by the Lean proof assistant, a\nprogram that verifies every step of a proof and rejects a proof with a\ngap. The verified theorem does refute the hypothesis. The title of this\npaper says what else is true of it. Its sentence has four clauses.\nFirst, what is verified is a \\emph{guarantee}, not a running time. The\ntheorem says that a machine halts within a stated bound; it says\nnothing about how long the machine actually takes on any input. Second,\nthe bound contains constants that the theorem only says \\emph{exist}.\nNo value of any of them is computed anywhere. Where the proof does\nwrite a value down, the constant in the exponent is at least 257\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). Third, the \\emph{exponent}, the quantity\nthat two is raised to in the bound, is a fixed function of the number\nof variables. With every constant set to one, it is larger than the\nnumber of variables at every size from 50 variables up to a number with\n2,391 digits (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, Figure~\\ref{fig:central}).\nOver that whole range the verified bound is above $2^n$, the cost of\ntrying every one of the $2^n$ assignments of $n$ variables. So the\nverified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size, than the\nguarantee of the method everyone already has. Fourth, the manuscript\nstates two sharper exponents, which would make a different sentence.\nNeither is in the Lean library, and the manuscript itself calls the\nverified one ``the weaker exponent'' (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\nThis paper is a careful reading by people who checked, not a debunking.\nWe read the verified statement line by line. We inspected the proof's\nsource. We rebuilt the proof from source in isolation and re-checked it\nwith a second proof checker that shares no code with Lean's own. We\nfound no flaw: the statement means what the manuscript's verified form\nsays, and the proof rests on the three standard axioms, the assumptions\nevery ordinary Lean proof makes, and nothing else. Every sentence that\ncould read as ``wrong'' or ``overclaimed'' is replaced by what the Lean\nstatement and the manuscript actually say, with the file and line, or\nthe page and line, beside it. Nothing is quoted from memory. Every\nnumber has a script that regenerates it, named where the number\nappears.\n\n\\subsection{The words we use}\n\\label{sec:intro-words}\n\nEach section glosses its own terms where they first appear, so that a\nreader may enter anywhere. The ones the whole paper needs are these,\neach in a sentence or two.\nA \\emph{formula} is a list of clauses, each clause an ``or'' of a few\nvariables or negated variables. A choice of true or false for every\nvariable \\emph{satisfies} the formula when it makes every clause true.\nA \\emph{$k$-CNF} is a formula with at most $k$ variables per clause,\nand $k$ is called the \\emph{clause width}; 3-SAT is the problem of\ndeciding whether a 3-CNF can be satisfied.\n\\emph{Trying all $2^n$ assignments} is the method of checking every one\nof the $2^n$ ways to set $n$ variables; its cost is the yardstick in the\ntitle.\n\\emph{ETH} is the conjecture that 3-SAT needs time exponential in $n$.\n\\emph{SETH}, its strong form, says that as $k$ grows no algorithm for\n$k$-SAT does much better than trying all assignments. Several variants\nexist, and Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} names each.\nA \\emph{guarantee} is a bound a theorem promises; a \\emph{running time}\nis what a clock measures; the two are compared only with their own\nkind.\n\\emph{Lean} is the proof assistant. Its \\emph{kernel} is the small core\nthat does the final checking, and ``verified'' in this paper always\nmeans ``accepted by the kernel''. \\emph{Mathlib} is Lean's community\nlibrary of mathematics.\nThe \\emph{comparator} is OpenAI's checking tool. It takes a\n\\emph{challenge file}, which states a theorem with the proof left\nblank, and a \\emph{solution}, which proves it. It checks that both\nstate the same theorem and that the proof uses only the three standard\naxioms.\n\\emph{nanoda} is a second, independent checker for Lean proofs, written\nin another language. A \\emph{replay} is a rebuild of a proof from\nsource on our own machine, inside a \\emph{sandbox}, an isolated\nenvironment that sees nothing else on the machine, at the \\emph{pinned}\nLean version, the exact version the repository names.\nA \\emph{SAT solver} is the program a practitioner runs to decide a\nformula, and a \\emph{model counter} the one that counts its satisfying\nassignments.\n\n\\subsection{What this paper does}\n\\label{sec:intro-summary}\n\nIn one paragraph. The verified statement (Section~\\ref{sec:statement})\nsays that for each fixed clause width there exist a finite machine of\nthe textbook kind, a Turing machine with several tapes, and five\npositive constants. On every formula of that width the machine halts\nwith the exact number of satisfying assignments written on a tape. It\ndoes so within a bound: a constant, times a power of the input length,\ntimes two raised to a constant times the exponent. The exponent is\nwritten out in Section~\\ref{sec:statement}. Its ratio to the number of\nvariables rises above one at 50, peaks at about 2.17 near a number with\n24 digits, and falls back below one only past a number with 2,391\ndigits. The manuscript claims more than the statement\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}): two sharper exponents; a bound on the size\nof logic circuits that depends on one of them; and a chapter of proofs\nby a technique of its own, tensor contraction. We tag every claim\nverified, supported on paper by the verified statement, or paper-only.\nWe inspected the proof (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}): all 3,091 source\nfiles it depends on. They contain no axiom, no omitted proof, no step\nthat trusts a compiled program, and no patch to Mathlib, and the\nstatement they prove is the one the comparator posed. We replayed it\n(Section~\\ref{sec:replay}). The 3,091 files built from source in a\nsandbox at the pinned Lean version in 33 minutes; the kernel reported\nthe three standard axioms; and nanoda re-checked 95,176 declarations,\nthe theorem and everything it depends on, with no error. We say what\nfollows (Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}). The statement alone refutes\nETH, SETH, the gap form, the form for guessing (nondeterministic)\nmachines, the counting form, and the randomised forms of ETH, SETH and\nthe gap form. A lower bound that was proved \\emph{from} one of them is\nan ``if \\ldots{} then'' whose ``if'' is now false: it derives nothing,\nand the statement it concluded is not itself refuted. We say what it\nchanges for a practitioner (Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}): nothing on\nany machine. We show that by timing an ordinary SAT solver and two model\ncounters on random formulas of up to 500 variables and drawing the two\nguarantees beside the measurements. We restate the verified sentence in\nour own Lean library under one name, with its axioms audited\n(Section~\\ref{sec:lean}). And we place the paper beside what the\ncollection, the hypotheses' authors, a published human audit, and the\ntwo checking tools say about themselves (Section~\\ref{sec:related}).\n\n\\subsection{What is claimed, and what is not}\n\\label{sec:intro-claims}\n\nClaimed, each with the section that establishes it:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item The verified statement is a running-time \\emph{guarantee} with\n  constants that exist and are never computed, for each fixed clause\n  width, with no lower limit on the number of variables\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item With every constant set to one, the verified exponent exceeds the\n  number of variables at every size from 50 up to a number with 2,391\n  digits, so the verified bound is above $2^n$ there. The true constants\n  are unknown and at least 257 in the proof on record, so with them the\n  bound is higher still (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item The manuscript's two sharper exponents, and the circuit bound that\n  rests on one of them, are not in the Lean library\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\\item The proof's source shows none of the known ways of weakening a\n  verified proof, and its statement is the challenge's\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). It builds from source at the pinned\n  version with the three standard axioms, and a second checker agrees\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n\\item The verified statement refutes ETH, SETH, their gap, guessing and\n  counting forms, and the randomised forms of ETH, SETH and the gap\n  form, by the parts of the statement named in each case. A lower bound\n  derived from any of them loses its premise and nothing more\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}).\n\\item On random formulas, the solvers a practitioner has are many\n  orders of magnitude below $2^n$ at every size measured, and the\n  verified guarantee is above $2^n$ from 50 variables on. The verified\n  result changes nothing that runs (Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}).\n\\end{enumerate}\nNot claimed:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item No running time of the verified machine, in either direction. No\n  implementation of it exists; the title compares two guarantees.\n\\item No flaw in the statement, the proof, the Lean kernel, Mathlib, or\n  the collection's checking tool. We found none and assert none.\n\\item No value of any constant beyond what the proof writes down, and\n  nothing about what a different proof of the same statement could\n  achieve.\n\\item No judgement on the manuscript's paper-only proofs, which were\n  located and quoted, not read for correctness.\n\\item No refutation of any lower bound's conclusion, for any problem,\n  and nothing about any specific problem's complexity beyond what the\n  manuscript's own remarks state.\n\\item Nothing about any problem outside satisfiability. This paper is\n  about the limitation of the ETH refutation and nothing else.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{How the paper is organised}\n\\label{sec:intro-organisation}\n\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement} reads the statement.\nSection~\\ref{sec:beyond} draws the line between it and the manuscript.\nSections~\\ref{sec:proof} and \\ref{sec:replay} inspect and replay the\nproof. Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} says what follows.\nSection~\\ref{sec:practitioner} is for the practitioner.\nSection~\\ref{sec:lean} is our own Lean. Section~\\ref{sec:related} is\nrelated work. Section~\\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes and lists the\nquestions this reading left for its author to decide. Every section\nends with a list headed ``what this does not claim''.\nAppendix~\\ref{app:lean} lists every Lean name the paper cites.\nAppendix~\\ref{app:tech} holds the technical detail behind the plain\ntext of the body: the definitions with their line numbers, the theorem\nwith its quantifiers, the spine of the proof. The OpenAI repository is\nread throughout at one commit, \\texttt{4eaee875}, as untrusted data:\nwith text tools only, never built or run in place. The one build is the\nsandboxed replay.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:intro-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the sections it summarises.} Every\n  sentence of this introduction is a sentence of a later section said\n  shorter; where the two differ, the later section, with its file and\n  line, is the one that counts.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the result's importance.} Whether the\n  refutation matters is not a question this paper asks; what the\n  verified statement says, and at what sizes, is.\n\\item \\textbf{No prior-art claim.} ``We found no earlier statement'' of\n  the title's observation is bounded to the six texts\n  Section~\\ref{sec:related} names; the literature at large was not\n  searched.\n\\end{itemize}\nEOF\nmake check 2>&1 | tail -1; grep -c \"Overfull\" main.log; grep -n \"^EXIT\\|GATE\" /tmp/gate_it10.log | tail -3","description":"Rewrite the introduction with the reader's fixes, rebuild, and check the gate"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1761,"cache_read_input_tokens":241744,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1761,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1cad2094-11ee-40b3-a86f-8ba4c86457ea","uuid":"0ccf6fff-7d9d-429c-9a95-71c50c259088","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:22:49.958Z","request_id":"req_011CgjNuJLviarSwZyeQ2oLs","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01JFnmfhi8bcfghUUGgSmDKa":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex && cat > sec1_intro.tex <<'EOF'\n% Introduction -- drafted by loop0015 item 09 (2026-11-05), under the owner's\n% plain-English rule of 2026-11-05 05:30: no formula beyond 2^n in this\n% section, every term of art glossed in one clause on first use, every\n% number with the section that regenerates it. The title's four clauses are\n% made precise in the first paragraph; the symbols (the exponent, the\n% constants) are introduced in Section 2 and used from there on. A blind\n% reader's pass (one subagent, 2026-11-05) moved the glossary ahead of the\n% summary and shortened the long sentences.\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nIn late October 2026, OpenAI's collection of machine-checked mathematics\nadded a result dated 25 October 2026 that it calls family 378,\n\\emph{The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False}\n\\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}.\nThe Exponential Time Hypothesis, ETH, is a conjecture from 2001\n\\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}. It says that deciding whether a formula\nwith three variables per clause can be satisfied needs time that grows\nexponentially in the number of variables. The result comes with a\n67-page manuscript and a proof checked by the Lean proof assistant, a\nprogram that verifies every step of a proof and rejects a proof with a\ngap. The verified theorem does refute the hypothesis. The title of this\npaper says what else is true of it. Its sentence has four clauses.\nFirst, what is verified is a \\emph{guarantee}, not a running time. The\ntheorem says that a machine halts within a stated bound; it says\nnothing about how long the machine actually takes on any input. Second,\nthe bound contains constants that the theorem only says \\emph{exist}.\nNo value of any of them is computed anywhere. Where the proof does\nwrite a value down, the constant in the exponent is at least 257\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). Third, the \\emph{exponent}, the quantity\nthat two is raised to in the bound, is a fixed function of the number\nof variables. With every constant set to one, it is larger than the\nnumber of variables at every size from 50 variables up to a number with\n2,391 digits (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}, Figure~\\ref{fig:central}).\nOver that whole range the verified bound is above $2^n$, the cost of\ntrying every one of the $2^n$ assignments of $n$ variables. So the\nverified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size, than the\nguarantee of the method everyone already has. Fourth, the manuscript\nstates two sharper exponents, which would make a different sentence.\nNeither is in the Lean library, and the manuscript itself calls the\nverified one ``the weaker exponent'' (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\nThis paper is a careful reading by people who checked, not a debunking.\nWe read the verified statement line by line. We inspected the proof's\nsource. We rebuilt the proof from source in isolation and re-checked it\nwith a second proof checker that shares no code with Lean's own. We\nfound no flaw: the statement means what the manuscript's verified form\nsays, and the proof rests on the three standard axioms, the assumptions\nevery ordinary Lean proof makes, and nothing else. Every sentence that\ncould read as ``wrong'' or ``overclaimed'' is replaced by what the Lean\nstatement and the manuscript actually say, with the file and line, or\nthe page and line, beside it. Nothing is quoted from memory. Every\nnumber has a script that regenerates it, named where the number\nappears.\n\n\\subsection{The words we use}\n\\label{sec:intro-words}\n\nEach section glosses its own terms where they first appear, so that a\nreader may enter anywhere. The ones the whole paper needs are these,\neach in a sentence or two.\nA \\emph{formula} is a list of clauses, each clause an ``or'' of a few\nvariables or negated variables. A choice of true or false for every\nvariable \\emph{satisfies} the formula when it makes every clause true.\nA \\emph{$k$-CNF} is a formula with at most $k$ variables per clause,\nand $k$ is called the \\emph{clause width}; 3-SAT is the problem of\ndeciding whether a 3-CNF can be satisfied.\n\\emph{Trying all $2^n$ assignments} is the method of checking every one\nof the $2^n$ ways to set $n$ variables; its cost is the yardstick in the\ntitle.\n\\emph{ETH} is the conjecture that 3-SAT needs time exponential in $n$.\n\\emph{SETH}, its strong form, says that as $k$ grows no algorithm for\n$k$-SAT does much better than trying all assignments. Several variants\nexist, and Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} names each.\nA \\emph{guarantee} is a bound a theorem promises; a \\emph{running time}\nis what a clock measures; the two are compared only with their own\nkind.\n\\emph{Lean} is the proof assistant. Its \\emph{kernel} is the small core\nthat does the final checking, and ``verified'' in this paper always\nmeans ``accepted by the kernel''. \\emph{Mathlib} is Lean's community\nlibrary of mathematics.\nThe \\emph{comparator} is OpenAI's checking tool. It takes a\n\\emph{challenge file}, which states a theorem with the proof left\nblank, and a \\emph{solution}, which proves it. It checks that both\nstate the same theorem and that the proof uses only the three standard\naxioms.\n\\emph{nanoda} is a second, independent checker for Lean proofs, written\nin another language. A \\emph{replay} is a rebuild of a proof from\nsource on our own machine, inside a \\emph{sandbox}, an isolated\nenvironment that sees nothing else on the machine, at the \\emph{pinned}\nLean version, the exact version the repository names.\nA \\emph{SAT solver} is the program a practitioner runs to decide a\nformula, and a \\emph{model counter} the one that counts its satisfying\nassignments.\n\n\\subsection{What this paper does}\n\\label{sec:intro-summary}\n\nIn one paragraph. The verified statement (Section~\\ref{sec:statement})\nsays that for each fixed clause width there exist a finite machine of\nthe textbook kind, a Turing machine with several tapes, and five\npositive constants. On every formula of that width the machine halts\nwith the exact number of satisfying assignments written on a tape. It\ndoes so within a bound: a constant, times a power of the input length,\ntimes two raised to a constant times the exponent. The exponent is\nwritten out in Section~\\ref{sec:statement}. Its ratio to the number of\nvariables rises above one at 50, peaks at about 2.17 near a number with\n24 digits, and falls back below one only past a number with 2,391\ndigits. The manuscript claims more than the statement\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}): two sharper exponents; a bound on the size\nof logic circuits that depends on one of them; and a chapter of proofs\nby a technique of its own, tensor contraction. We tag every claim\nverified, supported on paper by the verified statement, or paper-only.\nWe inspected the proof (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}): all 3,091 source\nfiles it depends on. They contain no axiom, no omitted proof, no step\nthat trusts a compiled program, and no patch to Mathlib, and the\nstatement they prove is the one the comparator posed. We replayed it\n(Section~\\ref{sec:replay}). The 3,091 files built from source in a\nsandbox at the pinned Lean version in 33 minutes; the kernel reported\nthe three standard axioms; and nanoda re-checked 95,176 declarations,\nthe theorem and everything it depends on, with no error. We say what\nfollows (Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}). The statement alone refutes\nETH, SETH, the gap form, the form for guessing (nondeterministic)\nmachines, the counting form, and the randomised forms of ETH, SETH and\nthe gap form. A lower bound that was proved \\emph{from} one of them is\nan ``if \\ldots{} then'' whose ``if'' is now false: it derives nothing,\nand the statement it concluded is not itself refuted. We say what it\nchanges for a practitioner (Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}): nothing on\nany machine. We show that by timing an ordinary SAT solver and two model\ncounters on random formulas of up to 500 variables and drawing the two\nguarantees beside the measurements. We restate the verified sentence in\nour own Lean library under one name, with its axioms audited\n(Section~\\ref{sec:lean}). And we place the paper beside what the\ncollection, the hypotheses' authors, a published human audit, and the\ntwo checking tools say about themselves (Section~\\ref{sec:related}).\n\n\\subsection{What is claimed, and what is not}\n\\label{sec:intro-claims}\n\nClaimed, each with the section that establishes it:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item The verified statement is a running-time \\emph{guarantee} with\n  constants that exist and are never computed, for each fixed clause\n  width, with no lower limit on the number of variables\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item With every constant set to one, the verified exponent exceeds the\n  number of variables at every size from 50 up to a number with 2,391\n  digits, so the verified bound is above $2^n$ there. The true constants\n  are unknown and at least 257 in the proof on record, so with them the\n  bound is higher still (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item The manuscript's two sharper exponents, and the circuit bound that\n  rests on one of them, are not in the Lean library\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\\item The proof's source shows none of the known ways of weakening a\n  verified proof, and its statement is the challenge's\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). It builds from source at the pinned\n  version with the three standard axioms, and a second checker agrees\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n\\item The verified statement refutes ETH, SETH, their gap, guessing and\n  counting forms, and the randomised forms of ETH, SETH and the gap\n  form, by the parts of the statement named in each case. A lower bound\n  derived from any of them loses its premise and nothing more\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}).\n\\item On random formulas, the solvers a practitioner has are many\n  orders of magnitude below $2^n$ at every size measured, and the\n  verified guarantee is above $2^n$ from 50 variables on. The verified\n  result changes nothing that runs (Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}).\n\\end{enumerate}\nNot claimed:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item No running time of the verified machine, in either direction. No\n  implementation of it exists; the title compares two guarantees.\n\\item No flaw in the statement, the proof, the Lean kernel, Mathlib, or\n  the collection's checking tool. We found none and assert none.\n\\item No value of any constant beyond what the proof writes down, and\n  nothing about what a different proof of the same statement could\n  achieve.\n\\item No judgement on the manuscript's paper-only proofs, which were\n  located and quoted, not read for correctness.\n\\item No refutation of any lower bound's conclusion, for any problem,\n  and nothing about any specific problem's complexity beyond what the\n  manuscript's own remarks state.\n\\item Nothing about any problem outside satisfiability. This paper is\n  about the limitation of the ETH refutation and nothing else.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{How the paper is organised}\n\\label{sec:intro-organisation}\n\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement} reads the statement.\nSection~\\ref{sec:beyond} draws the line between it and the manuscript.\nSections~\\ref{sec:proof} and \\ref{sec:replay} inspect and replay the\nproof. Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} says what follows.\nSection~\\ref{sec:practitioner} is for the practitioner.\nSection~\\ref{sec:lean} is our own Lean. Section~\\ref{sec:related} is\nrelated work. Section~\\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes and lists the\nquestions this reading left for its author to decide. Every section\nends with a list headed ``what this does not claim''.\nAppendix~\\ref{app:lean} lists every Lean name the paper cites.\nAppendix~\\ref{app:tech} holds the technical detail behind the plain\ntext of the body: the definitions with their line numbers, the theorem\nwith its quantifiers, the spine of the proof. The OpenAI repository is\nread throughout at one commit, \\texttt{4eaee875}, as untrusted data:\nwith text tools only, never built or run in place. The one build is the\nsandboxed replay.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:intro-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the sections it summarises.} Every\n  sentence of this introduction is a sentence of a later section said\n  shorter; where the two differ, the later section, with its file and\n  line, is the one that counts.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the result's importance.} Whether the\n  refutation matters is not a question this paper asks; what the\n  verified statement says, and at what sizes, is.\n\\item \\textbf{No prior-art claim.} ``We found no earlier statement'' of\n  the title's observation is bounded to the six texts\n  Section~\\ref{sec:related} names; the literature at large was not\n  searched.\n\\end{itemize}\nEOF\nmake check 2>&1 | tail -1; grep -c \"Overfull\" main.log; grep -n \"^EXIT\\|GATE\" /tmp/gate_it10.log | tail -3","description":"Rewrite the introduction with the reader's fixes, rebuild, and check the gate"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01JFnmfhi8bcfghUUGgSmDKa":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex"}}}
